STOP_SCRIPTS.push(
  { id: "athens-acromuseum", v: "1-3160aa", city: /Athens|Piraeus/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(acropolis museum)\b/i, title: "Acropolis Museum", where: "Acropolis Museum", checked: "2026-11-21",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Acropolis Museum","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acropolis_Museum","when":"2026-11-21"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Elgin Marbles","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elgin_Marbles","when":"2026-11-21"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Erechtheion","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Erechtheion","when":"2026-11-21"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Arrival","minutes":1,"stand":"Outside the entrance, on the glass floor of the forecourt, looking down.","paras":["This museum was built for one job: to hold everything found on the Acropolis rock and its slopes. It opened in June 2009, and today it shows more than 4,250 objects.","Look down through the glass. The whole building stands on pillars above an excavated neighbourhood, with houses and workshops from classical and early Byzantine times. Parts of the floor inside are glass too."]},{"name":"The climb","minutes":1,"stand":"Inside, at the foot of the long sloping gallery.","paras":["This first gallery slopes upward on purpose, like the climb up to the rock. Then comes a big hall of archaic finds, and pieces from the Erechtheion, the temple of Athena Nike and the gateway you may have walked through.","Look out for the Maidens, the Caryatids from the Erechtheion. One of them ended up in the British Museum in London."]},{"name":"The Parthenon hall","minutes":1,"stand":"On the top floor, at the north windows, with the Parthenon in view.","paras":["This top floor is the heart of the museum. Notice it sits at an angle to the rest of the building. That's so it lines up with the Parthenon itself. Look out the north windows and there it is.","The 48 columns in this hall mark out the outline of the temple, and the sculptures face outward, the way they did on the building.","Now look closely at the sculptures. Where Parthenon pieces are missing, the museum shows casts in their place. Many of the missing pieces are in London. Between 1801 and 1812, agents of Lord Elgin took about half the surviving Parthenon sculptures.","Whether that was legal is still disputed. A British inquiry in 1816 said it was. In 1983 Greece formally asked Britain to return them, and Greek officials hope this room will help bring them home.","Not everyone loves the display. Some archaeologists and museum professionals criticise the lighting and the politics of the design. Decide for yourself."]}] },
  { id: "athens-acropolis", v: "1-16d7e4", city: /Athens|Piraeus/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b)(?!.*acropolis museum)(?!.*acropolis promenade).*\b(acropolis|parthenon)\b/i, title: "The Acropolis and the Parthenon", where: "The Acropolis and the Parthenon", checked: "2026-11-21",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Acropolis of Athens","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acropolis_of_Athens","when":"2026-11-21"},{"pub":"Acropolis Restoration Service","title":"Parthenon - YSMA","url":"https://www.ysma.gr/en/monuments/","when":"2026-11-21"},{"pub":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","title":"Acropolis, Athens - UNESCO World Heritage Centre","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/404/","when":"2026-11-21"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Propylaea (Acropolis of Athens)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Propylaea_(Acropolis_of_Athens)","when":"2026-11-21"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Temple of Athena Nike","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Athena_Nike","when":"2026-11-21"},{"pub":"Hellenic Ministry of Culture","title":"Ministry of Culture and Sports | Parthenon","url":"https://odysseus.culture.gr/h/2/eh251.jsp?obj_id=912","when":"2026-11-21"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Parthenon","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parthenon","when":"2026-11-21"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Elgin Marbles","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elgin_Marbles","when":"2026-11-21"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Erechtheion","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Erechtheion","when":"2026-11-21"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Areopagus","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Areopagus","when":"2026-11-21"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Arrival","minutes":1,"stand":"Stand just past the ticket gate at the west entrance, to the side of the path, where you can look up at the gateway of columns.","paras":["Here we are, at the foot of the Acropolis. Take a breath before the climb.","Almost everything you'll see up top was built in one burst of confidence. In 480 BC the Persians sacked Athens and destroyed the temples on this hill. After the Greeks won that war, the city rebuilt it all under the statesman Pericles.","Two practical things before we climb. Take the marble steps slowly and watch your footing. And carry water with you."]},{"name":"The gateway","minutes":1,"stand":"Stop inside the gateway, between the big columns.","paras":["This gateway is the Propylaia, designed by the architect Mnesikles and built from 437 to 432 BC.","Here's something most people walk straight past. The Propylaia was never finished. Work stopped in 432 BC, with the builders' lifting bosses still left on the blocks.","Look up to your right at the little temple on its own bastion. That's the temple of Athena Nike, Athena of Victory, the smallest temple on the hill. Its date isn't settled: UNESCO gives 427 to 424 BC, other sources say it was finished around 420 BC."]},{"name":"The Parthenon","minutes":1,"stand":"Walk through the gateway onto the open rock and stop where you see the west end of the Parthenon at an angle.","paras":["And there it is. The Parthenon, the temple of Athena, protector of the city. It was begun in 447 BC and completed in 438 BC, with the carving going on until 432. The architects were Iktinos and Kallikrates, and the sculptor Pheidias was in charge of the sculpture.","Now here's the secret of this building. It looks perfectly straight, but it isn't. The floor bulges upward in the middle, about 10 centimetres over 70 metres. The columns swell slightly too. Scholars still argue about exactly why the builders did it.","Picture it whole. Inside stood the statue of Athena by Pheidias, in gold and ivory. According to the historian Thucydides, Pericles told the Athenians it held forty talents of pure gold, a reserve the city could use in an emergency."]},{"name":"The Parthenon's other lives","minutes":2,"stand":"Stay beside the Parthenon, anywhere along its long side.","paras":["Here's the part of the story most visitors never hear. For a very long time this was a church. It became a church, first of Saint Sophia, Holy Wisdom, and later of the Virgin Mary. The Greek Ministry of Culture says that happened in the fifth century AD; other sources say the late 500s, so the sources differ. Medieval Greeks called it the church of the Theotokos Atheniotissa, Our Lady of Athens.","Then came Latin crusader lords, and for about 250 years it was a Catholic church of Our Lady. The Ottomans took the hill in 1458, or 1460 by another account, and before the end of that century the Parthenon was a mosque. Exactly how that happened is unclear.","Then came the worst day in its history. On 26 September 1687 a Venetian army was bombarding the hill. The Ottomans were storing gunpowder inside the Parthenon. A mortar round hit, and the middle of the building blew apart. Accounts from the time disagree on whether the gunners meant to hit it.","More was lost later. From 1801 to 1812, agents of the British ambassador, Lord Elgin, removed about half the surviving sculptures and sent them to Britain. Whether that was legal is still disputed, and Greece has asked for them back.","The cranes you see are the restoration teams. Their current work began in 1975, and it's still going."]},{"name":"The Erechtheion and the Maidens","minutes":1,"stand":"Cross to the north side and face the porch held up by six statues of young women.","paras":["This is the Erechtheion, and these are the Maidens, the Caryatids, carrying the roof on their heads. One of the original Maidens ended up in the British Museum in London.","This is also where Athens kept its founding legend. As the story goes, Athena and Poseidon competed for the city, a contest carved on the west end of the Parthenon. It's legend, not history, but Athenians pointed to a sacred olive of Athena here, and to marks in the rock said to be from Poseidon's trident."]},{"name":"Closing","minutes":1,"stand":"On your way back down, at the top of the gateway steps.","paras":["Take one last look back. A temple, a treasury, a church of the Virgin Mary, a mosque, a gunpowder store, then a ruin, and now a building that people are patiently putting back together. Every age has found something here worth saving.","Below the entrance is a bare rocky hill, the Areopagus. The book of Acts links it to the apostle Paul's speech to the Athenians, although historians aren't sure whether he spoke on the hill itself or before the council named after it."]}] },
  { id: "athens-agora", v: "1-2308ba", city: /Athens|Piraeus/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(ancient agora|agora of athens|classical agora)\b/i, title: "Ancient Agora", where: "Ancient Agora", checked: "2026-11-21",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Ancient Agora of Athens","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ancient_Agora_of_Athens","when":"2026-11-21"},{"pub":"American School of Classical Studies at Athens","title":"Athenian Agora | American School of Classical Studies at Athens","url":"https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/excavations/athenian-agora","when":"2026-11-21"},{"pub":"King James Version (public domain), via bible-api.com","title":"","url":"https://bible-api.com/acts%2017:16-34?translation=kjv","when":"2026-11-21"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Temple of Hephaestus","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Hephaestus","when":"2026-11-21"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Stoa of Attalos","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoa_of_Attalos","when":"2026-11-21"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Arrival","minutes":1,"stand":"Just inside the entrance, where you can see the long two-storey colonnade to your left and the temple on the hill to your right.","paras":["Welcome to the Agora, the heart of ancient Athens. It was the business, political and legal centre of the city, where citizens and foreigners, litigants and jurors, merchants and philosophers all met, in one open space below the Acropolis.","Picture it crowded. In the 400s and 300s BC there were women running inns and selling goods here, alongside everyone else.","On the west side stood the Monument of the Eponymous Heroes. It worked as the city's notice board, where new laws, public events and military call-ups were posted.","This is also the market where, according to Acts chapter seventeen, the apostle Paul argued daily with anyone who would listen."]},{"name":"The Temple of Hephaestus","minutes":1,"stand":"Walk up Market Hill and stop at the corner of the temple.","paras":["This is one of the best-preserved Greek temples anywhere, still standing largely intact. It honours Hephaestus, god of fire and metalwork, and fittingly, potters and metalworkers had their shops all around it. Work began in 449 BC.","For a long time people called it the Theseion, thinking the hero Theseus was buried here. That was a mistake. Inscriptions found inside tie it firmly to Hephaestus.","Why did it survive when so much else fell? It kept being used. It became a church of Saint George, probably around AD 700, although nobody knows exactly when, and it stayed a church until 1834."]},{"name":"The Stoa of Attalos","minutes":1,"stand":"At the foot of the long two-storey building on the east side.","paras":["This long building is a gift. King Attalos the Second of Pergamon had studied in Athens and paid for it as a thank-you. Behind the colonnade were rows of rooms used as shops.","Invaders called the Heruli burned its woodwork in AD 267. What you see now was rebuilt from 1952 to 1956 by the American School of Classical Studies, which has been digging here since 1931.","Inside is the Agora museum, and most of it is about the birth of democracy. A good place to end: in the square where Athenians argued, voted and traded, a few steps from where the first citizens tried to govern themselves."]}] },
  { id: "athens-areopagus", v: "1-ea0d7d", city: /Athens|Piraeus/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(areopagus|mars hill|areios pagos)\b/i, title: "Areopagus (Mars Hill)", where: "Areopagus (Mars Hill)", checked: "2026-11-21",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Areopagus","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Areopagus","when":"2026-11-21"},{"pub":"King James Version (public domain), via bible-api.com","title":"","url":"https://bible-api.com/acts%2017:16-34?translation=kjv","when":"2026-11-21"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Areopagus sermon","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Areopagus_sermon","when":"2026-11-21"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Arrival","minutes":1,"stand":"On a flat spot near the top of the rock, well back from the edges, facing the Acropolis gateway.","paras":["This bare, rocky hill is the Areopagus. The name means the Hill of Ares, the god of war. In the King James Bible it's Mars' hill.","Watch your footing up here. The rock is uneven, and there are no railings at the edges.","Long before Paul, this rock was famous for justice. The council that met here became the court of Athens for murder and certain religious crimes. Exactly how early that began, historians aren't sure, but it may go back to the 7th century BC.","The Athenians liked a good origin story. Myth said the war god Ares himself was put on trial here by the other gods. And in the play The Eumenides, from 458 BC, the playwright Aeschylus set the trial of Orestes on this hill."]},{"name":"Paul in Athens","minutes":1,"stand":"Stay facing the Acropolis.","paras":["Now to the apostle Paul. The book of Acts, chapter seventeen, tells how the apostle Paul waited in Athens, and his spirit was stirred in him, when he saw the city wholly given to idolatry. He argued daily in the synagogue, and in the market below us.","Some of the philosophers brought him unto Areopagus, Acts says, to explain his new teaching. Here historians aren't sure what that means: this hill itself, or the council named after it, which by Paul's time probably met down in the marketplace instead. One scholar has even suggested the hearing may have been part lecture, part trial.","Here are his opening words, in the King James Version: \"Ye men of Athens, I perceive that in all things ye are too superstitious. For as I passed by, and beheld your devotions, I found an altar with this inscription, to the unknown god. Whom therefore ye ignorantly worship, him declare I unto you.\"","Picture saying this in a city Acts describes as wholly given to idolatry: that God that made the world dwelleth not in temples made with hands.","He even quoted their own Greek poets back to them. It was a sermon built for this audience."]},{"name":"How it ended","minutes":1,"stand":"Turn to look down over the Agora.","paras":["Acts is honest about the result. When they heard of the resurrection of the dead, some mocked, and others said they would hear him again. But a few believed, among them Dionysius the Areopagite, and a woman named Damaris.","Paul did not win the crowd that day. But a few people listened, and the words Acts records have been read all over the world ever since."]}] },
  { id: "athens-byzantine-museum", v: "1-d40ab5", city: /Athens|Piraeus/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(byzantine and christian museum|byzantine museum|villa ilissia|ilissia)\b/i, title: "Byzantine and Christian Museum", where: "Byzantine and Christian Museum", checked: "2026-11-23",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Byzantine and Christian Museum","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Byzantine_and_Christian_Museum","when":"2026-11-23"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Athens","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Athens","when":"2026-11-21"}],
    parts: [{"name":"The villa","minutes":1,"stand":"Outside, facing the Villa Ilissia, the old villa at the heart of the museum.","paras":["This is the Byzantine and Christian Museum. Inside are more than 25,000 objects: pictures, frescoes, manuscripts, fabrics and pottery, from the 3rd century AD to the late Middle Ages. That's well over a thousand years in one museum.","The museum was founded in 1914. At first it was on the first floor of the Academy. Then it moved here, to the Villa Ilissia, the villa of the Duchesse de Plaisance, a building from 1848, designed by Stamatios Kleanthis. In June 2004, for its 90th birthday and the Athens Olympics, it reopened with a new wing."]},{"name":"Before you go in","minutes":1,"stand":"Still outside, before you head in.","paras":["Inside, follow the signs, and ask the staff if you're looking for something in particular.","One more thing worth knowing. Close by is the site of the Lyceum, where Aristotle had his school. It was only rediscovered in 1996. Ask at the museum how to get in.","As you walk through, don't try to see everything. Find one face that holds your eye, and stay with it a while."]}] },
  { id: "athens-corinth-canal", v: "1-f55be2", city: /Athens|Piraeus/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(corinth canal|diolkos|dioryga)\b/i, title: "Corinth Canal", where: "Corinth Canal", checked: "2026-11-23",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Corinth Canal","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corinth_Canal","when":"2026-11-23"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Corinth","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Corinth","when":"2026-11-23"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Diolkos","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diolkos","when":"2026-11-23"}],
    parts: [{"name":"On the bridge","minutes":1,"stand":"Where you can look safely down into the canal from behind a railing.","paras":["First, hold on to the railing, keep a firm grip on your phone and anything you're carrying, and mind the traffic behind you. It's a long way down.","Below you is the Corinth Canal, finished in 1893. It slices through the Isthmus, joining the Gulf of Corinth on one side to the Saronic Gulf on the other. It cut the Peloponnese off from the mainland, so you could argue that southern Greece is now an island.","Look at those walls, cut at a near-vertical angle of 80 degrees. The bridges here cross at a height of about 148 feet. And yet the canal is only about 81 feet wide at the surface, and 4 miles long. It was dug at sea level, so there are no locks at all."]},{"name":"A very old dream","minutes":1,"stand":"Stay at the railing and look along the canal toward the far end.","paras":["People dreamed of this cut for some two and a half thousand years. The first was Periander, ruler of Corinth in the 7th century BC. He gave up. Instead, he's credited with a stone ship road across the land, the Diolkos, which dates from about his time.","Why did he give up? Nobody knows for sure. It may have been the cost, or a lack of workers, or a fear that a canal would take trade away from Corinth itself.","The idea seemed cursed. Plutarch and Suetonius both wrote that Julius Caesar considered a canal, but was assassinated before he could begin. Later, the story went that the philosopher Apollonius of Tyana warned that anyone who proposed one would fall ill.","The emperor Nero actually started. In 67 AD he broke the ground himself with a pickaxe. The digging was done by 6,000 Judean prisoners of war. When Nero died soon after, the work stopped. But today's canal follows the same line as his.","His workers left a relief of Hercules carved into the rock. It's on the north side of the cutting, at the Gulf of Corinth end, below an old bridge foundation. You can't see it from up here."]},{"name":"Building it, and keeping it open","minutes":1,"stand":"Look down at the walls, at the lines and scars in the rock.","paras":["Work started again in 1881, and it was a struggle. Rock and money troubles bankrupted the original builders. The canal was finally completed on July 25, 1893.","Those walls have never been stable. In 1923 alone, 41,000 cubic meters of material fell into the water. More recently, a landslide closed the canal at the start of 2021. It reopened for a few months in 2022, and then, after more safety work, on June 1, 2023.","War hit it too. In October 1944, retreating German forces put the canal out of action, and it didn't reopen to all traffic until 1948.","Today it's mostly a sight in itself. It saves ships a 430 mile trip around the Peloponnese, but it's too narrow for many modern ships. In a normal year, around 15,000 ships still squeeze through, mostly tourist boats. At each end there's a bridge that sinks under the water to let them pass."]},{"name":"The Diolkos","minutes":1,"stand":"If you go on to the western end of the canal, by the sinking bridge, look for the old stone roadway beside the water.","paras":["Before the canal, there was the Diolkos: a paved road for dragging boats across the Isthmus. It ran from about 600 BC to the middle of the first century AD. Pieces of it survive next to the canal, mostly at the western end.","Look for the grooves in the hard limestone, about 63 inches apart. Boats, or perhaps only their cargo, were presumably hauled on some sort of wheeled vehicle, pulled by men and animals with ropes. One estimate says a warship needed a team of some 112 to 142 people. It worked well enough that the playwright Aristophanes used the phrase \"as fast as a Corinthian.\"","Please keep to the walkway. Ships passing in the canal have eroded much of the old road, and it's fragile.","From the walkway, look from one to the other: the ancient stone road and the modern cut beside it. Two and a half thousand years of people looking at the same narrow strip of land, and refusing to sail the long way round."]}] },
  { id: "athens-corinth", v: "1-772fb9", city: /Athens|Piraeus/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(ancient corinth|archaia korinthos|acrocorinth|akrokorinthos)\b/i, title: "Ancient Corinth", where: "Ancient Corinth", checked: "2026-11-23",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Ancient Corinth","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ancient_Corinth","when":"2026-11-23"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Corinth","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Corinth","when":"2026-11-23"},{"pub":"King James Version (public domain), via bible-api.com","title":"","url":"https://bible-api.com/acts%2018%3A1-18?translation=kjv","when":"2026-11-23"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Delphi","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Delphi","when":"2026-11-23"},{"pub":"King James Version (public domain), via bible-api.com","title":"","url":"https://bible-api.com/1%20corinthians%2013%3A1-13?translation=kjv","when":"2026-11-23"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Acrocorinth","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acrocorinth","when":"2026-11-23"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Arrival","minutes":1,"stand":"Inside the site, at a spot where you can see the old columns of the Temple of Apollo on their rocky rise.","paras":["Welcome to Ancient Corinth. It grew rich on the Isthmus, the narrow neck of land joining southern Greece to the mainland, where all land traffic had to pass.","There's little shade here. Keep your hat on, drink often, and watch your step on the old paving.","In its prime it was one of the largest cities in Greece, with an estimated population of about 90,000 in 400 BC. Then, in 146 BC, the Romans destroyed it. It lay largely deserted until Julius Caesar refounded it as a Roman colony in 44 BC, and Rome later made it the capital of the province of Achaea.","So the city Paul walked into was a Roman one, and a crowded one. Romans, Greeks and Jews lived side by side.","American archaeologists have been digging here since 1896. They can't dig everywhere, though: the modern village of Ancient Corinth sits right on top of the old city."]},{"name":"The Temple of Apollo","minutes":1,"stand":"Walk toward the temple and stop where you can see all of its standing columns.","paras":["Up on the rocky hill stand seven columns of the Temple of Apollo, built in the middle of the 500s BC. It's one of the earliest Doric temples in Greece.","It's also one of the few things here that never disappeared from view. And it had several lives. The Romans restored it for the worship of the emperor. In Byzantine times it became a church. Under the Ottomans part of it was pulled down, and the local Bey built a house on top of its base.","Somewhere near the temple is the Glauke Fountain, said to have been built in Roman times. As the story goes, Glauke was about to marry Jason, the hero of the Argonauts, when Medea sent her a poisoned robe. It burst into flames, and she threw herself into the fountain that now carries her name."]},{"name":"Paul comes to Corinth","minutes":2,"stand":"Walk on into the remains of the Roman city and find a spot to stop.","paras":["Now to the reason many people come here. The book of Acts, chapter eighteen, begins: \"After these things Paul departed from Athens, and came to Corinth.\"","He moved in with a couple, Aquila and Priscilla, and Acts says why: \"for by their occupation they were tentmakers.\" So was he.","Aquila was a Jew, lately come from Italy with his wife Priscilla, because the emperor Claudius had ordered all Jews to leave Rome. In the words of Acts: \"because he was of the same craft, he abode with them, and wrought.\"","Think about that for a moment. The apostle earned his keep with his hands, working alongside new friends. That's where the modern Christian idea of \"tentmaking\" gets its name.","Every sabbath he reasoned in the synagogue. When Silas and Timotheus came down from Macedonia and he was opposed, he said: \"from henceforth I will go unto the Gentiles.\" He went next door, into the house of a man named Justus, \"whose house joined hard to the synagogue.\" And Crispus, the chief ruler of the synagogue, believed, with all his house.","Then, Acts says, the Lord spoke to Paul in the night by a vision: \"Be not afraid, but speak, and hold not thy peace: For I am with thee, and no man shall set on thee to hurt thee: for I have much people in this city.\"","So he stayed, Acts says, \"a year and six months, teaching the word of God among them.\" Historians place that first visit around AD 49 or 50. Christian tradition counts Corinth as a church founded by an apostle, by Paul himself."]},{"name":"Before Gallio","minutes":2,"stand":"Stay where you are.","paras":["Acts tells of one dramatic day here. \"When Gallio was the deputy of Achaia,\" Paul's opponents \"brought him to the judgment seat.\"","Gallio was the Roman governor, the brother of the philosopher Seneca. The charge was that Paul persuaded men to worship God contrary to the law. When Paul was now about to open his mouth, Gallio would not hear it. His answer ended: \"But if it be a question of words and names, and of your law, look ye to it; for I will be no judge of such matters.\" And he drove them from the judgment seat.","It got ugly. The Greeks took Sosthenes, the chief ruler of the synagogue, and beat him right in front of the judgment seat. And Acts adds, dryly: \"Gallio cared for none of those things.\"","Where was that judgment seat? Among the important monuments of this site is one called the Bema, which later became a church of the Apostle Paul. We don't know whether Paul stood on this very spot, so that question is yours to ponder.","Gallio isn't only a name in the Bible. Historians date the trial to AD 51 or 52, and an inscription that mentions the Roman proconsul Gallio is on display in the museum at Delphi."]},{"name":"Letters to Corinth","minutes":1,"stand":"Find a bit of shade nearby and take a breather.","paras":["Paul stayed a good while longer. Then he sailed for Syria, and Priscilla and Aquila went with him. Acts adds a small detail: \"having shorn his head in Cenchrea: for he had a vow.\" Cenchreae was Corinth's harbor on the Saronic Gulf, to the east.","He didn't forget this city. He wrote at least two letters to the church here, First and Second Corinthians, and he came back. He stayed again for about three months, and it is believed he wrote his letter to the Romans here.","From his first letter to the Christians of this city, in the King James Version: \"And now abideth faith, hope, charity, these three; but the greatest of these is charity.\"","A few lines earlier in the same chapter: \"Charity suffereth long, and is kind; charity envieth not; charity vaunteth not itself, is not puffed up.\""]},{"name":"Acrocorinth, the hill above","minutes":2,"stand":"Look up at the great rock that towers over the site.","paras":["That huge rock above us is Acrocorinth, Upper Corinth, the city's acropolis. The scholar George Forrest called it the most impressive acropolis of mainland Greece.","If you go up, the climb is steep and shadeless. Go early or late on a hot day, wear good shoes, and carry water.","It had its own water supply and it commanded the Isthmus, so it became a last line of defense for southern Greece. People lived up there from archaic times into the early 1800s. Three rings of walls defended it. The Byzantines fortified it heavily, and later Franks, Venetians and Ottomans all held it. From 1205 the Corinthians held out up there against the crusaders, at first under Leo Sgouros. The rock did not fall until 1210.","On the highest peak stood a temple to Aphrodite. It became a church, and later a mosque.","The Corinthians had myths for this rock too. In one, a being called Briareus settled a quarrel between the gods: the Isthmus went to Poseidon, god of the sea, and this rock to Helios, the sun. And legend says the winged horse Pegasus drank at the Upper Peirene spring, inside the walls, where the hero Bellerophon caught and tamed him.","One last thought. Acts tells of three tentmakers and a house next door to the synagogue. Not much, next to a fortress like that. Yet the words written to the people of this city are still read today, far beyond it."]}] },
  { id: "athens-delphi", v: "1-9da985", city: /Athens|Piraeus/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(delphi|delfi|delphoi|charioteer)\b/i, title: "Delphi", where: "Delphi", checked: "2026-11-23",
    sources: [{"pub":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","title":"Archaeological Site of Delphi - UNESCO World Heritage Centre","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/393/","when":"2026-11-23"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Delphi","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Delphi","when":"2026-11-23"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Pythia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pythia","when":"2026-11-23"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Delphi Archaeological Museum","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Delphi_Archaeological_Museum","when":"2026-11-23"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Charioteer of Delphi","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charioteer_of_Delphi","when":"2026-11-23"},{"pub":"King James Version (public domain), via bible-api.com","title":"","url":"https://bible-api.com/acts%2018%3A1-18?translation=kjv","when":"2026-11-23"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Ancient Corinth","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ancient_Corinth","when":"2026-11-23"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Arrival","minutes":1,"stand":"Near the entrance to the site, by the old paved square, looking up the slope toward the cliffs.","paras":["Welcome to Delphi. Look up: the sanctuary climbs Mount Parnassus, between two towering cliffs called the Shining Rocks. This was the home of the oracle, the Pythia, consulted on the biggest decisions in the Greek world.","A word before we climb. The path goes steadily uphill on uneven stone, and the sun can be fierce. Wear a hat, carry water, and take your time.","For the Greeks, this was the center of the world, the omphalos, the navel. According to myth, Zeus released two eagles, one to the east and one to the west, and they met right here. By the 6th century BC, Delphi was the religious heart of the whole Greek world.","This paved square is the first thing you see as you come in: the Roman Agora. It was once lined with porches, and processions gathered here during festivals. There was probably a market too, where pilgrims bought little statuettes and tripods to leave for the gods."]},{"name":"Up the Sacred Way","minutes":1,"stand":"Walk up the Sacred Way and stop at the restored Athenian Treasury.","paras":["You're walking the Sacred Way, the same road pilgrims climbed. All along it stood statues and little temple-like buildings called treasuries. Cities built them to celebrate victories and to thank the oracle for her advice.","This one is the Athenian Treasury, restored, and built to celebrate the victory at Marathon in 490 BC. What went inside? Very often a tithe: a tenth of the spoils of a battle.","The city of Siphnos also had a treasury here. The Siphnians gave a tenth of what their silver mines produced, until the day the sea flooded the mines.","Just beyond, between this treasury and the colonnade ahead, look for a rough outcrop of rock like a pulpit. It's called the Sibyl rock. The story goes that a prophetess, older even than the Pythia, sat here to give her prophecies."]},{"name":"The wall of freedom","minutes":1,"stand":"Continue a little way up to the row of columns set against a wall of big, many-sided stones.","paras":["This wall of many-sided stones is the polygonal wall, and it's covered in writing. From 200 BC on, contracts freeing slaves were carved into it, about a thousand of them.","Along its base is the Stoa of the Athenians. Its inscription says the Athenians built it after a naval victory over the Persians in 478 BC, to show off their war trophies. Notice the columns: unusually, each one is cut from a single piece of stone."]},{"name":"The Temple of Apollo and the Pythia","minutes":3,"stand":"Climb up to the temple terrace and stand in front of the temple, near the big altar.","paras":["This is the heart of Delphi, the Temple of Apollo. Inside, in a sunken inner room, the Pythia sat on a tripod and gave her answers. Cities asked her before going to war or founding colonies.","The ruins you see date from the 300s BC; earlier temples on this spot burned down. The big altar in front was paid for by the people of Chios, in the 5th century BC. And the Pythia herself was a local woman of blameless life, yet among the most powerful women of the ancient world.","How did she prophesy? Here's the legend. Apollo killed the great serpent Python on this spot. Its body fell into a crack in the earth, and fumes rose from it, and the priestess, breathing them, fell into a trance in which Apollo spoke through her.","What really happened? Honestly, nobody knows. The details are scarce, and the ancient writers who describe it in any detail came late, and contradict each other. Some scholars argue she spoke plainly in her own voice. A geologist reported evidence of a fault line under the temple, and some researchers suggest a gas, ethylene, seeping up from below. That theory is still debated.","Carved in the temple's forecourt, ancient writers say, were sayings such as \"Know thyself\" and \"Nothing to excess.\" Who first said them? Scholars, ancient and modern, have had their doubts. They may simply have been popular proverbs.","There was an older tradition, too, of mythical temples here before any of these: one of branches brought from Tempe, one made of beeswax and wings by bees, and one built by the gods Hephaestus and Athena.","And the end? As the story is told, in the time of the emperor Julian, who tried to bring the old gods back, a doctor named Oreibasius came to ask the oracle about the future of the old religion. The answer: \"Tell the king that the flute has fallen to the ground. Phoebus does not have a home any more, neither an oracular laurel, nor a speaking fountain, because the talking water has dried out.\" Later, the emperor Theodosius shut the oracle down."]},{"name":"Theatre and stadium","minutes":1,"stand":"Follow the path up past the theatre, stop where it rises above the seats, and turn around.","paras":["From up here, spectators could see the whole sanctuary and the valley below. This theatre dates from the 300s BC and held about 4,500 people.","The path keeps climbing, and it's steep and stony. If it's hot, rest in any shade you find, and don't push on to the stadium unless you feel up to it.","Delphi's games were different. Every four years, from 586 BC, athletes came for the Pythian Games, but there were singing and music contests too, and in later centuries they were held right here in this theatre. Winners got a crown of laurel, cut by a boy who acted out Apollo's slaying of the Python.","Higher still, beyond the theatre, is the stadium. Its track is 177 meters, or 581 feet, long and it could seat 6500 spectators."]},{"name":"The museum","minutes":1,"stand":"Inside the museum, at the start of the first rooms.","paras":["Now the museum. It holds what the excavations found, in fourteen rooms on two levels, arranged in time order, so the Charioteer comes near the end.","Room 3 has two early statues of young men. They're believed to be Kleobis and Biton, and the story goes that the brothers pulled their mother's cart all the way to Hera's sanctuary, then fell asleep in the temple and never woke. Room 5 has the Sphinx of Naxos, which once crowned a column; together they stood 12 meters high."]},{"name":"The Charioteer","minutes":2,"stand":"In the room of the Charioteer, near the end of the museum, standing in front of him.","paras":["And here he is: the Charioteer of Delphi. He's bronze, about 470 BC, and life-size at 1.8 meters. He once stood in a chariot with horses, celebrating a win at the Pythian Games.","Look at his hands. The fingers are still curved around the reins. He's shown after the race, in the victory parade, wearing the winner's headband. And look at his eyes: he's one of the few Greek bronzes that still has his inlaid glass eyes.","He survived because an earthquake buried him in 373 BC, and he was found in 1896. The damaged inscription on the base is read as: \"Polyzalus dedicated me.\" And then: \"Make him prosper, honoured Apollo.\" Polyzalus was the ruler of Gela in Sicily. Who made the statue, nobody knows.","During the Second World War, he was sent to Athens and stored in the vaults of the Bank of Greece.","One more thing for Bible readers, right by the museum exit: an inscription that mentions the Roman proconsul Gallio. Acts chapter eighteen names Gallio as the governor Paul was brought before in Corinth: \"when Gallio was the deputy of Achaia.\"","Before you leave, look at his face once more. Calm, a little inward, the race already won. All those cities came here to ask about the future. He just holds the reins, and waits."]}] },
  { id: "athens-kapnikarea", v: "1-d65c21", city: /Athens|Piraeus/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(kapnikarea|kapnikarea church)\b/i, title: "Panagia Kapnikarea", where: "Panagia Kapnikarea", checked: "2026-11-23",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Church of Panagia Kapnikarea","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Church_of_the_Panagia_Kapnikarea","when":"2026-11-23"}],
    parts: [{"name":"The church in the street","minutes":1,"stand":"On Ermou Street, a few steps back from the little church that sits in the middle of it, so you can see the whole building.","paras":["Here's a surprise in the middle of the shopping street. This is the Panagia Kapnikarea, a Greek Orthodox church, and one of the oldest churches in Athens that's still in use. It's thought to date from the 11th century, perhaps around 1050. The street was planned around it much later. The church was here first.","It nearly didn't survive. When the new city plan of Athens was drawn up for King Otto, this church was considered for demolition. It was the King of Bavaria, Ludwig the First, who objected and saved it."]},{"name":"Reading the building","minutes":1,"stand":"Walk slowly around the outside of the church.","paras":["It's really three pieces joined together, built one after another. The bigger church, on the south side, with the dome, is dedicated to the Presentation of Mary in the Temple. On the north side is a chapel of Saint Barbara. And on the west end there's an outer porch with a gateway.","What was here before? Probably an ancient temple to a goddess, perhaps Athena, perhaps Demeter; nobody knows which. And the church may once have been the main church of a monastery.","If the door is open and a service isn't on, step inside quietly for a moment. Outside, the street rushes past. In here, people have been praying for close to a thousand years."]}] },
  { id: "athens-lycabettus", v: "1-6ae8ee", city: /Athens|Piraeus/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(lycabettus|lykavittos|lycabettos)\b/i, title: "Lycabettus Hill", where: "Lycabettus Hill", checked: "2026-11-21",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Mount Lycabettus","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Lycabettus","when":"2026-11-21"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Athens","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Athens","when":"2026-11-21"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Getting up","minutes":1,"stand":"At the start of the path or the funicular station on Aristippou Street.","paras":["Lycabettus is the highest point in central Athens, 277 metres above sea level. You can ride the funicular from Aristippou Street in Kolonaki, or walk the paved path, which Wikivoyage reckons takes about 30 minutes.","If you walk, you'll climb through pines that cover the lower slopes of this big limestone hill."]},{"name":"At the top","minutes":1,"stand":"On the terrace beside the small white chapel, looking southwest toward the Acropolis.","paras":["Here's your reward. Take a moment to look out over the city and find the Acropolis.","Up here are the little Chapel of Saint George, from the 1800s, a restaurant, and just below, an open-air theatre that reopened in September 2023 after 15 years closed.","And the name? It may come from the Greek word for wolf, from a time when wolves roamed here, or from an older, pre-Greek word. Nobody's sure. There's a myth too: Athena was carrying a mountain to build up the Acropolis, dropped it, and here it is.","Stay for sunset if you can, and then watch the city lights come on."]}] },
  { id: "athens-metropolis", v: "1-f19dbc", city: /Athens|Piraeus/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(metropolitan cathedral|mitropoli|metropolis|little metropolis)\b/i, title: "Athens Cathedral and the Little Metropolis", where: "Athens Cathedral and the Little Metropolis", checked: "2026-11-21",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Metropolitan Cathedral of Athens","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metropolitan_Cathedral_of_Athens","when":"2026-11-21"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Athens","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Athens","when":"2026-11-21"}],
    parts: [{"name":"The cathedral","minutes":1,"stand":"In Mitropoleos Square, facing the cathedral's front.","paras":["This is the Metropolitan Cathedral of the Annunciation, the cathedral of the Archbishop of Athens and all Greece. King Otto and Queen Amalia laid its first stone on Christmas Day 1842.","It wasn't quick. It took several architects and 20 years, and builders used marble from 72 demolished churches. It was dedicated in May 1862.","Inside lie two people Greeks hold very dear. Saint Philothei, martyred in 1589, remembered for buying the freedom of Greek women enslaved under Ottoman rule. And Patriarch Gregory the Fifth, hanged in 1821 in revenge for the Greek uprising. Greek sailors rescued his body, and eventually it was enshrined here in Athens.","If a service is on, step in quietly. A good rule of thumb for Greek churches: no short sleeves or bare legs, as a mark of respect."]},{"name":"The Little Metropolis","minutes":1,"stand":"Walk to the small stone church just south of the cathedral.","paras":["Now look at this little church beside the giant. It's Saint Eleftherios, nicknamed the Little Metropolis.","We don't yet have a source we trust for its history, so we won't guess. It's well worth a slow walk around the outside.","In the square, the two statues are Constantine the Eleventh, the last Byzantine emperor, and Archbishop Damaskinos, who led the church in Athens through the Second World War."]}] },
  { id: "athens-monastiraki", v: "1-7df3c0", city: /Athens|Piraeus/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(monastiraki|flea market)\b/i, title: "Monastiraki and the flea market", where: "Monastiraki and the flea market", checked: "2026-11-21",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Monastiraki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monastiraki","when":"2026-11-21"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Church of the Pantanassa, Athens","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pantanassa_Monastiraki_church","when":"2026-11-21"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Tzistarakis Mosque","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tzistarakis_Mosque","when":"2026-11-21"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Temple of Olympian Zeus, Athens","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Olympian_Zeus%2C_Athens","when":"2026-11-21"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Hadrian's Library","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hadrian's_Library","when":"2026-11-21"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Athens","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Athens","when":"2026-11-21"}],
    parts: [{"name":"The square","minutes":1,"stand":"In the middle of Monastiraki Square, by the metro entrance, with the small church in front of you and the Acropolis rising behind the rooftops.","paras":["Welcome to Monastiraki, the little monastery. The name comes from that small church in the square, the Pantanassa. It was the main church of a monastery that has since vanished. The encyclopedia dates it to the 900s AD; we haven't found a second source to confirm that.","Its monastery was once called the Great Monastery. Later it became the little monastery, and that name spread to the whole neighbourhood.","Today it's a flea market district and one of the main places to shop in Athens: antiques, souvenirs, crafts and plenty of oddities. Keep your bag zipped in the crowds."]},{"name":"The mosque","minutes":1,"stand":"Turn to face the building with the dome and the arched porch on the square.","paras":["That domed building is the Tzistarakis Mosque, built in 1759 by the Ottoman governor of Athens. Our source lists it as part of the Museum of Greek Folk Art, with a pottery collection inside, but that may be out of date. Check whether it's open before you plan to go in.","It comes with a dark story. An inscription at the Temple of Olympian Zeus records a column pulled down there in 1759, and a chronicler blamed this governor, who wanted marble to make lime for his mosque. Another account says the column came from Hadrian's Library next door instead. Either way, tradition says he was dismissed for sacrilege, and when plague broke out later that year, some Athenians said it was the price.","After independence it changed roles many times. In March 1834 it even hosted a ball for the new king, Otto."]},{"name":"Hadrian's Library","minutes":1,"stand":"Walk a few steps behind the mosque to the railings along the line of tall columns.","paras":["Behind the mosque, those columns are the front of Hadrian's Library, built by the emperor Hadrian in AD 132.","Picture the inside: shelves of papyrus rolls, reading rooms, lecture halls in the corners, gilded wooden ceilings and marble walls. Invaders wrecked it in 267, and later three churches were built inside the ruins.","Monastiraki is easy to reach and easy to leave. The metro station right on the square serves Lines 1 and 3."]}] },
  { id: "athens-nam", v: "1-19e48f", city: /Athens|Piraeus/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(national archaeological museum|archaeological museum of athens|ethniko archaiologiko)\b/i, title: "National Archaeological Museum", where: "National Archaeological Museum", checked: "2026-11-23",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"National Archaeological Museum, Athens","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Archaeological_Museum%2C_Athens","when":"2026-11-23"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Mask of Agamemnon","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mask_of_Agamemnon","when":"2026-11-23"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Artemision Bronze","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artemision_Bronze","when":"2026-11-23"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Antikythera mechanism","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antikythera_mechanism","when":"2026-11-23"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Arrival","minutes":1,"stand":"In front of the museum's main entrance, facing the building.","paras":["This is the National Archaeological Museum. It's considered one of the greatest museums in the world, with the richest collection of ancient Greek treasures anywhere. The garden in front, with its sculptures, is part of the welcome, if it's open. A big underground extension is planned right here, so there may be building work.","The building took a long time. It was begun in 1866 and finished in 1889. But the idea is older: Greece's first national archaeological museum was set up on the island of Aigina in 1829, by the country's first governor, Ioannis Kapodistrias.","This is a big building, and it's easy to get lost in it. I'll take you to three highlights. Follow the signs, and if you can't find something, just ask the staff. They're used to the question."]},{"name":"The Mask of Agamemnon","minutes":2,"stand":"Inside, follow the signs for the Mycenaean collection and stop in front of the gold funeral masks. Ask the staff if you can't find them.","paras":["Look for the gold face with the pointed beard. This is the famous Mask of Agamemnon, a gold funeral mask from Mycenae. The historian Cathy Gere called it the Mona Lisa of prehistory. But here's the twist: the famous name was given in error.","The German archaeologist Heinrich Schliemann dug it up in 1876. He believed he'd found the body of Agamemnon himself, the king from Homer's Iliad. The story goes that he telegraphed King George: I have gazed upon the face of Agamemnon.","So why the doubt? Modern research suggests the mask dates to around the 16th century BC. That's some 300 to 400 years before the time of the Trojan War, which is itself the stuff of myth. Near the end of his life, Schliemann accepted the doubts. He's quoted as saying, all right, let's call him Schulze.","Lean in and look at how it was made. One thick sheet of gold, hammered over a wooden form, with the details cut in afterwards. Masks like these covered the faces of Mycenaean nobles in their graves.","There's one more argument you should know about. Some scholars, mainly William Calder and David Traill, have questioned whether the mask is genuine at all. It is three-dimensional where three of the other masks are flat, and it's the only Mycenaean face with that full beard and handlebar moustache. Modern research, though, suggests it is genuine. The debate is part of its story now."]},{"name":"The god from the sea","minutes":1,"stand":"Follow the signs or ask for the Artemision Bronze, and stand back far enough to see the whole figure.","paras":["Here is a god, caught in bronze, about to throw. This statue was pulled from the sea off Cape Artemision, in northern Euboea. It's slightly over life size, 2.09 meters tall. Most scholars think it's Zeus, about to hurl a thunderbolt. Some think it's Poseidon, with a trident. Opinion is still divided.","Look at the right hand. It's empty, and that's the whole argument. Whatever it held is lost. One point for Zeus: walk round to the side, and imagine a long trident there. It would hide the face.","Now look at the eyes. They're empty sockets today, but once they were inlaid, probably with bone. The eyebrows were silver, and the lips were copper. Imagine those eyes looking back at you. And the sculptor? Nobody knows.","It's dated to around 460 BC. It came up from a shipwreck, first found in 1926. A diver died there in 1928, the exploration was abandoned, and it was never taken up again. Later, the god's head went on a Greek 500 drachma postage stamp and a 1,000 drachma banknote.","The scholar Carol Mattusch put it beautifully. The figure is poised to throw, but the action is just beginning. Stand here a moment and you'll feel it too."]},{"name":"The Antikythera mechanism","minutes":2,"stand":"Follow the signs or ask for the Antikythera mechanism, and stand in front of the corroded bronze pieces and the models beside them.","paras":["These corroded bronze pieces are the Antikythera mechanism. It was a hand-powered model of the heavens, probably built in the 2nd century BC, though scholars' dates differ, and found in 1901. It's the oldest known example of an analogue computer. Turn a handle, and it could predict eclipses decades ahead.","Sponge divers from the island of Symi found the shipwreck in early 1900: a Roman cargo ship, 45 meters down, or 148 feet. When the finds reached this museum, the mechanism looked like a lump of corroded bronze and wood. At first nobody noticed it, while the staff worked on the statues.","Then, on May 17, 1902, the archaeologist Valerios Stais and his cousin Spyridon spotted a gear wheel inside one of the pieces. Valerios Stais had been the museum's curator since 1887. Today it survives in 82 fragments, all kept by this museum, with reconstructions and replicas to show how it worked.","Scans made in 2005 suggest it had 35 bronze gears meshing together, and 30 can still be seen. It could even track the four-year cycle of the ancient Olympic Games. The astronomer Hipparchus, known as Hipparchus of Rhodes, may have been consulted on it, though nobody knows for sure.","Think about this. Machines this complex did not appear again until the 14th century, in western Europe. Somebody, all those centuries ago, was thinking very far ahead."]},{"name":"Closing","minutes":1,"stand":"On your way out, back outside the main entrance.","paras":["One last thing. During the Second World War, this museum closed, and its treasures were sealed in boxes and buried, to save them from destruction and looting. In 1945 they came back on display. Think of the people who did that work, so that one day you could stand here and look."]}] },
  { id: "athens-national-garden", v: "1-b3537d", city: /Athens|Piraeus/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(national garden|national gardens|royal garden|zappeion|ethnikos kipos)\b/i, title: "National Garden and Zappeion", where: "National Garden and Zappeion", checked: "2026-11-23",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"National Garden, Athens","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Garden%2C_Athens","when":"2026-11-23"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Athens","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Athens","when":"2026-11-21"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Zappeion","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zappeion","when":"2026-11-23"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Queen Amalia's garden","minutes":1,"stand":"At the main entrance on Amalias Avenue, among the tall palms, facing into the garden.","paras":["Welcome to the National Garden, right behind the Parliament building. It used to be the Royal Garden. Queen Amalia commissioned it in 1838, and it was finished by 1840. Those palms around the entrance? The entrance was moved here to the 12 palms she planted, in her honor.","If the city is hot today, you've come to the right place. One travel guide calls this easily the coolest place to hide from the summer sun.","It was laid out by a German agronomist, Friedrich Schmidt. He brought in over 500 kinds of plants, and peacocks, ducks and turtles too. Sadly a lot of the plants couldn't take the dry Mediterranean climate and died. For a long time part of it was the private refuge of the King and Queen. People could already visit in the afternoons; in the 1920s it was opened to the public as a park, and in time it took the name National Garden."]},{"name":"Inside the garden","minutes":1,"stand":"Follow the paths into the garden, and stop wherever you find shade.","paras":["Keep your eyes open as you wander. There are ancient ruins tucked in among the trees, mostly Roman: columns, and even a Roman villa with a mosaic. There's a duck pond, and look out for the turtles and the tortoises that live here.","Look out, too, for the busts. One is of Ioannis Kapodistrias, the first governor of Greece. Another is the poet Dionysios Solomos, who wrote the words of the Greek national anthem."]},{"name":"The monkey's bite","minutes":1,"stand":"Anywhere on the shaded paths.","paras":["Now a very strange story. In 1920, King Alexander was bitten by a pet monkey, and about three weeks later he died of the infection. One account says it happened here in the garden; we're not sure of that.","His death brought back his deposed father, King Constantine. Historians have argued that the political change that followed helped lead to Greece's defeat in Asia Minor, the disaster Greeks call the Catastrophe. Winston Churchill wrote that perhaps a quarter of a million people died of this monkey's bite."]},{"name":"The Zappeion","minutes":1,"stand":"Take the gate that leads from the garden into the Zappeion park, and face the large building.","paras":["This grand building is the Zappeion. It was the first building ever put up specifically for the revival of the Olympic Games. It was designed by Theophil Hansen and opened on October 20, 1888.","It's named for Evangelis Zappas, who had started his own Zappian Olympic Games, a forerunner of the modern Olympics. He paid for this, but didn't live to see it built. And here's an odd detail: his head is said to be buried inside one of the building's walls.","At the 1896 Olympics this was the main fencing hall, and in 1906 it housed the athletes. In 1938 Greece's first national radio station started broadcasting from here. And in May 1979, in its marble atrium, Greece signed the papers to join the European Community."]},{"name":"Closing","minutes":1,"stand":"Find a bench in the shade before you leave.","paras":["The writer Henry Miller, writing in 1939, said this garden stayed in his memory like no other park he had known. He called it the quintessence of a park. Sit a minute before you go back into the traffic. That's what this garden has been for, ever since the gates opened to everyone."]}] },
  { id: "athens-olympieion", v: "1-37d216", city: /Athens|Piraeus/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(olympian zeus|olympieion|hadrian's arch|arch of hadrian)\b/i, title: "Temple of Olympian Zeus and Hadrian's Arch", where: "Temple of Olympian Zeus and Hadrian's Arch", checked: "2026-11-21",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Temple of Olympian Zeus, Athens","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Olympian_Zeus%2C_Athens","when":"2026-11-21"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Tzistarakis Mosque","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tzistarakis_Mosque","when":"2026-11-21"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Arch of Hadrian (Athens)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arch_of_Hadrian_(Athens)","when":"2026-11-21"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Hadrian's Arch","minutes":1,"stand":"On the pavement beside the marble arch, well back from the kerb of busy Amalias Avenue.","paras":["This marble gateway is Hadrian's Arch. Over the opening, on each side, a line of Greek is carved. On the side facing the Acropolis, it says: this is Athens, the ancient city of Theseus.","The other side, facing the temple, says: this is the city of Hadrian, and not of Theseus. Quite a compliment to a Roman emperor. Nobody knows for certain who put the arch up, but it was probably the citizens of Athens.","Historians aren't sure what the words mean: the whole city, or an old part and a new part. The arch itself was probably put up to honour Hadrian around AD 131 or 132."]},{"name":"The temple that took six centuries","minutes":1,"stand":"Inside the site, at the end of the path where the tall columns stand in a group.","paras":["Now look at those giant columns. This was meant to be the greatest temple in the ancient world, for Zeus, king of the gods. Its story is one of the great builders' sagas.","Around 550 BC the tyrant Peisistratus built a temple here, and around 520 BC his sons started this enormous one. Then it stalled. Only the platform and parts of the columns were done, and it sat that way for 336 years. In 174 BC a Greek king, Antiochus the Fourth, restarted the work, until his death in 164 BC stopped it again. Then the Roman general Sulla carried off some of the unfinished columns to Rome.","Finally the emperor Hadrian finished it and dedicated it in AD 132, more than six centuries after it was started. It had 104 columns, each about 17 metres tall, the largest temple in Greece. Inside stood a gold-and-ivory Zeus, and next to the god, a statue of Hadrian himself."]},{"name":"What happened to the columns","minutes":1,"stand":"Stand where you can see the single column lying on the ground in pieces.","paras":["So where did 104 columns go? By 1436 a traveller counted only 21 standing. Over the centuries the marble was simply taken.","One loss is written on the stone itself. An inscription on a surviving column records that on 27 April 1759 a column was pulled down. A chronicler blamed the Ottoman governor, Tzistarakis, who wanted marble for plaster for his new mosque. Another account says it was more likely a column from Hadrian's Library. Either way, his mosque still stands in Monastiraki.","Fifteen columns stand today. The one lying in pieces at your feet came down in a storm in 1852. It's the clearest sense you'll get of just how huge they are."]}] },
  { id: "athens-philopappos", v: "1-fe39b2", city: /Athens|Piraeus/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(philopappos|philopappou|filopappou|muses hill|mouseion)\b/i, title: "Philopappos Hill", where: "Philopappos Hill", checked: "2026-11-23",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Philopappos Monument","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philopappos_Monument","when":"2026-11-23"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Athens","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Athens","when":"2026-11-21"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Pnyx","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pnyx","when":"2026-11-23"}],
    parts: [{"name":"On the hill","minutes":1,"stand":"On the path up the hill, at a spot under the trees where you can look back at the Acropolis.","paras":["This is Philopappos Hill. It's also called the Hill of the Muses. Today it takes its name from the marble tomb up ahead. It's planted with pines and other trees, more like a small forest than a city park.","A word about your feet. Wear shoes with good grip. And this is a walk for daylight: the hill is best avoided at night."]},{"name":"The Philopappos Monument","minutes":2,"stand":"At the marble monument, facing its front.","paras":["This is the tomb of Philopappos, a prince from the Kingdom of Commagene, who lived from 65 to 116 AD. When he died, his sister Julia Balbilla and the people of Athens built this for him. Putting it here, opposite the Acropolis, inside the city, says how much Athens thought of him.","Look at the front. Along the lower level, a carved frieze shows him as a Roman consul, riding in a chariot. Above, there were three statues: his grandfather, King Antiochus, on the left, Philopappos himself in the center, and on the right, Seleucus, the king his family claimed descent from. That one is now lost.","Here's what makes him interesting. In the niche below his statue, a Greek inscription gives his name as an Athenian citizen. In the niche to the left, a Latin one lists his Roman career, consul included. So here's a prince from the East who was also an Athenian and a Roman.","Tradition held that this spot was already a grave: the poet and seer Musaeus, said to be from the 6th century BC, was believed to be buried here. And the ancient travel writer Pausanias summed the tomb up as a monument built for a Syrian man.","Only two-thirds of the front survives. A traveler who visited in 1436 wrote that it was still intact. Later some of its stones were carried off and used to build the Ottoman minaret in the Parthenon, right across the way."]},{"name":"The Pnyx","minutes":1,"stand":"Make your way to the Pnyx, following the signs, and stand on the open ground in front of the big flat stone platform cut into the hillside.","paras":["This hillside is the Pnyx. From perhaps as early as 507 BC, this is where the Athenian assembly met. It's one of the earliest and most important places in the story of democracy. That stone platform in front of you was the bema, the speakers' platform.","Pericles spoke on this hill, and Aristides, and Alcibiades, all within sight of the Parthenon. Demosthenes stood here to attack Philip of Macedon. And from up here, the Pnyx looks down on the old Agora, the city's marketplace.","Every debate opened with the same question: Who wishes to speak? That was the idea of isegoria, the equal right of every citizen to debate.","Let's be honest about who that meant. Citizens were free men born in the city, perhaps 20 percent of the adults. About 6,000 could stand on this ground, and scholars think later changes may have made room for as many as 13,000. And in practice, relatively few ever spoke. Men over 50 had the right to be heard first.","By the 1st century BC the assembly had moved to the Theatre of Dionysus. One more thing up here: the foundations of the oldest known observatory, Meton's. His 19-year calendar cycle turns up on the Antikythera mechanism."]},{"name":"Closing","minutes":1,"stand":"Stay facing the speakers' platform.","paras":["Picture the presiding officer standing up, looking out over thousands of men, and asking, Who wishes to speak? Then the silence, while someone found the nerve. It's a good question to carry down the hill with you."]}] },
  { id: "athens-plaka", v: "1-581883", city: /Athens|Piraeus/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(plaka|anafiotika)\b/i, title: "Plaka and Anafiotika", where: "Plaka and Anafiotika", checked: "2026-11-21",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Plaka","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plaka","when":"2026-11-21"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Anafiotika","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anafiotika","when":"2026-11-21"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Plaka","minutes":1,"stand":"On Adrianou Street, any spot where you can see the Acropolis rock above the rooftops.","paras":["This is Plaka, the old neighbourhood wrapped around the slopes below the Acropolis. People call it the Neighbourhood of the Gods. It's the oldest district in Athens, with people living here since the Stone Age. Even so, it was briefly emptied during the battles of 1826, in the War of Independence.","You're walking on very old lines. Some of these streets, this one, Adrianou, among them, follow routes that go back to ancient times.","Even the name is a small mystery. Some say Plaka comes from words meaning old Athens in the language of Albanian settlers, who lived here in large numbers until the late 1800s. Others say it's from a stone plaque that once marked a crossroads. Nobody knows for sure.","In 1884 a fire burned a large part of the neighbourhood. Archaeologists used the gap to dig at the Roman Agora and Hadrian's Library.","One more detail. According to the encyclopedia, Plaka's power, water and phone lines all run in tunnels underground, and it's the only Athens neighbourhood done that way."]},{"name":"Anafiotika","minutes":1,"stand":"From Stratonos Street, take the narrow lanes up toward the Acropolis rock. The steps are steep and uneven.","paras":["Suddenly you're on a Greek island. This is Anafiotika. In the time of King Otto, builders from the island of Anafi came to work on the new royal palace, and they built themselves homes here the way they did back home.","Small white cube houses, steps that end at little terraces, and dead ends everywhere. Refugees from Asia Minor joined them in 1922.","Only 45 houses are left. The lanes have no names, and the houses go by numbers.","People live in these houses, so keep your voice down.","Find a terrace, look out over the city, and catch your breath. This is one of the quietest corners in the centre of Athens."]}] },
  { id: "athens-promenade", v: "1-55d71e", city: /Athens|Piraeus/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(dionysiou areopagitou|areopagitou|acropolis promenade)\b/i, title: "Acropolis promenade", where: "Acropolis promenade", checked: "2026-11-23",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Dionysiou Areopagitou Street","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dionysiou_Areopagitou_Street","when":"2026-11-23"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Athens","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Athens","when":"2026-11-21"},{"pub":"King James Version (public domain), via bible-api.com","title":"","url":"https://bible-api.com/acts%2017:16-34?translation=kjv","when":"2026-11-21"}],
    parts: [{"name":"The start of the walk","minutes":1,"stand":"At the eastern end of the street, near Amalias Avenue and the Arch of Hadrian, facing west along the car-free street.","paras":["This is Dionysiou Areopagitou Street, the walking street that runs along the south slope of the Acropolis. From here it heads west, all the way to Philopappos Hill, and along the way you get views up to the Acropolis and the Parthenon.","In summer this walk gets very hot. Carry water, drink enough of it, and put on sunscreen.","The street got its present shape in 1955, from the architect Dimitris Pikionis. He also designed the paved paths up on the archaeological site. Cars were finally taken off it in 2003.","For the 2004 Olympics, Athens joined up its ancient sites with a network of walking streets. Today a mostly car-free walk runs from here, past the Acropolis Museum and the Odeon, all the way to Kerameikos."]},{"name":"Who the street is named for","minutes":1,"stand":"Keep walking west, and pause anywhere you can see the Acropolis rock on your right.","paras":["The street's name takes you back to the Bible. The street is named after Dionysius the Areopagite. The book of Acts tells how the apostle Paul spoke to the Athenians, and Dionysius was among those who believed.","Here are the words, from Acts chapter seventeen, in the King James Version: Howbeit certain men clave unto him, and believed: among the which was Dionysius the Areopagite, and a woman named Damaris, and others with them.","That's Scripture. What came later is the Church's memory of him: today Dionysius is the patron saint of Athens."]},{"name":"Along the slope","minutes":1,"stand":"Past Makrygianni Street, where the buildings on your right stop and the slope of the Acropolis begins.","paras":["Notice how the houses on the right have stopped. From here on, that side of the street belongs to the archaeological site. Near here is the entrance to the Theatre of Dionysus, and across the street, on your left, is the Acropolis Museum.","A small detail along the way: near the Acropolis Museum, the composer Vangelis owned a neoclassical mansion."]},{"name":"Toward Philopappos Hill","minutes":1,"stand":"Near the western end, by the entrance to the Odeon of Herodes Atticus.","paras":["Here, near the end, is the entrance to the Odeon of Herodes Atticus. When the street was first mapped, in 1857, it actually ran further north, right beside the Odeon.","Ahead, the street ends near Philopappos Hill. The walking route carries on as Apostolou Pavlou, around the Acropolis and the Agora.","So the man this street is named after was one listener who said yes. Of all the people who heard Paul in Athens, his is the name you've been walking on. One person who listened, remembered by a whole city."]}] },
  { id: "athens-roman-agora", v: "1-b2bdee", city: /Athens|Piraeus/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(roman agora|tower of the winds|aerides|archegetis|horologion)\b/i, title: "Roman Agora and Tower of the Winds", where: "Roman Agora and Tower of the Winds", checked: "2026-11-23",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Roman Agora","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Agora_Athens","when":"2026-11-21"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Tower of the Winds","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tower_of_the_Winds","when":"2026-11-21"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Antikythera mechanism","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antikythera_mechanism","when":"2026-11-23"}],
    parts: [{"name":"The Roman gate","minutes":1,"stand":"At the Gate of Athena Archegetis, the western gate of the Roman Agora, facing into the site.","paras":["This is the Roman Agora, a marketplace from Roman times, just north of the Acropolis. The gate in front of you is the Gate of Athena Archegetis, the old western entrance. Behind the colonnade on this side there used to be shops.","Historians aren't sure of the exact date: it was built between 27 and 17 BC, or possibly in 10 BC, by a man called Eucles of Marathon. The money came from the emperor Augustus, to keep a promise Julius Caesar had made back in 51 BC. Athens already had a marketplace, about 100 meters west of here. This was the new one.","This gate carries a very Roman detail. An inscription on it records a decree from the reign of the emperor Hadrian, about the taxes owed by oil merchants. Even Athena's gateway had a tax notice on it."]},{"name":"The market square","minutes":1,"stand":"Walk in a little and look across the open ground toward the eight-sided tower at the far side.","paras":["This was a big open square, surrounded by a colonnade. Not all of it has been dug up, even today. Picture stalls, traders, and arguments over prices.","Watch your feet. The ground here is uneven, with loose stones and old foundations.","There was a fountain on the south side. Another inscription mentions an office for the market officials, the people who looked after the market. And next to the east gate, a very practical thing: public toilets."]},{"name":"The Tower of the Winds","minutes":2,"stand":"Walk to the eight-sided marble tower and stand where you can look up at the carved figures around its top.","paras":["This is the Tower of the Winds. It's made of marble, with eight sides, and it's named after the eight wind gods carved around the top. It's one of very few buildings from classical antiquity still standing almost whole, and the only clock tower that survives from the ancient world.","Look up at the winds. They all have big wings, and they're all men, some young, some old. You can tell them apart by what they carry and how warmly they're dressed. Boreas, the north wind, and Zephyrus, the west wind, are among them. Their names are carved above them, but they're hard to read from down here.","Now look at the straight lines cut into the walls. Those are sundials. The lines marked the hours, and the little rods that cast the shadows are modern replacements. Inside there was once a big water clock. And on the very top, the Roman writer Vitruvius tells us, stood a bronze Triton holding a rod that showed which way the wind blew. That has completely disappeared.","It's 12 meters tall, about 39 feet, and about 8 meters across. Nobody knows exactly when it was built: probably around 50 BC, though some put it earlier. The ancient writers Vitruvius and Varro say it was designed by an astronomer, Andronicus of Cyrrhus. Beyond that, very little is known about him.","The water for the clock came down from a spring on the Acropolis. How the clock worked, nobody's sure. It may have been an astronomical clock, showing a calendar and the stars, like the Antikythera mechanism you can see in the National Archaeological Museum."]},{"name":"The tower's later lives","minutes":1,"stand":"Stay by the tower.","paras":["This tower has kept busy. In late antiquity it was probably used as a church or a baptistery. Under the Ottomans it was half buried. From 1751 to 1821 dervishes of the Qadiri order used it for their religious rituals.","One writer put it this way: with this building, citizens could orient themselves in space and time. That's what it was for. Someone hurrying through this market could glance up, see the hour and the wind, and know where they were in their day. Not so different from us, checking the time as we walk."]}] },
  { id: "athens-sounion", v: "1-c68bf7", city: /Athens|Piraeus/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(sounion|sounio|sunium)\b/i, title: "Temple of Poseidon at Sounion", where: "Temple of Poseidon at Sounion", checked: "2026-11-23",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Sounion","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sounion","when":"2026-11-23"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Temple of Poseidon, Sounion","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Poseidon%2C_Sounion","when":"2026-11-23"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Arrival","minutes":1,"stand":"On the path up to the temple, where you first see the columns against the sky.","paras":["This is Cape Sounion, the southernmost tip of Attica, and that's the Temple of Poseidon on the headland, with the Aegean on three sides. It was built around 444 to 440 BC, in the time of Pericles, the same man behind the Parthenon.","Two things to keep in mind here. Stay well back from the cliff edges. And there's almost no shade, so a hat and water are a good idea.","Why Poseidon? The Greeks called him master of the sea, and Athens lived by the sea: by its trade and by its navy. A god of the sea mattered here."]},{"name":"The temple","minutes":1,"stand":"Walk up close to the temple and face its columns.","paras":["Originally there were 34 columns. Fifteen are counted standing today. They're Doric, of local white marble, and each was 20 feet tall.","In the middle was a windowless hall, and it would have held a colossal bronze Poseidon, 20 feet tall, right up to the ceiling. If you've seen the Temple of Hephaestus in Athens, picture this one looking much like it. They may even have had the same architect, though nobody knows for sure.","This wasn't the first temple here. An older one stood on this spot, probably destroyed by the Persians in 480 BC. When the Athenians beat the Persians at sea at Salamis, they brought a whole captured enemy warship here, as a trophy for Poseidon. The temple you see was built on the old one's ruins."]},{"name":"Watching the sea","minutes":1,"stand":"Go to the seaward side of the temple, staying well back from the edge, and look out over the water.","paras":["You're standing almost 200 feet above the sea. From here you could see ships sailing to and from Piraeus, first as they came in and last as they went out. Some scholars think that view is a big reason the temple is here.","Sounion is old in story, too. The earliest mention is in Homer's Odyssey. In the poem, sailing home from Troy, King Menelaus loses his helmsman as they round \"Holy Sounion, Cape of Athens,\" and he lands here to give him a proper funeral.","Northeast of the temple, about 300 meters away, is a low hill. That's where the temple of Athena stood, next to a shrine identified as the tomb of that same helmsman, Phrontis.","Athens took this place seriously. Herodotus says that in the 6th century BC, Athenians held a festival here every four years, and their leaders sailed down to the cape in a sacred boat. Later, in 413 BC, during the war with Sparta, they fortified this place with a wall and towers to protect the grain ships. And then, in a twist, rebel slaves from the nearby silver mines of Laurium seized the fortress."]},{"name":"Names on the stone","minutes":1,"stand":"Walk slowly around the outside of the temple and look at the bases of the columns.","paras":["From the 1600s, this cape was known as Capo Colonne, the Cape of Columns, because, unusually, several of these columns had stayed standing since ancient times.","On the base of one of these columns is the name of Lord Byron, carved into the stone. You may not be able to get close enough to pick it out. Did he carve it himself? There's no direct evidence, so nobody knows.","Pieces of this temple are scattered far away. In 1825 two columns collapsed. One was taken to Venice, and drums from a column ended up in England, in the garden at Chatsworth House and in the British Museum."]},{"name":"Sunset","minutes":1,"stand":"Find a safe spot with a view of the temple and the western sky.","paras":["People have come here for the sunset over the Aegean, seen from these ruins, ever since modern tourism began in the early 1800s.","The philosopher Martin Heidegger came here in 1962. In the strong sea breeze, he wrote, \"these few standing columns were the strings of an invisible lyre.\"","And for ships nearing Attica, this headland was easy to see. Stand still a moment, and imagine being out on that water, watching these columns catch the last of the light."]}] },
  { id: "athens-syntagma", v: "1-8705e9", city: /Athens|Piraeus/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(syntagma|tomb of the unknown soldier|changing of the guard|evzones)\b/i, title: "Syntagma Square and the changing of the guard", where: "Syntagma Square and the changing of the guard", checked: "2026-11-21",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Syntagma Square","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Syntagma_Square","when":"2026-11-21"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Old Royal Palace","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_Royal_Palace_Athens_Hellenic_Parliament","when":"2026-11-21"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Hellenic Parliament","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hellenic_Parliament","when":"2026-11-21"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Tomb of the Unknown Soldier (Athens)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tomb_of_the_Unknown_Soldier_Athens","when":"2026-11-21"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Presidential Guard (Greece)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Presidential_Guard_(Greece)","when":"2026-11-21"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Athens","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Athens","when":"2026-11-21"}],
    parts: [{"name":"The square","minutes":1,"stand":"At the top of the marble steps on the east side of the square, facing the big pale building across the avenue.","paras":["This is Syntagma, which means Constitution. The square is named for the constitution the first king of Greece, Otto, was forced to grant after an uprising on 3 September 1843.","The building across the road was Otto's palace, designed by the Bavarian architect Friedrich von Gärtner, and begun in 1836. In 1929 the government decided it would house Parliament for good, and the Senate first met there in 1934. The Parliament isn't open to visitors, but the National Gardens beside it are."]},{"name":"The Tomb of the Unknown Soldier","minutes":1,"stand":"Cross at the lights and stand at the edge of the open space in front of the Parliament, facing the carved wall with the guards. Stay behind any barrier.","paras":["The carved wall is the Tomb of the Unknown Soldier, a memorial for every Greek soldier killed in war. The sculptor Fokion Rok carved a fallen soldier lying on the ground, and it was unveiled on 25 March 1932.","The guards are Evzones, from the Presidential Guard, the last Evzone unit in the Greek army. Watch them. They hold perfectly still, and every fifteen minutes they swap places in slow, highly stylised moves. A changeover happens every hour on the hour.","The uniform comes from the fighters of the War of Independence. On most days the guards wear a long tunic, navy blue in winter and light khaki in summer. On Sundays and holidays they wear the white kilt, the fustanella, made from 30 metres of cloth. Tradition says it has 400 pleats, one for each year of Ottoman rule. And those red clogs with the black pompoms weigh around 1.5 kilos each.","On Sunday mornings at 11 there's the Grand Change, with the whole guard, its officers and a military band.","Please don't touch the guards or step into their path. They are on duty at a war memorial."]}] },
  { id: "athens-theatre-dionysus", v: "1-e31813", city: /Athens|Piraeus/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(theatre of dionysus|theater of dionysus|theatre of dionysos)\b/i, title: "Theatre of Dionysus", where: "Theatre of Dionysus", checked: "2026-11-23",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Theatre of Dionysus","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theatre_of_Dionysus","when":"2026-11-23"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Athens","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Athens","when":"2026-11-21"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Odeon of Herodes Atticus","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Odeon_of_Herodes_Atticus","when":"2026-11-23"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Arrival","minutes":1,"stand":"Inside the theatre, as close to the front rows of seats as the barriers allow, looking up the slope toward the Acropolis.","paras":["You're standing in the Theatre of Dionysus, on the south slope of the Acropolis. It's considered the oldest theatre of all. This is where Athens honored Dionysus, god of wine and theatre, and where it watched the plays of Aeschylus, Sophocles, Euripides and Aristophanes. Many of the great Greek plays were performed here for the first time.","Watch your step here. The stone is old and uneven, and the edges of the seats aren't always where you expect.","It didn't start in stone. The first performance terrace went in around the mid to late 500s BC, for a festival called the City Dionysia. For a long time the seats were wooden. Then in the 300s BC a statesman called Lycurgus, who ran the city's finances, turned it into the stone theatre you see today.","How many people fit in? Accounts differ. One source says it may have held up to 25,000 at its fullest; another says 19,000. Either way, imagine this whole hillside full of faces."]},{"name":"The seats of honor","minutes":1,"stand":"Move to face the front row of stone chairs, close to the center.","paras":["Now look at these stone chairs in the front rows. These were the seats of honor. Each one carries the name of the priest or official who sat in it. And the throne in the middle belonged to the priest of Dionysus himself.","Behind them, the ordinary seats were 33 centimeters deep and 33 high, and there were seventy-eight rows. Not much room. People sat close here.","Here's a story with a sting in it. Aristophanes' comedy The Clouds, which made fun of Socrates, was first performed on this spot. Plato thought that play helped bring about the trial and execution of Socrates. Laughter can be dangerous."]},{"name":"The theatre's later lives","minutes":1,"stand":"Stay where you can see the open floor in front of the seats.","paras":["That open floor is the orchestra. In Roman times it was paved in colored marble, with a rhombus pattern in the center. And a marble barrier went up around it, in 61 AD or later. Historians think it may have been there to protect the audience during gladiator fights.","Then the plays stopped. After the late 400s AD the theatre was abandoned. The orchestra became the courtyard of a Christian church, and the seats were used as a stone quarry. Real excavation only began in the nineteenth century, with Rousopoulos in 1861."]},{"name":"The Odeon of Herodes Atticus","minutes":1,"stand":"Walk west along the slope, the Acropolis on your right, to where you can look at the tall stone front wall of the Odeon.","paras":["Further along the slope is a second theatre, the Odeon of Herodes Atticus. It was built in AD 161 by Herodes Atticus, in memory of his Roman wife, Regilla.","It had a three-story front wall, which still stands because it was later built into a fortification, and once a roof of cedar from Lebanon. It held 5,000 people for concerts, until it was wrecked by the Heruli in AD 267.","In the 1950s the seats and stage were restored in marble, and it became the main stage of the Athens Festival. Maria Callas sang here in 1957, and Frank Sinatra gave two benefit concerts in 1962. Plans were to close it after the 2026 festival for at least three years of restoration, so don't be surprised by scaffolding, or to find it closed."]},{"name":"Closing","minutes":1,"stand":"Take a last look back toward the Theatre of Dionysus.","paras":["Sit for a moment if you can. Many of the great Greek plays were first heard back there, in the Theatre of Dionysus, by a hillside full of Athenians. The voices are gone, but it's the same hill, and the same sky over it."]}] },
  { id: "barcelona-camp-nou", v: "1-1012d9", city: /Barcelona/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(camp nou|nou camp|spotify camp nou|fc barcelona museum|barca museum|barça museum)\b/i, title: "Camp Nou", where: "Camp Nou", checked: "2026-11-23",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Camp Nou","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Camp_Nou","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"FC Barcelona","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FC_Barcelona","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Johan Cruyff","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johan_Cruyff","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Outside the stadium","minutes":1,"stand":"Stand outside the stadium where you can see the great curve of the stands.","paras":["This is Camp Nou, the New Field, FC Barcelona's home since 1957. It's being rebuilt, and when it's done it will hold 105,000 people, the biggest stadium in Spain and in Europe.","You may see construction work. It started on June 1, 2023, and the team came back to play here in November 2025, with part of the stands still closed. The club's latest plan is to move out again for a while at the start of the 2027-28 season, while the roof goes on. It expects the full 105,000 seats ahead of the 2028-29 season.","It's a building site in places, so watch for barriers, uneven ground and work vehicles around the stadium.","To understand this place, you need the club's motto: Més que un club, More than a club. Barça, founded in 1899, became a symbol of Catalan culture. And it's owned and run by its own supporters."]},{"name":"Building the New Field","minutes":1,"stand":"Stay where you can see the stands.","paras":["The old ground, Les Corts, had no room to grow. Then in 1950 the club signed László Kubala, one of its greatest players ever, and that added to the push for something bigger. The first stone here was laid on March 28, 1954.","It cost more than four times what was planned, and the club borrowed for years to pay for it. But on September 24, 1957 it opened, with a Mass and Handel's Hallelujah chorus, in front of some 90,000 people.","Everybody expected it to be named after the founder, Joan Gamper. Franco's authorities said no. People often say it was because Gamper took his own life. More probably it was who he was: foreign-born, a Catalan nationalist, a liberal and a Protestant.","So the fans just called it Camp Nou, the New Field. It took until July 2001 for a members' vote to make that official.","Language was part of the fight. From 1941 the club was forced to use a Spanish name. Only in November 1973 could it call itself Futbol Club Barcelona again, in Catalan."]},{"name":"Great nights","minutes":1,"stand":"If you're inside on the tour, find a seat looking out over the pitch.","paras":["The biggest crowd for a Barça match here was 120,000, against Juventus, on March 5, 1986. This stadium hosted the opening match of the 1982 World Cup, the Olympic football final in 1992, and European finals.","One of them, in 1999, is still talked about. Bayern Munich were winning as the clock reached 90 minutes. Then Manchester United scored twice in injury time, through Teddy Sheringham and Ole Gunnar Solskjær, and won.","And one strange night of silence. On October 1, 2017, during political turmoil in Catalonia, a league match was played here with the stands completely empty."]},{"name":"Cruyff and the museum","minutes":1,"stand":"If the club museum is open, head there.","paras":["The club museum opened in 1984. In a normal year more than 1.2 million people come through it, which makes it the second-most visited museum in Catalonia.","One name runs through it all: Johan Cruyff. He arrived as a player in 1973 and won the fans over at once. He said he chose Barcelona over Real Madrid because he couldn't play for a club tied to Franco. Then he named his son Jordi, after Saint George, the local Catalan saint.","Later he came back as coach and built what fans called the Dream Team. Before the European Cup final against Sampdoria, his last words to the players were simple: go out and enjoy it. Ronald Koeman's free kick in extra time won the club its first European Cup.","One detail people miss: there's a chapel inside the stadium, for the players."]},{"name":"Closing","minutes":1,"stand":"On your way out, look back at the stands.","paras":["More than a club, they say here. A club owned by its own fans, in a stadium the fans named themselves: the New Field. Standing here, you can feel it."]}] },
  { id: "barcelona-casa-batllo", v: "1-f66dff", city: /Barcelona/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(casa batllo|casa batlló|batllo|batlló|casa amatller|amatller|lleo morera|lleó morera|illa de la discordia|illa de la discòrdia)\b/i, title: "Casa Batlló", where: "Casa Batlló", checked: "2026-11-23",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Casa Batlló","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Casa_Batll%C3%B3","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Casa Amatller","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Casa_Amatller","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Antoni Gaudí","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antoni_Gaud%C3%AD","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Saint George and the Dragon","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_George_and_the_Dragon","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","title":"Works of Antoni Gaudí - UNESCO World Heritage Centre","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/320/","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"The Block of Discord","minutes":1,"stand":"Stand on the far side of Passeig de Gràcia, across from the colorful house with the scaly roof, so you can see its neighbors too.","paras":["Look at this stretch of the avenue. Locals call it the Block of Discord, because of these strikingly different buildings, each by a famous architect.","It was a contest between rich neighbors. In 1898 the chocolate maker Antoni Amatller had his house redone by Josep Puig i Cadafalch, with that stepped Flemish gable. In 1902 Francesca Morera, a few doors along, answered with Lluís Domènech i Montaner. Then in 1904 Josep Batlló, Amatller's next-door neighbor, hired Gaudí, to put the others to shame.","One writer on Gaudí, Michael Eaude, puts it perfectly. The city's industrialists \"fought with each other to build the finest house on the street.\"","Casa Amatller, the one with the stepped gable, stayed in the family until 1960, so its rooms from 1900 are still largely intact. It's a house museum now."]},{"name":"The façade","minutes":1,"stand":"Stay across the street, facing Casa Batlló.","paras":["And here's Gaudí's answer: Casa Batlló. Batlló wanted to knock the old house down. Gaudí talked him into transforming it instead, and it was done by 1906.","Locals call it the House of Bones. Look at the lower floors. Those thin columns look like the bones of a limb, and the balcony railings above are shaped like masks.","The middle of the façade is meant to suggest a lake with water lilies. It sparkles with colored glass and 330 round pottery pieces, designed by Gaudí with Josep Maria Jujol. According to one critic, the broken tile was gathered from the dumpsters of other building sites.","Gaudí is said to have put it this way: \"There are no straight lines or sharp corners in nature. Therefore, buildings must have no straight lines or sharp corners.\" See if you can find one here.","In 1906 the city shortlisted it for building of the year. It lost, to another architect."]},{"name":"The dragon","minutes":1,"stand":"Look up to the roofline.","paras":["Now the roof. That arched ridge looks like the back of a dragon, with ceramic tiles for scales. Green on the right, where the head begins, then blue and violet, then pink and red on the left. The small triangular window toward the right is its eye.","Why a dragon? There's an old legend of Saint George, a soldier saint who defeats a dragon. The dragon was being fed victims chosen by lot, and one day the lot fell on the princess herself. George is the patron saint of Catalonia, and this is known as the House of the Dragon.","That rounded tower with the cross, left of center? A common theory says it's George's lance, plunged into the dragon's back. Nobody knows for sure if Gaudí meant it that way.","On that tower, look for the gold letters for Jesus, Mary and Joseph. The bulb on top arrived broken. Gaudí liked it that way and had the pieces fixed in place."]},{"name":"Inside","minutes":1,"stand":"If you go in, stop on the main floor landing where you can see down the blue light well.","paras":["This blue shaft is the light well. The tiles are deeper blue at the top and paler lower down, so the light spreads evenly to every floor. Gaudí wanted the bottom to feel like the bottom of the sea.","On this floor lived the Batlló family. In the entrance hall of this floor, look up for skylights shaped like tortoise shells, and find the mushroom-shaped fireplace, tucked into a little nook for courting couples.","Up in the white loft, the old laundry, sixty arches curve overhead like the ribs of an animal. And then you step out onto the dragon's back.","Today UNESCO lists Casa Batlló as World Heritage. Not bad for an old house that was nearly torn down."]},{"name":"Closing","minutes":1,"stand":"Back out on the avenue, take one last look up.","paras":["It started as a contest between neighbors, to show off. Gaudí took that bit of vanity and turned an ordinary old house into a dragon, a lake and a legend, right here on the avenue."]}] },
  { id: "barcelona-casa-mila", v: "1-0c95ca", city: /Barcelona/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(casa mila|casa milà|la pedrera|pedrera)\b/i, title: "Casa Milà (La Pedrera)", where: "Casa Milà (La Pedrera)", checked: "2026-11-23",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Passeig de Gràcia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Passeig_de_Gr%C3%A0cia","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Antoni Gaudí","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antoni_Gaud%C3%AD","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"La Pedrera, Barcelona","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Casa_Mil%C3%A0","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","title":"Works of Antoni Gaudí - UNESCO World Heritage Centre","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/320/","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Passeig de Gràcia","minutes":1,"stand":"Stand on the opposite side of Passeig de Gràcia, across from the wavy stone building.","paras":["You're on Passeig de Gràcia, one of the great avenues of Barcelona, and home to some of its most famous buildings. And look down at the pavement under your feet.","Those hexagonal tiles, with seaweed, shells and starfish, are a Gaudí design. He made them for Casa Batlló, used them in the building across the street instead, and later the city chose them to pave this whole avenue.","It wasn't always grand. This was once a country lane, called the Jesus Road, between Barcelona and the separate town of Gràcia. By 1827 it was 42 meters wide, and aristocrats came here to show off their horses and carriages. Those ornate benches and street lights were designed in 1906 by Pere Falqués."]},{"name":"The stone façade","minutes":1,"stand":"Stay across the street and take in the whole curving façade.","paras":["That's Casa Milà. Everybody calls it La Pedrera, the stone quarry, because of that rough, rolling stone. It was the last private house Gaudí designed, built between 1906 and 1912 for Pere Milà and his wife, Roser Segimon.","The nickname wasn't a compliment. People who disliked the building gave it, and the humorous papers of the day made fun of it. Look at the balconies. That twisted wrought iron was part of the controversy too.","Gaudí was thinking of a mountain. Which mountain? Nobody knows for sure, and people still disagree. But look up at the top level, faced in white tiles, like snow on a peak.","Here's the clever part. That stone front holds itself up. It's not a load-bearing wall at all, so the walls inside can be moved without touching the building's stability.","And look along the top edge. Gaudí, devoted to the Virgin Mary, carved words from the Rosary up there. He also planned statues of Mary with the archangels Michael and Gabriel. But after the Tragic Week, an outbreak of anti-church violence in the city, Milà dropped the statues. Gaudí nearly walked away from the job. A priest talked him into staying."]},{"name":"The courtyards","minutes":1,"stand":"Inside, stop in the courtyard and look straight up.","paras":["No dark lobby here. The whole building is wrapped around two open courtyards, which pour light down through every floor.","It is said that Gaudí wanted the neighbors to know each other, so the elevators stopped only on every other floor, and people from different floors would run into each other.","Cars were a thrilling new invention, so there was a garage in the basement. When one neighbor's Rolls-Royce couldn't get down the ramp, Gaudí took a pillar out for it.","Not every owner loved living in a Gaudí. It is said that Roser Segimon complained there wasn't one straight wall for her Steinway piano. Gaudí's answer: \"So play the violin.\""]},{"name":"The attic","minutes":1,"stand":"Up in the attic, stand in the long brick passage and look along the arches.","paras":["This was the laundry. Look at the arches, 270 of them, about 80 centimeters apart. It feels like standing inside the ribs of a huge animal.","How did they get that shape? The builders hung a chain against the wall, let it sag, and traced the curve. Then they built the arch along that line."]},{"name":"The roof","minutes":1,"stand":"Out on the roof, find a flat spot among the chimneys.","paras":["And here's the roof. Twenty-eight chimneys and six staircase towers. The poet Pere Gimferrer called it the garden of warriors, because the chimneys seem to stand guard.","The roof goes up and down in steps and slopes. Watch your feet, and hold on to children.","Look at the chimneys covered in tiles. Their shapes suggest medieval helmets. And the staircase towers are topped with Gaudí's four-armed cross.","Look for the chimney topped with glass. The story goes that Gaudí made it the day after the party for the building's opening, from the empty bottles. When it was restored, they used the bottoms of old champagne bottles.","UNESCO lists La Pedrera as World Heritage, and this roof and the attic have been carefully restored."]},{"name":"Closing","minutes":1,"stand":"Before you head down, look out over the city from the roof terrace, keeping back from the edge.","paras":["People laughed at this building. They called it a quarry. And still, as of 2026, one person, the writer Ana Viladomiu, calls it home. Imagine having these warriors as your neighbors."]}] },
  { id: "barcelona-casa-vicens", v: "1-db0471", city: /Barcelona/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(casa vicens|vicens)\b/i, title: "Casa Vicens", where: "Casa Vicens", checked: "2026-11-23",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Casa Vicens","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Casa_Vicens","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","title":"Works of Antoni Gaudí - UNESCO World Heritage Centre","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/320/","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Antoni Gaudí","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antoni_Gaud%C3%AD","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"From the street","minutes":1,"stand":"On Carrer de les Carolines, across from the house, where you can see the whole street front.","paras":["This is Casa Vicens, and it's where Gaudí really begins. It's considered his first major project, built between 1883 and 1885. Back then this wasn't even Barcelona. Gràcia was its own town, with its own council, and it only joined the city in 1897.","It caused a great sensation in its day. Here, for the first time, Gaudí tried out ideas that would become regular features of Catalan Modernisme. Since 2005 it's been part of Gaudí's UNESCO World Heritage listing.","Look at the walls. Bands of stone alternate with tiles, some with flowers, some in a green and white check. Those yellow flowers have a story. Gaudí said that when he went to measure the site, it was totally covered with yellow flowers, so he used them as the theme for the tiles. He also found a palmetto palm, whose leaves fill the gate.","And there's the gate, at the street entrance: iron palm leaves and carnations. Gaudí drew it, the sculptor Llorenç Matamala made the mold, and Joan Oñós cast it in iron."]},{"name":"Two architects, one house","minutes":1,"stand":"Stay on the street side and look at the right half of the front.","paras":["Here's a little puzzle. The right half of this front, the part that sticks out slightly into the street, isn't Gaudí's. It was added between 1925 and 1927 by Joan Baptista Serra. Compare the tiles. Gaudí alternated them to give movement. Serra laid them out evenly.","Why didn't Gaudí do it himself? By then he was devoting himself fully to the Sagrada Família. So he turned it down, and recommended Serra, one of his protégés.","Who was it built for? Manuel Vicens, a stock and currency broker, who in 1878 asked for a summer house here. We know surprisingly little about him. Serra later passed on a story about the original building work: Gaudí sat under a parasol, watching, and now and then had poor work knocked down."]},{"name":"The garden side","minutes":2,"stand":"In the garden, facing the garden front of the house and its covered porch.","paras":["In Gaudí's design, this was the main front, looking out over the garden. The garden was much bigger once. There was even a waterfall as tall as the house. Bit by bit the land was sold off, and the waterfall was torn down in 1946.","Look at the covered porch. It opens to the garden through wooden latticework that recalls a kind of Japanese shutter, and Gaudí was able to learn about that system at an exhibition of Japanese architecture in Barcelona in 1881. For decades the porch was closed in with glass; the lattice shutters you see came back in the recent restoration. This whole house leans east: Gaudí was drawing on India, Persia and Japan, and on the Islamic art of Spain.","In the porch is a fountain. The water fell in a thin sheet that showed the colors of the rainbow when the light came through. It had been taken out, and the restorers brought it back.","Look up at the band of writing around the porch. It carries lines from Catalan folk songs. Right in front of you, on the southwest wall: from the hearth, the fire, long live the fire of love. On the southeast side, \"Sol, solet, vinam a veure\": sun, little sun, come and see me. And on the northwest side: oh, the shade of summer. Perfect words for a summer house."]},{"name":"Inside","minutes":1,"stand":"In the dining room on the main floor, next to the covered porch.","paras":["Look at the paintings on the doorposts between this room and the porch, by Francesc Torrescassana. Count the birds: twenty-four in all, every one flying except the flamingos at the bottom.","Next door is the smoking room. Look up. The ceiling hangs down like stalactites, in the Islamic style, painted plaster with palm leaves and bunches of dates.","If you head upstairs, take the stairs slowly, and mind the other visitors in the small rooms.","Upstairs, in the living room, look up again. The dome is painted to fool your eye: you seem to look straight up into a cupola, with white doves flying off into the sky."]},{"name":"A home again","minutes":1,"stand":"Back in the garden, with the house in front of you.","paras":["In 2014 the family sold it to the Andorran bank MoraBanc, and it opened as a house museum on November 16, 2017.","In 1881 Gaudí wrote, \"The house is the small family nation.\" And look how he built this one: with the flowers and the palm he found growing right here. Sometimes the best start is simply to notice what's already at your feet."]}] },
  { id: "barcelona-cathedral", v: "1-7f20a2", city: /Barcelona/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(barcelona cathedral|catedral de barcelona|barri gotic|barri gòtic|gothic quarter|placa del rei|plaça del rei)\b/i, title: "Barcelona Cathedral", where: "Barcelona Cathedral", checked: "2026-11-23",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Barcelona Cathedral","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barcelona_Cathedral","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Gothic Quarter, Barcelona","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gothic_Quarter%2C_Barcelona","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Eulalia of Barcelona","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eulalia_of_Barcelona","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Barcelona","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Barcelona","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"The square","minutes":1,"stand":"Stand in the open square in front of the cathedral, far enough back to see the whole front and its spire.","paras":["This is Barcelona Cathedral, the Cathedral of the Holy Cross and Saint Eulalia, seat of the city's archbishop. It's Gothic, and most of it was built in the fourteenth century.","Here's a twist. The front you're looking at is far younger than the church. This neo-Gothic façade went up between 1882 and 1913, over a plain wall. Look up along the roofline for the gargoyles, animals and mythical creatures.","Christians have worshipped on this spot for a very long time. The Gothic cathedral was begun on May 1, 1298 on top of older churches. Before it stood a Romanesque cathedral, consecrated in 1058. And before that, an early Christian church, badly damaged when al-Mansur attacked the city in 985.","A word of caution in this square. People dressed as mimes, faces painted white, sometimes approach tourists to grab their things. Don't stop. Just walk on."]},{"name":"Saint Eulalia","minutes":1,"stand":"Go inside and walk up the nave toward the high altar, until you can see down into the crypt beneath it.","paras":["Under the high altar, in the crypt, is the tomb of Saint Eulalia, one of the city's patron saints. Tradition says she was a Christian girl of thirteen, martyred here in Barcelona under the emperor Diocletian.","In one account, she walked into the city and confronted the Roman governor to his face for persecuting Christians, and he had her tortured to death. Tradition adds more: a miraculous snowfall in spring to cover her. And a barrel, lined with knives in one telling and broken glass in another, that she was shut inside and rolled down the street still called Baixada de Santa Eulàlia, Saint Eulalia's descent.","Her body was hidden in 713, during the Moorish invasion, found again in 878, and laid in this crypt, in an alabaster tomb, in 1339.","Historians aren't all sure she's a separate person from another Eulalia, of Mérida, whose story is very similar."]},{"name":"The choir and the chapels","minutes":1,"stand":"Find the choir stalls, and then the side chapels.","paras":["See the coats of arms on the choir stalls? They belong to the knights of the Order of the Golden Fleece. The emperor Charles V held a meeting of the order right here in 1519.","Look for the side chapel of the Holy Christ of Lepanto. The figure on that crucifix leans to one side. A Catalan legend says it rode into the Battle of Lepanto in 1571 on the flagship of Juan of Austria, and bent aside to dodge a cannonball.","Among the tombs here are Count Ramon Berenguer I and his wife Almodis, the couple who started the Romanesque cathedral nearly a thousand years ago."]},{"name":"The cloister","minutes":1,"stand":"Go out into the cloister and find the fountain with the geese.","paras":["Listen. Those are geese. Thirteen white geese live in this cloister, and tradition says it's one for each year of Eulalia's life.","The cloister was finished in 1448, and it wraps around the Well of the Geese. At Corpus Christi, in spring, an egg is set dancing on the fountain's jet of water."]},{"name":"The Gothic Quarter","minutes":1,"stand":"Leave the cathedral and wander a few steps into the narrow lanes around it.","paras":["You're in the Gothic Quarter, the old heart of Barcelona: a maze of little streets that open suddenly into squares, with pieces of the old Roman wall still standing.","But don't take the name too literally. Much of what looks medieval here was restored, or simply built, in the late 19th and early 20th centuries, timed for the 1929 exhibition. The lacy stone bridge over Carrer del Bisbe, if you pass under it, looks ancient. It went up in 1928.","Elsewhere in this quarter is the Plaça del Rei. One of its old houses, Casa Padellàs, built around 1500, was moved here in 1931 from another street. Today it's the City History Museum, and underground you can walk among remains of the early Christian church, with a baptistery from the 300s."]},{"name":"Closing","minutes":1,"stand":"Before you move on, pause wherever you are.","paras":["Old Gothic stone, a leaning crucifix, a cloister full of geese. And at the center of it all, by tradition, a young girl remembered for her courage, whose years the geese still count."]}] },
  { id: "barcelona-girona", v: "1-0e0a51", city: /Barcelona/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(girona|gerona)\b/i, title: "Day trip: Girona", where: "Day trip: Girona", checked: "2026-11-23",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Girona","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Girona","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Girona","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Girona","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Girona Cathedral","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Girona_Cathedral","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"The river","minutes":1,"stand":"On the Eiffel footbridge, the Pont de les Peixateries Velles, looking along the river at the colored houses.","paras":["Welcome to Girona, about 99 kilometers northeast of Barcelona. You're standing on a footbridge over the river Onyar, a metal lattice bridge designed by Gustave Eiffel. On the east bank, the old town climbs a steep hill.","Those houses along the river look timeless, but the colors are young. Most of the houses are from the 1800s. Local artists dreamed up the palette of 29 colors in the last years of the Franco dictatorship, and it was finally painted in 1982.","One of those artists, Enric Ansesa, called the colors a great signal of change. He remembered the city of his youth as a Girona in black and white."]},{"name":"A city that held out","minutes":1,"stand":"At the end of the bridge on the hill side, toward the cathedral, before you head uphill.","paras":["A quick word for your feet. The old town has slopes, lumpy cobblestones and steep stairs, so wear good shoes and take your time.","This is an old city. It's the ancient Gerunda, where the Romans built a citadel. The Moors took it in 715, and Charlemagne won it back in 785.","Girona has lived through twenty-five sieges. One came in 1809. 35,000 of Napoleon's troops surrounded the city from May to December, and Girona held out until disease and famine forced it to surrender on December 12."]},{"name":"The Jewish quarter","minutes":1,"stand":"On Carrer de la Força, the narrow street that leads toward the cathedral.","paras":["This street, Carrer de la Força, was the heart of the Call, the Jewish quarter. The first record of Jews in Girona dates to about 885. By the 1100s the community was flourishing, with one of the most important schools of Kabbalah in Europe. In the next century its rabbi, Nahmanides, who lived from 1194 to 1270, was appointed Great Rabbi of Catalonia.","Then came 1492. The remaining Jews were forced to choose between conversion and exile. After they left, the quarter was sealed off, and new houses were built right over the old ones.","It stayed buried for centuries. After Franco died in 1975, interest in the past revived, and excavations uncovered old buildings of the quarter, including one that is said to be the home of Nahmanides. Digs in the early 2000s turned up 1,200 old documents. Along this street, the Centre Bonastruc ça Porta stands on the site of a 15th-century synagogue, and today it houses the Museum of Jewish History.","As you walk, look at the doorframes. On some buildings in the Call, for example an old doorway on Carrer de Sant Llorenç, there's a small rectangular groove where a mezuzah once hung."]},{"name":"The cathedral","minutes":1,"stand":"At the foot of the cathedral stairs, looking up at the front.","paras":["And here's the cathedral, up a long, broad flight of stairs. Take them slowly. If you'd rather skip them, you can come at it from the side streets.","Game of Thrones fans may know these steps: outdoor scenes for season 6 were filmed at the cathedral and on this stairway.","Inside is something you won't see anywhere else. The Gothic nave is 22.98 meters wide. That's the widest Gothic nave in the world, and only St. Peter's in Rome has a wider church nave of any kind.","This site has seen it all. An early Christian church here became a mosque in 717, and was consecrated as a church again in 908. The older bell tower, named after Charlemagne, was begun in the early 1000s. In the cathedral museum, look for the Tapestry of Creation, from the 11th or early 12th century.","Nearby is the church of Sant Feliu, Saint Felix, said to have been martyred in Girona in 304 AD. By tradition, Saint Narcissus was one of the city's early bishops, and his tomb is inside."]},{"name":"The walls","minutes":1,"stand":"Up on the city walls, at a spot where you can look out over the rooftops.","paras":["When you're ready, follow the signs for the Passeig de la Muralla, the walking route along the walls.","You can walk these walls and climb their towers. The Romans built the first wall in the 1st century BC. It was rebuilt in the 1300s, and much of this eastern stretch was rebuilt again between 1983 and 2003.","Mind the edges and the steps up here, especially on the towers.","For a good view over the town and gardens, find Torre Gironella, a watchtower at the highest point of the walls.","Look down at those rooftops. Somewhere under them, a whole neighborhood was sealed away for centuries and then patiently uncovered. Cities forget. But people can choose to remember."]}] },
  { id: "barcelona-la-rambla", v: "1-1e6bcb", city: /Barcelona/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(la rambla|las ramblas|les rambles|placa de catalunya|plaça de catalunya|plaza de cataluna|plaza de cataluña|boqueria|placa reial|plaça reial)\b/i, title: "La Rambla and Plaça de Catalunya", where: "La Rambla and Plaça de Catalunya", checked: "2026-11-23",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Plaça de Catalunya","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pla%C3%A7a_de_Catalunya","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"La Rambla, Barcelona","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_Rambla%2C_Barcelona","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Liceu","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gran_Teatre_del_Liceu","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Royal Square (Barcelona)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pla%C3%A7a_Reial","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Antoni Gaudí","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antoni_Gaud%C3%AD","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Barcelona","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Barcelona","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Plaça de Catalunya","minutes":1,"stand":"Stand near the fountains in Plaça de Catalunya, with the top of La Rambla in view.","paras":["This big square is Plaça de Catalunya, the place most people call the center of Barcelona, where the old city meets the newer Eixample. Look for the compass rose in the pavement, and, well, the pigeons.","It's younger than it looks. The square was first laid out in 1902 and reshaped for the 1929 exhibition. And it's still the city's stage: in May 2011 it was the main site of Barcelona's anti-government protests.","One honest warning before we walk. Pickpockets are the biggest problem visitors face here. Keep your bag in front of you, and if you see a group betting on a cardboard table, walk on. It's a known trick to rob onlookers."]},{"name":"The top of La Rambla","minutes":1,"stand":"Walk to the start of La Rambla and stop by the Font de Canaletes fountain.","paras":["Here begins La Rambla, a tree-lined walk of 1.2 kilometers down to the old port. The Gothic Quarter lies on the east side, El Raval on the west.","This fountain is the Font de Canaletes, a favorite place to meet. In fact La Rambla is really five streets in a row, each with its own name. That's why locals say Les Rambles. This first stretch is the Rambla de Canaletes.","The street is being rebuilt in stages, so watch for fences and work zones as you walk.","If the old paving is still here, look down: it ripples, like water. That's a nod to what this was: a stream bed. The word rambla comes from the Arabic for sand, and means a dry riverbed. In 1440 the stream was sent outside the new city walls, and slowly it became a street.","The first trees were planted along here in 1703. The plane trees shading you now have been the main tree since 1859."]},{"name":"Flowers and the Boqueria","minutes":1,"stand":"Walk down past the flower stalls and stop at the entrance to the Boqueria market.","paras":["This stretch is the Rambla de les Flors, named for its flower market. And opening off it here is La Boqueria, Barcelona's famous food market, built where a Carmelite monastery once stood.","Somewhere under your feet on this walk, at a spot called the Pla de l'Os, is a pavement mosaic by the painter Joan Miró, laid in 1971. Watch for it as you go."]},{"name":"The Liceu","minutes":1,"stand":"Continue down to the opera house, the Liceu, and look at its front from the middle of the promenade.","paras":["This is the Liceu, Barcelona's opera house, opened here in 1847. For well over a century it was the biggest opera house in Europe by number of seats, and it has burned and been rebuilt twice.","It has a darker memory too. On November 7, 1893, on the opening night of the season, an anarchist threw two bombs into the stalls. About twenty people died."]},{"name":"Plaça Reial","minutes":1,"stand":"Turn off through the short passage on the Gothic Quarter side into Plaça Reial.","paras":["Step into Plaça Reial, the Royal Square, with its tall palm trees and arcades. It went up in the 1800s on the site of a Capuchin convent, pulled down in 1835.","Gaudí designed lampposts for this square, one of his very first projects, in 1878. They were installed in 1879. Look for the most elaborate lampposts here."]},{"name":"Down to the sea","minutes":1,"stand":"Head back to La Rambla and walk down until you can see the Columbus column at the end.","paras":["At the bottom stands the Columbus Monument, and beyond it the old port. If you came off a cruise ship, your shuttle may well have dropped you down at that end.","Not all of this street's money was clean. In the late 1800s, some Catalans who had grown rich from the slave trade, or from slave plantations in the Americas, came home and built grand houses on streets like this. One historian says La Rambla was built on the backs of slaves. That's disputed: The Guardian newspaper called it an exaggeration.","And a moment of remembrance. On August 17, 2017, a van was deliberately driven down this promenade in a terrorist attack. Many people were killed, and many more hurt."]},{"name":"Closing","minutes":1,"stand":"Turn around and look back up the long avenue of trees.","paras":["A stream bed, then a street of monasteries, mansions, flower sellers and opera. The poet García Lorca called it the only street in the world he wished would never end. Look back up it now, full of people, and you can see what he meant."]}] },
  { id: "barcelona-mnac", v: "1-94197f", city: /Barcelona/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(mnac|museu nacional d'art de catalunya|national art museum of catalonia|palau nacional|font magica|font màgica|magic fountain)\b/i, title: "National Art Museum of Catalonia (Montjuïc)", where: "National Art Museum of Catalonia (Montjuïc)", checked: "2026-11-23",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Montjuïc","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Montju%C3%AFc","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"1929 Barcelona International Exposition","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1929_Barcelona_International_Exposition","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Museu Nacional d'Art de Catalunya","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Museu_Nacional_d'Art_de_Catalunya","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Palau Nacional","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palau_Nacional_(Barcelona)","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"The Magic Fountain","minutes":1,"stand":"At the foot of Montjuïc, by the Magic Fountain, looking up the long staircase toward the palace with the great dome.","paras":["Look up the hill. That grand building with the dome is the Palau Nacional, home of the National Art Museum of Catalonia. Its great dome was inspired by St. Peter's in Rome, with a smaller dome on each side. The palace, this fountain and the staircase between them all survive from the 1929 International Exposition.","The fountain in front of you is the Magic Fountain. It was designed by Carles Buïgas and built in 1929, and it amazed the public with its light and water.","A word before the climb. It's a long way up, with plenty of steps. Take your time, carry water on a hot day, and stop to look back at the view."]},{"name":"The palace","minutes":1,"stand":"At the top of the stairs, in front of the palace entrance.","paras":["The palace went up between 1926 and 1929 as the main building of the Exposition, the centerpiece of the whole fair.","Here's the surprise. All this grandeur was meant to be temporary. It was built fast, with modest materials, and by 1934 it already needed restoring.","Politics shaped this building too. Puig i Cadafalch was first in charge of the project, but when Primo de Rivera seized power in 1923 he was removed. A competition was then won by the architects Eugenio Cendoya, Enric Catà and Pere Domènech i Roura. In 1929 the palace held a huge show called Art in Spain, with more than 5,000 works from across the country."]},{"name":"The Oval Hall","minutes":1,"stand":"Inside, in the great Oval Hall beyond the lobby.","paras":["This is the Oval Hall, 2,300 square meters of it. King Alfonso XIII and Queen Victoria Eugenie opened the Exposition right here. And it was reopened for the 1992 Olympic Games.","Look for the organ. It's one of the biggest in Europe, built in 1929 by the Walcker company of Ludwigsburg, Germany, with more than two thousand pipes. In 1955 another two thousand five hundred were added. Today it's waiting for a major restoration.","Now look between the arches for the small coats of arms. There are fifty-six of them. Fifty stand for the Spanish provinces of 1929. The last six, over by the organ, show musical instruments.","One more detail for when you're back outside: at the four corners of this hall stand towers modeled on the cathedral of Santiago de Compostela."]},{"name":"The Romanesque rooms","minutes":1,"stand":"In the Romanesque section, in front of the painted apse from Sant Climent de Taüll.","paras":["This is the heart of the museum. Many of these paintings, from the 11th to the 13th centuries, once covered the walls of country churches in the Pyrenees. No other museum in the world has a Romanesque collection like it.","In front of you is the apse from Sant Climent de Taüll, with its 12th-century Pantocrator, Christ in Majesty. Right beside it are works from Santa Maria de Taüll, a church that was painted from top to bottom.","So how did church walls end up in a museum? In 1919 an American art dealer bought frescoes from a monastery at Mur. He brought in Italian experts who could peel paintings off a wall, a technique called strappo, and those frescoes now hang in the Museum of Fine Arts in Boston.","Spain had no law against it, and Catalonia's museum board was alarmed. So they hired the same Italian experts, and from 1919 to 1923 they took many of the Pyrenean frescoes down and brought them to Barcelona, to keep them safe."]},{"name":"Gothic to modern","minutes":1,"stand":"Wherever you choose to go next in the collection.","paras":["If you go on to the Gothic rooms, the first one is presided over by the mural paintings of the Conquest of Majorca.","The modern collection is Barcelona's own story. There are paintings by Ramon Casas and Santiago Rusiñol, decorative pieces Gaudí designed for Casa Batlló, and since 2007, Picasso.","The museum's own road was long. An art museum has been in this palace since 1934, but the plan to bring all of Catalonia's art together here was cut short by the Civil War, and many works were sent away for safety, to Olot, Darnius and even Paris. The full national museum you see today was officially inaugurated on December 16, 2004."]},{"name":"Closing","minutes":1,"stand":"Back outside on the terrace in front of the palace, looking out over the city.","paras":["Think of those painters in the Pyrenees, centuries ago, working on the walls of a little mountain church. People had to lift their work off the walls to save it. It survived because someone cared enough to carry it down the mountain."]}] },
  { id: "barcelona-palau-guell", v: "1-ba5acf", city: /Barcelona/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(palau guell|palau güell|guell palace|güell palace)\b/i, title: "Palau Güell", where: "Palau Güell", checked: "2026-11-23",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Palau Güell","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palau_G%C3%BCell","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","title":"Works of Antoni Gaudí - UNESCO World Heritage Centre","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/320/","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Antoni Gaudí","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antoni_Gaud%C3%AD","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Eusebi Güell","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eusebi_G%C3%BCell","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"The gates","minutes":1,"stand":"On Carrer Nou de la Rambla, across the narrow street from the two iron gates.","paras":["This is Palau Güell, the Güell Palace. Antoni Gaudí designed it for the industrial tycoon Eusebi Güell, and it went up between 1886 and 1888, here on Carrer Nou de la Rambla in the Raval. Since 1984 it's been part of Gaudí's UNESCO World Heritage listing.","Look at the two gates. Guests rolled in here by horse and carriage, under the parabolic arch of each gate. Look closely at the ironwork: it's meant to look like seaweed, and in places like a horsewhip.","Now look between the gates, at the emblem with the Catalan coat of arms. Most accounts call the creature on top a phoenix, a symbol of Catalonia's rebirth, but one calls it a winged dragon, so the accounts differ a little. The ironwork is credited to Joan Oñós. Look for yourself and decide.","On the street front, the gallery uses arches and columns in a style Gaudí used only on this building."]},{"name":"Patron and architect","minutes":1,"stand":"Stay by the gates for a moment, out of the way of other visitors.","paras":["Who was Eusebi Güell? A businessman who did very well out of Catalonia's industrial revolution. He met the young Gaudí after seeing his work at the Paris World Fair of 1878. They became lifelong friends, both devout Catholics, and Güell became his great patron.","There's a harder side to this wealth, and it's worth saying plainly. Güell's father made a fortune in Cuba, by some accounts as a slave trader, and his father-in-law, the Marquis of Comillas, was a notorious slave trader too."]},{"name":"Coming in","minutes":1,"stand":"Just inside the entrance.","paras":["The house was built around arrival. Horses went down a ramp to the stable in the basement, where the servants lived. Guests went up the stairs to the receiving room.","If you go down to the basement, take the ramp slowly and watch your footing.","Here's a lovely detail upstairs. Hidden in the ornate walls of the receiving room are small windows, high up. The owners could take a sneak peek at their guests from the floor above, and fix their clothes if needed before coming down."]},{"name":"The great hall","minutes":1,"stand":"In the tall central hall, under the dome.","paras":["This hall is the heart of the house. It rises three stories, with the main rooms around it. Look up into the dome. It's a double dome, curved inside and cone-shaped outside, an idea that goes back to Byzantine buildings.","The palace's main party room has small holes near the top of its tall ceiling. At night, lanterns were hung outside them, so the guests below saw what looked like a starlit sky.","As you walk through the rooms, look up at the ceilings. Gaudí decorated the inside in a rich Mudejar style, and the wood and iron ceilings are the stars of the show."]},{"name":"The roof","minutes":1,"stand":"Up on the roof terrace, near the tall central spire.","paras":["Take care up here: watch the steps and the uneven surface, and keep children close.","This is the playful side. The chimneys are covered in brightly colored tile. And the tall spire in the middle is the outside of the dome you just stood under, also tiled and topped with an iron weather vane.","The palace hasn't had an easy life. In 2004 it closed to visitors, because some of the original stone was weak and had cracked. The restoration was finished in April 2011. Film fans may know this house too: it's where Jack Nicholson and Maria Schneider first meet in Antonioni's film The Passenger.","One last story. It is said that Gaudí once told Güell, \"Sometimes I think we are the only people who like this architecture.\" And Güell answered, \"I don't like your architecture, I respect it.\" Maybe that's the best a friend can give: not always agreement, but respect."]}] },
  { id: "barcelona-palau-musica", v: "1-16e0d8", city: /Barcelona/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(palau de la musica|palau de la música|palau de la musica catalana|palau de la música catalana|palace of catalan music)\b/i, title: "Palau de la Música Catalana", where: "Palau de la Música Catalana", checked: "2026-11-23",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Palau de la Música Catalana","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palau_de_la_M%C3%BAsica_Catalana","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Orfeó Català","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orfe%C3%B3_Catal%C3%A0","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","title":"Palau de la Música Catalana and Hospital de Sant Pau, Barcelona - UNESCO World Heritage Centre","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/804/","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"The corner","minutes":1,"stand":"Stand at the street corner where the big carved stone group juts out from the building, keeping clear of passing cars.","paras":["Squeezed into these narrow old-town streets is the Palau de la Música Catalana, the Palace of Catalan Music. UNESCO calls it a steel-framed building full of light and space. It was designed by Lluís Domènech i Montaner and opened on February 9, 1908.","It was built for a choir. The Orfeó Català was founded in 1891 by Lluís Millet and Amadeu Vives, and it was a leading force in the Renaixença, the rebirth of Catalan culture. The choir paid for most of this building itself, with help from the city's wealthy industrialists.","That great stone group on the corner, by the sculptor Miguel Blay, stands for Catalan music."]},{"name":"The façade","minutes":1,"stand":"Walk along to the main façade and look up at the balcony with the tiled columns.","paras":["Look up at the balcony columns. Each one is covered in a different pattern of colored tile, mostly flowers. And above them, the composers: Palestrina, Bach and Beethoven, with Wagner around the side.","Right at the top there's a big mosaic of the choir's members. Honestly, you can't see it clearly from down here. The street's too narrow.","The patrons wanted it to feel Catalan, through and through. So the architect handed the decoration to local craftsmen and gave them a free hand.","It looks like pure ornament. But as Tim Benton put it, to unaccustomed eyes it seems a riot of decoration without logic, and yet the structure, in brick and iron, is clearly expressed. Underneath it all is a steel frame. That's what let the walls become glass."]},{"name":"The staircase","minutes":1,"stand":"Inside, stop at the foot of the marble staircase.","paras":["Look up at the ceiling: ceramic stars. And look at the staircase rail. It's marble, but held up on little posts of transparent yellow glass.","Take the stairs slowly. Marble can be slippery, and it's easy to look up instead of down in here."]},{"name":"The concert hall","minutes":1,"stand":"In the hall, find a seat or a quiet spot along the side, and look up at the ceiling first.","paras":["Now look up. That's a stained-glass skylight by Antoni Rigalt: a golden sun in the middle, a blue sky around it, and a ring of women singing. This hall seats about 2,200, and in the daytime it's lit entirely by natural light, the only auditorium in Europe like that.","Now the stage. Look to its left: a bust of the choir director Anselm Clavé under a stone tree, with girls singing The Flowers of May, a Catalan song. That's folk music. On the right, the Valkyries ride out of Wagner's opera, above a bust of Beethoven. That's classical music. And they meet at the top of the arch.","Behind the stage, in a half circle, are the muses, 18 young women. Their upper bodies are sculpted, coming right out of the wall, and their skirts are mosaic. Each one plays a different instrument.","And up in the top balcony, winged horses, for Pegasus, the symbol of imagination taking flight.","Was it all good for the music? The writer Robert Hughes thought not. He said the glass walls carry sound like drum skins, and that the acoustics had always been awful. Since then, modern equipment has been carefully fitted, soundproofing included."]},{"name":"Closing","minutes":1,"stand":"Stay in your seat a moment longer.","paras":["UNESCO lists this hall as World Heritage, and it's still doing what it was built for. More than half a million people a year come to hear music here. It started with a choir that wanted a home for its songs. If you can, come back one evening and hear a choir sing here."]}] },
  { id: "barcelona-park-guell", v: "1-f319db", city: /Barcelona/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(park guell|park güell|parc guell|parc güell|parque guell)\b/i, title: "Park Güell", where: "Park Güell", checked: "2026-11-23",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Park Güell","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Park_G%C3%BCell","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","title":"Works of Antoni Gaudí - UNESCO World Heritage Centre","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/320/","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Eusebi Güell","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eusebi_G%C3%BCell","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Antoni Gaudí","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antoni_Gaud%C3%AD","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"The gate","minutes":1,"stand":"Stand just outside the main entrance, where you can see both gatehouses.","paras":["Welcome to Park Güell. Look at these two gatehouses, straight out of a fairy tale. Antoni Gaudí designed this hillside for his patron, Eusebi Güell, and built it between 1900 and 1914.","Here's the surprise. It wasn't meant to be a public park. Güell wanted a luxury housing estate, inspired by the English garden cities, which is why it's called Park, in English. There were sixty plots. Only two houses were ever built.","Back then this was a rocky hill people called Muntanya Pelada, the Bare Mountain. Notice the wall that runs around the site, made of stone and glazed ceramic.","One of the gatehouses now holds a small exhibit of the Barcelona City History Museum."]},{"name":"The dragon stairway","minutes":1,"stand":"Go through the gate and stop at the foot of the stairway with the fountains.","paras":["There's the famous one. The dragon fountain on these steps has become the symbol of the whole park.","Why a dragon? For Catalans, dragons were tied to their patron saint, George. Gaudí filled the park with Catalan symbols like this, including a mosaic of the Catalan flag.","Expect a crowd around the dragon. Be patient, and keep an eye on your bag."]},{"name":"The market hall and the terrace","minutes":1,"stand":"Climb to the top of the stairs, step in among the big columns, then go up to the open terrace above.","paras":["This forest of columns is the Hypostyle Hall. It was meant to be the market for the people who would live here. They're big Doric columns, the old classical kind.","Up on the big terrace above, laid out like a Greek theater, runs the long bench, curving like a sea serpent and covered in broken tiles. That mosaic is called trencadís. The bench was the work of Josep Maria Jujol.","Sit down for a moment. The curves of the bench make little nooks. It was designed to get people talking."]},{"name":"The stone viaducts","minutes":1,"stand":"Follow the paths uphill until you're walking along one of the raised stone walkways with columns underneath.","paras":["These raised walkways were built for carriages. Gaudí made them from the local stone, and the columns underneath are made to look like tree trunks.","The hillside here is rugged and steep. That's exactly why he built the viaducts. Take the slopes at your own pace, and on a hot day, carry water and find some shade.","Keep your eyes and ears open, too. The park is home to parrots, not native here, and to hummingbird hawk moths."]},{"name":"Gaudí's house","minutes":1,"stand":"Follow the signs to the Gaudí House Museum.","paras":["This was Gaudí's home for many years, from 1906. It was a show house nobody bought, so Güell persuaded Gaudí to buy it with his own savings. He moved in with his father and his niece.","The two men were lifelong friends, and both devout Catholics. Gaudí is said to have told him once, \"Sometimes I think we are the only people who like this architecture.\" And Güell answered, \"I don't like your architecture. I respect it.\"","It's fair to ask where the money came from, too. Güell's father grew rich in Cuba, and some accounts say he was a slave trader there. How far he was involved is not settled.","Güell himself died in 1918, in his house here in the park. Later the land passed from his family to the city, and in 1926 it opened as a public park."]},{"name":"The Three Crosses","minutes":1,"stand":"If your legs are up for it, climb to the highest point of the park, the small hill with the stone crosses.","paras":["This is the Hill of the Three Crosses, a little calvary. One cross points up to the sky. From here you can pick out the Sagrada Família, Montjuïc, and the Mediterranean.","UNESCO lists the park as World Heritage, and notes that it is still a public park and green space.","The housing estate failed. Hardly anyone wanted to build up here. It was too far from the city. But look around you at all these people, sitting, talking, looking out at the sea. Maybe it turned out better this way."]}] },
  { id: "barcelona-sagrada-familia", v: "1-2d399c", city: /Barcelona/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(sagrada familia|sagrada família|sagrada)\b/i, title: "Sagrada Família", where: "Sagrada Família", checked: "2026-11-23",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Sagrada Família","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sagrada_Fam%C3%ADlia","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","title":"Works of Antoni Gaudí - UNESCO World Heritage Centre","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/320/","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Antoni Gaudí","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antoni_Gaud%C3%AD","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Barcelona Cathedral","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barcelona_Cathedral","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Arrival","minutes":1,"stand":"Find a spot across the street where you can see the whole church, towers and all.","paras":["So here it is. The Sagrada Família, the great basilica designed by Antoni Gaudí. It's still being built, and it's already the tallest church in the world.","The tallest tower, in the middle, is the Tower of Jesus Christ, 172.5 meters high. Pope Leo XIV blessed it on June 10, 2026, a hundred years to the day after Gaudí died.","One small thing people get wrong. This is not Barcelona's cathedral. That title belongs to the Cathedral of the Holy Cross and Saint Eulalia in the Gothic Quarter.","The idea came from a bookseller, José María Bocabella. He wanted an expiatory temple, a church of atonement, paid for entirely by donations. Work began on March 19, 1882, the feast of Saint Joseph, with an ordinary Gothic revival design by another architect, Villar. In 1883 Gaudí took over, and he changed it radically.","People asked him why it was taking so long. He is said to have answered: \"My client is not in a hurry.\" When he died in 1926, less than a quarter of it was done."]},{"name":"The Nativity façade","minutes":1,"stand":"Walk around to the façade that faces the rising sun, crowded with carved figures, and stand back far enough to take it in.","paras":["This is the Nativity façade, about the birth of Jesus. Built between 1893 and 1936, it's the part that carries Gaudí's own hand most directly. UNESCO lists it, with the crypt, as World Heritage.","It faces the rising sun, a symbol for the birth of Christ. And there are three doorways, for Hope, Faith and Charity. Above the door of Jesus, in the doorway of Charity, the Tree of Life rises up, in the form of a cypress tree.","Now look down, to the bottom of the two big columns between the doorways. Each one rests on a turtle or a tortoise, one for the land and one for the sea, symbols of time that doesn't change. And at either side of the façade there's a chameleon, the symbol of change.","Why start here? Gaudí knew he would never finish this church. So he began with the warmest, most welcoming façade. He believed that if people had seen the hard, bare Passion side first, they would have turned away.","He even wanted every statue painted, so the people would look as alive as the plants and animals. Some of these figures were destroyed in 1936, in the Spanish Civil War. The Japanese sculptor Etsuro Sotoo later made them again."]},{"name":"Inside","minutes":2,"stand":"Once you're through the doors, step to one side, out of the flow of people, and look straight up.","paras":["Take a moment. The plan is a Latin cross, with five aisles. The vaults over the nave reach forty-five meters, and the central vault sixty.","Look at the columns around you. They branch as they rise, to carry the weight. The simplest ones start as a square, turn into an octagon, then a sixteen-sided shape, and end as a circle. Now look for a flat wall, or a flat ceiling. You'll struggle. There are no exact right angles anywhere in this church.","Even unfinished, this is a working church. Pope Benedict XVI consecrated it on November 7, 2010, with 6,500 people inside and some 50,000 more following outside.","In the apse, the rounded end of the church, there are gaps in the floor where you can look down into the crypt. That's where Gaudí is buried.","His death is a sad story. On June 7, 1926 he was walking, as he did every day, to church for prayer and confession, and he was hit by a tram. He was so poorly dressed by then that people are said to have taken him for a beggar, and he didn't get help right away. He died on June 10, at 73. His gravestone says his ashes await the resurrection of the dead."]},{"name":"The Passion façade","minutes":1,"stand":"Go around to the opposite side of the church and face the façade with the bare, bony stone figures.","paras":["What a change. This is the Passion façade, about the death of Jesus. Bare stone, cut with harsh straight lines like the bones of a skeleton. Gaudí meant it to strike fear into you.","It faces the setting sun, the symbol of Christ's death. Gaudí drew it in 1917, but work only began in 1954. From 1987 a team led by the sculptor Josep Maria Subirachs carved the scenes, and his figures are controversial.","Read it from the bottom, in an S shape. First Jesus' last night: the Last Supper, the kiss of Judas. In the middle, the road to Calvary, with Veronica's veil. Then his death, burial and resurrection at the top. And high up, on a bridge between two of the towers, a bronze figure shows the Ascension.","Here's a puzzle to find. There's a magic square carved on this façade. Every row and column adds up to 33."]},{"name":"Still being built","minutes":2,"stand":"Stay where you can see the cranes and the newest towers.","paras":["Gaudí planned eighteen spires: twelve for the apostles, four for the Evangelists, one for the Virgin Mary, and the tallest for Jesus Christ. Fourteen were finished as of 2026.","And that tallest tower stays lower than Montjuïc hill, because Gaudí believed his creation should not surpass God's.","The war stopped the work. In July 1936 anarchists set fire to the crypt and broke into Gaudí's workshop, partially destroying his plans. The builders carried on with what was saved, and with plans pieced back together from publications and photographs.","Not everyone wanted that. In 2008 some well-known Catalan architects argued it should stop, to respect what Gaudí really drew. Today computers shape the stone off-site, where it used to be carved by hand, and the work is paid for by donations and by visitors' tickets.","The main entrance isn't finished yet. The Glory façade, begun in 2002 on the south side, will be the front door. Its central doors carry the Our Father in Catalan, and the words \"Give us this day our daily bread\" in fifty languages.","If you've booked a tower, a word of warning. The way down is a very narrow spiral staircase, more than 300 steps, and there's a posted caution for anyone with a medical condition."]},{"name":"Closing","minutes":1,"stand":"Take one last look up at the towers before you go.","paras":["Gaudí knew that he would never see this finished. He built anyway, so that people he would never meet could carry it on. And they have."]}] },
  { id: "barcelona-sant-pau", v: "1-c4f3e6", city: /Barcelona/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(sant pau|santa creu i sant pau|recinte modernista)\b/i, title: "Hospital de Sant Pau", where: "Hospital de Sant Pau", checked: "2026-11-23",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Hospital de Sant Pau","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hospital_de_la_Santa_Creu_i_Sant_Pau","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","title":"Palau de la Música Catalana and Hospital de Sant Pau, Barcelona - UNESCO World Heritage Centre","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/804/","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Lluís Domènech i Montaner","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Llu%C3%ADs_Dom%C3%A8nech_i_Montaner","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Arrival","minutes":1,"stand":"Just inside the entrance, where you can see the first pavilions and the green space around them.","paras":["Welcome to Sant Pau. Its full name is the Hospital of the Holy Cross and Saint Paul. It was built between 1901 and 1930, and it's one of the great works of the Catalan architect Lluís Domènech i Montaner. Together with his concert hall, the Palau de la Música, it became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1997.","Now, here's the thing to keep in mind as you look around. This was not a palace. It was a working hospital, right up until June 2009.","UNESCO calls it the largest hospital complex in the Modernista style, which is Catalonia's own version of Art Nouveau."]},{"name":"An old hospital, a new home","minutes":1,"stand":"Stay where you are, with the pavilions in front of you.","paras":["The hospital itself is much older than these buildings. It was founded in 1401, when six small medieval hospitals merged. Its old home, near the center of the city, dates from the 1400s and now holds an art school and the National Library of Catalonia.","Over time the city grew, and the medieval buildings were worn out. So a new hospital was planned out here, and Domènech designed it.","And the name? The words Sant Pau, Saint Paul, were added in honor of a banker named Pau Gil, who paid for the new buildings."]},{"name":"A garden town for the sick","minutes":2,"stand":"Walk on among the pavilions and stop where you can see several of them at once.","paras":["Look at the layout. Instead of one huge block, Domènech built separate pavilions, set in a large green space and linked by long passages underground.","He'd done his homework. At the time, people were worried about health in big cities, and Domènech studied hospitals in Paris, London, Belgium and elsewhere in France. UNESCO says he found daring answers to what a modern hospital needed: fresh air, hygiene, and separate specialties.","This wasn't his first try, by the way. Domènech had already begun a hospital for the mentally ill in Reus, the Institut Pere Mata, and the new ideas he tried there were carried over and improved here. And by combining the traditional Catalan vault with steel, he could make large, open spaces inside.","Now look at the walls. The plain brickwork is typical of Domènech, and he warmed it with stone from Montjuïc. The decoration is bold, but it was all meant to serve the patients.","The tiles on these buildings have been looked after. When damaged ones had to be replaced, great care was taken to make exact copies of the originals.","The dream was even bigger than what you see. Domènech's original plan had 48 buildings. In the end, 27 were built. And the city noticed: in 1913 the hospital won the city council's annual architecture award."]},{"name":"A second life","minutes":1,"stand":"Find a quiet spot in the shade, facing the nearest pavilion.","paras":["A new hospital building went up just north of these pavilions in 2003, and almost all the departments moved across. By June 2009 Sant Pau was no longer a full working hospital, and that same year a careful restoration began.","Over 30 teams of experts and architects worked on it, with a budget of 100 million euros, and the doors reopened to visitors in 2014. Some of the buildings now give office space to organizations such as the World Health Organization. Fitting, isn't it?","In a way, this place has always been cared for. UNESCO notes it has been maintained continually since it was built. The Clock Tower, for example, was restored between 1985 and 1989. And if you're curious about the hospital's long story, its historical archive is open to visitors, with a reading room.","Before you go, picture a patient here a century ago. Sick, maybe frightened, and looking out at gardens and colored tile. Someone decided that people who were ill deserved beauty too. That's a good idea to carry home."]}] },
  { id: "barcelona-sitges", v: "1-05ae7d", city: /Barcelona/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(sitges)\b/i, title: "Day trip: Sitges", where: "Day trip: Sitges", checked: "2026-11-23",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Sitges","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sitges","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Sitges","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Sitges","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Santiago Rusiñol","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Santiago_Rusi%C3%B1ol","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"The seafront","minutes":1,"stand":"On the Passeig de la Ribera, the seafront promenade, facing the sea.","paras":["Welcome to Sitges, about 35 kilometers southwest of Barcelona, squeezed between the Garraf hills and the Mediterranean. It's famous for its beaches, its film festival, its Carnival, and for welcoming LGBTQ+ visitors. Somewhere along this promenade, look up for the old church standing above it.","On a sunny day the light off the sea is strong. Wear a hat, drink water, and find some shade now and then.","The name is humble. Sitges simply means silos, or granaries, in Catalan, and the first town probably grew up around the castle where they stood. The story goes that the Romans called it Blanca subur, the white town.","People have been here a very long time. Neanderthal bones and stone tools turned up in a cave near today's golf course, La Cova del Gegant, showing that Neanderthals lived here at least 50,000 years ago. And in 1958 this seaside town even made history far away: Colombia's rival Liberal and Conservative leaders met here and signed a peace treaty, the Declaration of Sitges.","There are 17 beaches in Sitges, so if this one's crowded, keep walking."]},{"name":"The Cuba connection","minutes":1,"stand":"Turn your back to the sea and look into the town.","paras":["For over a century, this little town looked across the Atlantic. Thousands of young people from Sitges went to eastern Cuba. Some made fortunes there. One of them, Facundo Bacardí, founded Bacardí rum.","Those who came home rich were called the Americanos. They liked to plant palm trees by their Caribbean-looking houses, and you can still see their legacy in the town's buildings.","Here's a fun one. Sitges claims Spain's first beach bar, El Kiosket, which opened here in 1913. The story goes that in 1949 a journalist renamed it the chiringuito, after a cloth coffee strainer he'd seen in Cuba called a chiringo. Now beach bars all over Spain are called chiringuitos.","Sitges made shoes, too. The first mechanized shoe factory in Spain opened here in 1874. It was tourism, booming in the 1960s, that finally ended the shoe-making era."]},{"name":"The painter's house","minutes":1,"stand":"Outside the Cau Ferrat Museum in the old town.","paras":["This is the Cau Ferrat, once the studio of Santiago Rusiñol, painter, writer and one of the leaders of Catalan modernisme. He set up here in 1893, and Sitges became a magnet for artists, writers and musicians, with festivals mixing theater, poetry, painting and music.","Inside, look for paintings by El Greco and Picasso, alongside works by Catalan modernists.","After the artists came the money. Wealthy Barcelona families built summer houses here, and the town became known abroad. The American collector Charles Deering kept his art in Sitges from 1910 to 1921, and built the Palau Maricel.","The party never really stopped. The film festival, devoted to fantasy and horror, has been held every October since 1968. And Carnival, celebrated for over a century, runs from Fat Thursday to the burial of the sardine on Ash Wednesday, with around forty floats and more than 2,000 people in the parades."]},{"name":"A glass of malvasia","minutes":1,"stand":"Back by the sea, wherever you'd like to sit for a moment.","paras":["Before you leave, try the local malvasia, a delicate dessert wine. The name comes from Monemvasía, a port in Greece's Peloponnese. Here in Sitges, the town's Hospital Sant Joan Baptista still makes it from its own vines, about 4,000 bottles a year, and the money goes to charity.","Young people left this town for Cuba, and came home with palm trees. A painter came and set up his studio. Sitges has always been a place people arrive at and make their own. Maybe you will too."]}] },
  { id: "barcelona-tibidabo", v: "1-6f5943", city: /Barcelona/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(tibidabo|sagrat cor|sagrado corazon)\b/i, title: "Tibidabo", where: "Tibidabo", checked: "2026-11-23",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Tibidabo","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tibidabo","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"King James Version (public domain), via bible-api.com","title":"","url":"https://bible-api.com/matthew%204%3A8-10?translation=kjv","when":"2026-11-23"},{"pub":"King James Version (public domain), via bible-api.com","title":"","url":"https://bible-api.com/luke%204%3A5-8?translation=kjv","when":"2026-11-23"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Temple Expiatori del Sagrat Cor","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_Expiatori_del_Sagrat_Cor","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Tibidabo Amusement Park","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tibidabo_Amusement_Park","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"The summit","minutes":1,"stand":"On the open summit, in front of the church, with the city spread out below you.","paras":["This is Tibidabo, at 512 meters the tallest hill in the Collserola range. Below you is Barcelona, out to the sea. Up here you've got the Sagrat Cor church, an old amusement park, and a short walk away, the Collserola telecom tower.","Hold on to your hat in the wind, and keep children close near the viewpoints.","If you came up on the funicular, you rode the line that was the first funicular in Spain."]},{"name":"A name from the Gospels","minutes":1,"stand":"Stay at the viewpoint, looking out over the city.","paras":["The name comes straight from the Bible, in Latin: tibi dabo, I will give to you. In Matthew's Gospel, chapter four, the devil takes Jesus up into an exceeding high mountain, and shows him all the kingdoms of the world, and the glory of them. And he says, in the King James words, \"All these things will I give thee, if thou wilt fall down and worship me.\"","Luke's Gospel, also in chapter four, tells the same moment: \"All this power will I give thee, and the glory of them.\"","So why name a Barcelona hill after the devil's offer? Nobody knows for sure. It may come from a folk belief that this was the mountain itself. More likely it's a wink: look at the view, and you'll see how tempting Barcelona can be."]},{"name":"The church","minutes":2,"stand":"Facing the front of the church, at the foot of its outdoor stairways.","paras":["This is the Church of the Sacred Heart of Jesus, by the architect Enric Sagnier, finished by his son. It took from 1902 to 1961 to build. On top stands a huge bronze statue of the Sacred Heart, by Josep Miret, from 1950.","Notice it's really two churches stacked up. The lower one, the crypt, looks like a Romanesque fortress in Montjuïc stone. Above it rises a neo-Gothic church, reached by two grand outdoor stairways. Over the crypt entrance, look for the Virgin of Mercy, Saint George and Saint James, patron saints of Barcelona, Catalonia and Spain.","The climb is the message. Going up from the crypt, through the church, to the statue at the top is meant to reflect the rise and the purification of the human condition. Take the stairs carefully if you go up.","How did a church get up here? In the late 1800s there were rumors of a Protestant church and a hotel-casino on the summit. A group of Catholic knights bought the land and gave it to Saint John Bosco in 1886. The first statue on top, by Frederic Marès, was made in 1935 and destroyed the next year as the Civil War began. And inside, the windows of the four towers carry the words tibi dabo."]},{"name":"The amusement park","minutes":1,"stand":"At the edge of the amusement park, where you can see the rides against the sky.","paras":["Next door is one of the oldest amusement parks in the world still running, and the longest running in Spain. The pharmacist Salvador Andreu proposed it in 1899, and the first rides were installed in 1905.","Look for the little plane hanging from its crane. That's the Avió, built in 1928, a copy of the plane from the first commercial flight from Madrid to Barcelona in 1927. It swings around in a circle, pushed by its own propeller. And the Talaia, opened in 1921, lifts riders up to 551 meters above sea level.","Here's a story worth telling. The story goes that in 1957 Walt Disney tried to buy the whole collection of the park's Museum of Automats, with blank checks. The management said no.","So here they are, side by side on one hilltop: a church and a funfair. Look out at the city again. Whatever the devil promised, the view is already a gift, and it's free."]}] },
  { id: "chania-agora-ch", v: "1-4f9ef1", city: /Chania|Hania|Souda/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(chania market|chania agora|municipal market of chania|central market of chania)\b/i, title: "Chania Municipal Market", where: "Chania Municipal Market", checked: "2026-11-22",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Chania","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chania","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Cretan State","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cretan_State","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Kydonia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kydonia","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Chania","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Chania","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Arrival","minutes":1,"stand":"Outside the market building, at the edge of the old town.","paras":["This is Chania's Municipal Market, the Agora. It's an indoor market from 1913, right on the edge of the old town, and it's popular with locals and visitors alike. It was renovated in 2026.","1913 is a telling date. From 1898 to 1913, Chania was the capital of the Cretan State, a semi-autonomous Crete, and many important buildings went up in those years. Then, on 1 December 1913, Crete was united with Greece. So this market belongs to the very end of that era.","The square around the market, the Market Square, is one of the largest central squares in town. And mosaics from Late Roman houses, signs of a prosperous city, were found near this square."]},{"name":"Inside","minutes":1,"stand":"If the market is open, step inside.","paras":["I'll be honest with you: my sources don't describe the stalls or the building in any detail, so let your eyes, and your nose, lead you. What I can tell you is what this region produces: olives and olive oil, citrus, wine, avocados, dairy, and honey. See how many you can find.","Ask before you photograph a stallholder, and keep an eye on your bag when it gets crowded."]},{"name":"Before you go","minutes":1,"stand":"Wherever you like, inside or out.","paras":["A market is where a town shows you what it eats and how it talks. Buy something small, take your time, and listen for a minute before you move on."]}] },
  { id: "chania-firkas", v: "1-9190f0", city: /Chania|Hania|Souda/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(firkas|firka fortress|fort firka|maritime museum of crete|nautical museum of crete)\b/i, title: "Firkas Fortress and the Maritime Museum", where: "Firkas Fortress and the Maritime Museum", checked: "2026-11-22",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Maritime Museum of Crete","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maritime_Museum_of_Crete","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Fortifications of Chania","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Firkas_Fortress","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Chania","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chania","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Cretan State","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cretan_State","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Eleftherios Venizelos","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eleftherios_Venizelos","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Battle of Crete","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Crete","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Kydonia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kydonia","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Arrival","minutes":1,"stand":"At the fortress, on the west side of the harbor, looking out over the water.","paras":["This is the Firkas Fortress, on the west side of Chania's harbor. It was built early in the seventeenth century to protect the harbor, close to the San Salvatore Bastion, the northwest corner of the old city walls.","The stone underfoot can be uneven, and there's little shade up on the walls. Take your time, and carry water."]},{"name":"Walls on walls","minutes":1,"stand":"Turn and look back toward the town.","paras":["Chania's walls come in layers. The inner walls first went up in antiquity, and the Byzantines rebuilt them. The outer walls are Venetian, from the 1500s. Parts of both still stand.","By around the 3rd century BC, ancient Cydonia had walls. The Byzantines strengthened them in the 500s and 600s, but in 828 Saracens destroyed the city. When the Byzantines came back, they built a new fortress on Kastelli hill in the 900s, to stop a second Arab invasion.","Venice took Crete in the 13th century. In 1336, as the town spilled outside its old walls, Venice decided to wall in the new neighborhoods, and the job was done within 20 years.","Then, in the early 1500s, the Ottoman Empire became the big threat. So in the fifteen-thirties, work began on new walls, designed by Michele Sanmicheli. They were finished in 1568. Together with a fortress at a place called Thodorou, they cost 87,000 ducats.","Picture the scale. The walls stood up to 20 meters high, about 66 feet, with a ditch in front 60 meters wide and 15 deep. In 1630 the city had 319 cannons and 30,695 cannonballs."]},{"name":"The siege of 1645","minutes":1,"stand":"Look out to sea, past the harbor entrance.","paras":["All that stone didn't save the city. In June 1645 the Ottoman fleet arrived. After a siege of 56 days, the city fell on 22 August, when the walls on the town's southwest side, away from the sea, were breached.","Here's how it began. The Ottoman navy arrived off Crete on 23 June. The troops landed 15 miles west of Chania, and the local militia fled before them.","Many Cretans fled to escape persecution. Many others were slaughtered, or converted to Islam.","The Ottomans repaired the breached walls and later modernized them, and Chania stayed under Ottoman rule until November 1898. Then, early in the twentieth century, parts of the south walls were torn down, because they were holding the growing city back."]},{"name":"The flag of 1913","minutes":2,"stand":"Stay up on the fortress, facing the harbor.","paras":["This fortress is remembered above all for one day. On 1 December 1913, at the ceremony of Crete's union with Greece, the Greek flag was raised here, with Eleftherios Venizelos and King Constantine the First looking on.","It was a long road. In 1898, Chania became the capital of the semi-autonomous Cretan State, with Prince George of Greece as High Commissioner. A young lawyer from Chania, Eleftherios Venizelos, pushed for union with Greece. In March 1905 he and his supporters gathered at Therisos, in the hills near Chania. In 1910 he was elected prime minister of Greece, and that, after the Balkan Wars, led to union.","And earlier in 1913, in February, the Great Powers lowered their own flags at the Souda fortress, their quiet way of accepting the union in practice.","As the story is told, early in 1897, during a revolt, a Greek flag was raised at the rebel camp at Akrotiri, and the warships of the Great Powers shelled the rebel positions. A shell knocked the flag down, and it went straight back up.","Akrotiri became part of the Venizelos myth. As the story was told, he turned toward the warships at Souda and said: You have cannon-balls, fire away! But our flag will not come down."]},{"name":"The Maritime Museum","minutes":1,"stand":"At the museum, by the entrance to the fortress.","paras":["Right at the entrance to the fortress is the Maritime Museum of Crete. Ship models, nautical instruments, paintings, old photographs and war relics, all laid out in order, from the Bronze Age to today.","On the museum's first exhibition floor, look for models of ancient ships, and a model of the walled town and port under Venice. It's the easiest way to picture the walls we've been talking about. There's a model of a shipyard with a rowing ship inside, and a whole room of sea life: shells, sponges, corals and fossils.","On the floor above, it's the modern Hellenic Navy: destroyers, a missile boat, a landing ship, even the full bridge of a destroyer, and two torpedo propulsion units. One section is given over to the German invasion of Crete.","That invasion began on the morning of 20 May 1941, when German paratroopers landed near Maleme Airfield and near the town of Chania. Most were met by New Zealanders defending the airfield and by Greek forces near Chania."]},{"name":"The Minoan ship","minutes":1,"stand":"If you have time, walk along the harbor to the Moro Shipyard at its eastern end, and see if it's open.","paras":["Here at the Moro Shipyard, at the harbor's eastern end, the museum keeps a 17-meter replica of a Minoan ship, built as an experiment between 2001 and 2004, and later sailed and rowed all the way to Piraeus.","It belongs here. In the late Minoan age, the town under old Chania, ancient Kydonia, was a large commercial and maritime center, shipping pottery, oil, perfume and wine all over the Aegean.","Walls, flags and ships. This harbor has seen people defend what they had, and fight for what they hoped for. Before you go, look out at the water, the same water those Minoan rowers knew."]}] },
  { id: "chania-harbor", v: "1-e7e8b2", city: /Chania|Hania|Souda/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(chania harbor|chania harbour|chania lighthouse|lighthouse of chania|old harbour of chania)\b/i, title: "Chania Venetian Harbor and Lighthouse", where: "Chania Venetian Harbor and Lighthouse", checked: "2026-11-22",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Chania","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chania","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Chania","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Chania","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Chania Lighthouse","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lighthouse_of_Chania","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Kydonia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kydonia","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Maritime Museum of Crete","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maritime_Museum_of_Crete","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Fortifications of Chania","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Firkas_Fortress","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Arrival","minutes":1,"stand":"On the waterfront, facing out across the water toward the lighthouse at the harbor mouth.","paras":["Here it is: the old harbor of Chania. The old town grew up right behind you, around this water, and the whole city spread out from here. And out at the harbor entrance, that stone tower is the lighthouse.","Mind the edge of the quay as you walk. There's not much shade by the water, so keep a hat and some water handy.","One travel guide puts it well. It calls the old town a maze of alleys and houses that stood for hundreds of years, were rebuilt, ruined, and rebuilt again, with details from different epochs. Keep that in mind as you look along this waterfront. Hardly anything here belongs to just one century.","The quays have their own names: Akti Tompazi, Akti Kountouriotou, and Akti Enoseos, which is the marina. All of them have historical buildings, and all of them have nightlife."]},{"name":"The lighthouse","minutes":1,"stand":"Keep facing the lighthouse at the harbor entrance.","paras":["The lighthouse you're looking at was rebuilt in the nineteenth century, on the base of an older Venetian one. That base is all that's left of the first lighthouse. It's a stone tower, rising 26 meters, about 85 feet, above the bay.","So who built the tower you see? Not the Venetians. Under Venice, this spot was a naval post, there to protect the city from oncoming Turks or pirates. In 1645 the Turks took the city, and the old lighthouse was left to fall into ruin. The rebuilding, in the nineteenth century, is credited to Egyptian troops who were helping the Ottoman Empire hold Crete.","It's had an eventful life since. In May 1915 it got a fixed white light, and became an official lighthouse in Greece's network. It was damaged in World War Two, and refurbished in 2006. Today the light at the top flashes red every 2.5 seconds. If you're here after dark, try counting along. And since 1962, the tower has been a listed archaeological site."]},{"name":"Who sailed from here","minutes":2,"stand":"Turn around and look back along the waterfront, toward the town.","paras":["This harbor is far older than Venice. The small hill right next to the seafront is Kastelli, the harbor's citadel. Digs there have turned up finds from the Neolithic all the way through the Minoan age.","In the late Minoan age, the town here, ancient Kydonia, was a large commercial and maritime center. It exported pottery, oil, perfume and wine across the Aegean. In 2025, UNESCO listed ancient Kydonia as a World Heritage Site, one of six Minoan palatial centers on that list.","The Odyssey even mentions a people called the Cydonians. Their city was important enough for that.","How did Venice get Crete at all? After the Fourth Crusade in 1204, the island went to Bonifacio, Marquess of Montferrat, and he sold it to the Venetians for 1000 silver marks. Historians estimate that about 10,000 Venetians moved to Crete in the first century, at a time when Venice itself had around 60,000 people. The cultures mixed closely, but the Cretans kept their Greek Orthodox faith.","Then came Venice. In 1252, Venetian settlers founded Canea, today's Chania, on the site of long-abandoned Kydonia. They strengthened its defenses and gave the town the shape it still has. Over on the west side of the harbor is the Firkas Fortress, built early in the seventeenth century to protect it.","After the Ottomans took the city, they built new mosques, among them the Küçük Hasan Pasha Mosque, also called the Yali Mosque, on the harbor. I'll be honest with you: beyond that, my sources say very little about it, so I won't guess at its story.","In Ottoman times, the pasha of Crete resided here, in Hanya, as they called the town."]},{"name":"Before you go","minutes":1,"stand":"Wherever you like along the water.","paras":["Minoan traders, Venetian sailors, Ottoman troops, Egyptian builders. They all looked out at this same water. Take a moment before you go. This harbor has been the town's front door for a very long time, and right now, it's yours.","In the 1970s, Crete became a major destination for travelers. You're part of that story now."]}] },
  { id: "chania-nikolaos", v: "1-3d31ee", city: /Chania|Hania|Souda/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(saint nicholas, splantzia|st\. nicholas, chania|hunkar mosque)\b/i, title: "Church of Saint Nicholas, Splantzia", where: "Church of Saint Nicholas, Splantzia", checked: "2026-11-22",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Church of St. Nicholas, Chania","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Nicholas_Church%2C_Chania","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Chania","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chania","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"The square","minutes":1,"stand":"In Splantzia Square, facing the church.","paras":["This is the Church of Saint Nicholas, on Splantzia Square. Look closely, and you'll see something odd: it has a bell tower, and a minaret. It's the only building in Greece with both.","Splantzia is one of the least changed corners of the old town, and these days its square is full of cafes."]},{"name":"A Venetian church","minutes":1,"stand":"Stay facing the church.","paras":["It began as a Roman Catholic church. Dominican friars built it around 1320. In form it's a basilica.","The original bell tower stood on the northeast side of the church. It didn't survive, and it had to be rebuilt.","On the west side was the church's enclosed courtyard. Under both the Venetians and the Turks, nobles were buried there."]},{"name":"The Sultan's mosque","minutes":1,"stand":"Move round to where you can see the minaret, on the south side of the church.","paras":["In 1645, under the Ottomans, the church became a mosque: the Hünkar Mosque, the Emperor's Mosque, named in honor of the Sultan. That's when this minaret was added, against the south side of the building, toward its west end. It's a round tower in the classical Ottoman style.","A mihrab and a minbar went in at the same time. It became the most important mosque in Chania, and Splantzia became the Muslim quarter of the town. The minaret stood out for its two balconies. Today it's about 36 meters tall, around 118 feet. With its conical roof, which is now gone, it would have topped 40 meters, over 130 feet.","There's a story told about this place. The story goes that a Dervish was the first to enter the city, climbed the bell tower, and, sword in hand, turned to the four points of the horizon, calling the faithful to prayer. His sword, they say, was kept here as a sacred relic.","The story goes that the sword stayed until 1923, when the exchange of populations between Turkey and Greece meant the Muslims of Crete had to leave. They took it with them."]},{"name":"A church again","minutes":1,"stand":"Step back so you can see both towers at once.","paras":["In 1918, the mosque was consecrated as an Orthodox church, dedicated to Saint Nicholas. And it kept its minaret.","If a service is on, step in quietly, or wait outside until it's over.","Catholic, Muslim, Orthodox: three communities have prayed under this one roof. Whatever you believe, it's worth standing here a quiet moment."]}] },
  { id: "chania-oldtown", v: "1-1d4826", city: /Chania|Hania|Souda/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(topanas|chania old town|old town of chania|etz hayyim)\b/i, title: "Chania Old Town and Topanas", where: "Chania Old Town and Topanas", checked: "2026-11-22",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Chania","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chania","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Chania","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Chania","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Etz Hayyim Synagogue","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Etz_Hayyim_Synagogue","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Kydonia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kydonia","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Fortifications of Chania","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Firkas_Fortress","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"The fountain square","minutes":1,"stand":"In Eleftherios Venizelos Square, the main square of the old town.","paras":["This is the main square of the old town, Eleftherios Venizelos Square. It's also known as Syntrivani, from the Turkish word for a fountain. It's the heart of the town's tourist life.","The old town's edge is the old Venetian wall, partly destroyed now. Only its eastern and western stretches survive. Inside that line, one account says, is the cradle of every civilization that grew up in this area.","Under Venice, the town became a blend of Byzantine, Venetian and Classical Greek culture, and many of its important buildings went up then. Later, under the Ottomans, Christians and Muslims lived mostly in different quarters. You're about to walk through both.","Good news for walkers: in much of the old town, no cars are allowed at all.","The lanes are narrow, so watch your step on the paving."]},{"name":"Topanas","minutes":1,"stand":"Walk west from the square into the lanes of Topanas.","paras":["You're in Topanas. Under the Ottomans, this was the Christian quarter. The name comes from a Venetian ammunition store that stood here, a tophane in Turkish.","Look around at the narrow alleys and old buildings. Some have been given new lives as hotels, restaurants, shops and bars. That's why it's so busy from April to October, and still lively at night in winter.","A travel guide calls these lanes a maze of houses that stood for hundreds of years, were rebuilt, ruined, and rebuilt again, with details from different epochs. So if one house seems to mix the styles of different ages, that's the point.","In the northwest corner of the old town, behind the harbor and inside Topanas, was the Jewish quarter, called Evraiki or Ovraiki. Our next stop is a synagogue. To get there, walk up Kondylaki Street from the harbor and take the second turn on the right."]},{"name":"Etz Hayyim Synagogue","minutes":1,"stand":"At the end of the short lane off Kondylaki Street, facing the synagogue's stone wall and courtyard entrance.","paras":["Straight ahead is the Etz Hayyim Synagogue, the last remnant of Crete's Romaniote Jewish community. In 1944, under the Nazi occupation, the Jewish community was deported, and most were drowned when their ship was sunk. The synagogue fell into ruin.","The building began as a church, and became a synagogue in the 1600s. The ship was the Tanais, and it was a British torpedo that sank her.","If it's open, step in quietly and respectfully.","One travel guide says that, until its restoration, it stood as a monument to the Nazis' success in wiping out 2,300 years of Jewish life on Crete.","Then, between 1996 and 1999, it was painstakingly restored and rededicated. The idea behind that work is summed up in a Hebrew phrase, Am Israel Hayy: the people of Israel live.","Today it's seen locally as a symbol of coexistence. Almost none of its congregants are Jewish, and Christians and Muslims are welcome to visit.","It hasn't been easy. In January 2010, arsonists struck twice. The first fire was quickly put out. The second destroyed 2,500 rare books and manuscripts."]},{"name":"Kastelli hill","minutes":2,"stand":"Go back past the main square and up onto the low hill just east of it.","paras":["This small hill is Kastelli, the heart of the old town, and the site of ancient Kydonia. People have lived up here since Neolithic times.","It's easy to see why: a safe height, right by the harbor, with a fertile valley to the south. Today it's a little quieter than the lanes to the west.","Kydonia even gave English a word. The Greeks linked the city with the quince fruit, and the word quince comes, in the end, from the Greek for Kydonian apple.","If you come across old walls, look closely. The base of a Hellenistic wall can be seen below the Byzantine wall of Kastelli hill, and the Byzantines built theirs partly with stones from the ancient city. More of the Byzantine wall survives in Sifaka Street.","Digs in ancient Kydonia found a large archive of Linear A tablets, 97 of them, the second largest archive known. They're mostly economic records: crops, people and animals.","One legend gives the city a founder: a king named Cydon, son of Hermes or Apollo, and of Akakallis, the daughter of King Minos.","And the name Chania? Scholars suggest it came from the Arabs, who called the city al-Khan, the caravanserai, or inn. It may also echo an old neighborhood where the Minoan god Welchanos was worshipped. Nobody knows for sure.","Under the Ottomans, Muslims lived mainly here on Kastelli and in Splantzia, next door to the east. Splantzia is still largely untouched."]},{"name":"Before you go","minutes":1,"stand":"Find a quiet corner of a lane.","paras":["A Christian quarter, a Jewish quarter, Muslim quarters, all inside one set of walls. These lanes have seen neighbors live side by side, and seen them torn apart. Walk them slowly."]}] },
  { id: "heraklion-heramuseum", v: "1-a0613c", city: /Heraklion|Iraklion|Iraklio/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(heraklion archaeological museum|archaeological museum of heraklion)\b/i, title: "Heraklion Archaeological Museum", where: "Heraklion Archaeological Museum", checked: "2026-11-22",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Heraklion Archaeological Museum","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heraklion_Archaeological_Museum","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Phaistos Disc","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phaistos_Disc","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Minoan snake goddess figurines","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snake_Goddess","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Heraklion","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Heraklion","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Bull-Leaping Fresco","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bull-leaping_fresco","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Minoan civilization","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minoan_civilization","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"King James Version (public domain), via bible-api.com","title":"","url":"https://bible-api.com/acts%2027%3A7-13?translation=kjv","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Knossos","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knossos","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Arrival","minutes":1,"stand":"Outside the museum entrance, where you can see the front of the building.","paras":["This is the Heraklion Archaeological Museum, one of the largest museums in Greece, and often called the best in the world for Minoan art. It holds the great majority of the finds from the palace at Knossos and other Minoan sites on Crete.","The collection covers 5,500 years of Cretan history, from the Neolithic to Roman times. It began in 1883 as a simple collection of antiquities, just as the Minoans were being rediscovered. Crete was then largely self-governing, and that helped keep its finds here, on the island.","If your time is short, look for three highlights first: the Phaistos Disc, the Snake Goddess figures, and the Bull-Leaping Fresco.","The building is worth a glance too. It's a modernist design by the architect Patroklos Karantinos, hired in 1935, and it came through the bombing of the German invasion in 1941. Look for the multicolored veined marble in the building. It echoes the painted imitation marble on Minoan palace walls."]},{"name":"The Phaistos Disc","minutes":1,"stand":"In front of the glass case with the round clay disc.","paras":["This small clay disc is the Phaistos Disc. It's about 16 centimeters across, with a spiral of signs on each side, in a script and a language nobody knows.","The Italian archaeologist Luigi Pernier found it in 1908, in the palace of Phaistos near the south coast. Look closely. There are 242 signs in all, of 45 different kinds, and each one was pressed into the soft clay with its own stamp before firing. That's why it's sometimes called an early example of printing with movable type.","Can anyone read it? Many have tried. Scholars think a real decipherment is unlikely unless more examples of these signs turn up somewhere else.","Because it's so unusual, some scholars once suspected a forgery. Archaeologists now generally accept it as genuine. Even its date is uncertain: one scholar puts it between 1850 and 1600 BC, another after 1400 BC."]},{"name":"The snake goddesses","minutes":1,"stand":"At the case with the small faience figures holding up snakes.","paras":["These figures came from the palace at Knossos, found in 1903, and they date to around 1600 BC. Arthur Evans called the larger one a Snake Goddess and the smaller one a Snake Priestess. Was he right? Scholars still argue.","They're made of faience, a crushed quartz paste that fires to a bright, glassy finish. Now look carefully, because a lot of what you see was restored. The larger figure was missing everything below the waist, and more. The smaller one had no head; Evans and one of his restorers made the head you see today.","Some scholars go further and argue they aren't really holding snakes at all, or at least not as many as Evans thought.","They caused such a stir that forgers got busy. A figure the Boston Museum of Fine Arts bought in 1914, and another in the Walters Art Museum in Baltimore, are now regarded as fakes."]},{"name":"The frescoes","minutes":1,"stand":"Among the frescoes, in front of the panel of the leaping bull.","paras":["Here is the Bull-Leaping Fresco from Knossos, one of the museum's highlights. Three figures and a bull, drawn with its legs stretched out as if at full gallop. A young man is over the bull's back.","By the accepted convention of Minoan art, women are painted pale and men dark. So by that convention, the figures are read as two women, one at each end, and a young man on the bull. The woman in front holds the horns. And remember, this is the most completely restored of several panels. There are more fragments than made it into this famous reconstruction.","What exactly were they doing, and why? Nobody knows for sure. A century after the discovery, the technique and the reasons remain obscure.","In the fresco rooms nearby, look for the one nicknamed La Parisienne. A French art historian thought she looked like the Parisian women of the day, and the name stuck."]},{"name":"Closing","minutes":1,"stand":"On your way out, wherever you can pause for a moment.","paras":["The museum's story runs on into Greek and Roman sculpture. And Crete appears in the Bible too. Acts chapter 27, in the King James Version, tells of the voyage of the apostle Paul. It says they \"sailed under Crete, over against Salmone,\" and \"came unto a place which is called The fair havens.\" There, with sailing now dangerous, Paul admonished them.","Think about what you've seen. They left writing we still can't read. What they left us is bulls, figures with snakes, and one clay disc. And somehow, standing here, they don't feel like strangers."]}] },
  { id: "heraklion-knossos", v: "1-05843d", city: /Heraklion|Iraklion|Iraklio/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(knossos)\b/i, title: "The Palace of Knossos", where: "The Palace of Knossos", checked: "2026-11-22",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Knossos","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knossos","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Arthur Evans","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arthur_Evans","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Heraklion","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Heraklion","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","title":"Minoan Palatial Centres - UNESCO World Heritage Centre","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1733/","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Bull-Leaping Fresco","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bull-leaping_fresco","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Minotaur","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minotaur","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Labyrinth","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Labyrinth","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Arrival","minutes":1,"stand":"In the wide paved court on the west side of the palace, looking toward the ruins.","paras":["Welcome to Knossos, about 5 kilometers south of the center of Heraklion. This hill is often called Europe's oldest city, and the palace in front of you was first built around 1900 BC.","Watch your step on the uneven old paving and stairs, and on a hot day, drink water as you go.","This open space is the West Court. It was a main way into the palace, and it probably had a ceremonial role too. In fact, what the whole building was for is a good question. It's generally read as a center for running things: trade, religion, ceremonies. Whether a king actually lived here, scholars still argue.","People were living on this hill long before any palace, from around 7000 BC. The palace covered about 20,000 square meters, several stories high. One count puts it at over 1,000 interlocking rooms."]},{"name":"A palace in layers","minutes":1,"stand":"Stay in the West Court, facing the palace walls.","paras":["What you see is mostly the second palace. The first was destroyed around 1700 BC, probably by an earthquake.","It was rebuilt bigger and grander, and that second palace was the height of Minoan Knossos. Then, around 1450 BC, it was partly destroyed again. It kept going, but Mycenaean Greek influence grew stronger. A great fire is usually dated to about 1350 BC, though when the palace finally came to an end, archaeologists still argue.","The palace kept careful records. Thousands of clay tablets in a script called Linear B turned up here. When Michael Ventris and John Chadwick deciphered it, it turned out to be an early form of Greek. The older Minoan script, Linear A, still hasn't been read."]},{"name":"The storerooms","minutes":1,"stand":"Walk along the west side of the palace to where you can see the long, narrow storerooms.","paras":["These long, narrow rooms are the magazines, the palace storerooms. They held huge clay jars called pithoi, for oil, grain, wine and other goods.","Storing, recording and handing out goods was at the heart of how this place worked. The clay records suggest the palace kept track of crops, livestock, crafts and offerings.","This wing is also where modern digging began. A merchant from Heraklion, Minos Kalokairinos, uncovered part of the west wing and found storerooms full of big jars. That was in 1878 and 1879, or 1877 by another account. Ottoman authorities soon made him stop."]},{"name":"The Central Court","minutes":1,"stand":"Come into the big open rectangle at the heart of the palace and stand near its middle.","paras":["This is the Central Court. The whole palace was arranged around it.","It was probably used for ceremonies, for gatherings, and simply for getting from one part of the building to another.","Get your bearings. The west wing held the storerooms, the pillar crypts and the Throne Room. The east wing was built into the slope of the hill, with fine rooms like the Hall of the Double Axes and the Queen's Hall.","Notice how the builders handled such a crowded building. Light wells and open shafts brought daylight and air deep inside, and drains carried away rainwater and waste. Up on this hill, the palace also caught the summer sea breezes."]},{"name":"The Throne Room","minutes":1,"stand":"Go to the Throne Room on the west side of the Central Court and look in at the stone seat.","paras":["This is the Throne Room: a gypsum seat against the wall, benches, and painted griffins. Arthur Evans called it the throne room of King Minos. Was it? Scholars still argue.","There's also a so-called lustral basin. Some scholars think the room was used for ritual, perhaps by priests, rather than by a king.","Now look at the painted walls. After the dig, Evans had this room repainted by a Swiss father and son, both named Émile Gilliéron. His critics say some of the best-known frescoes here were almost complete inventions of theirs."]},{"name":"Evans and the concrete","minutes":1,"stand":"Find a spot where you can see one of the rebuilt columns.","paras":["See how those columns get narrower toward the bottom? That's the Minoan shape. But what you're looking at is a modern reconstruction. The archaeologist Arthur Evans rebuilt parts of this palace using reinforced concrete.","Unlike later Greek columns, Minoan ones often taper downward, and the originals were usually wood set on stone bases.","Evans was a British archaeologist who bought the land here with private money and began digging in 1900. He uncovered most of the palace. He also made the name Minoan stick for this whole civilization, after the mythical King Minos.","His rebuilding is still argued over. It makes the palace easy to picture, but critics say it sometimes goes further than the evidence allows. UNESCO, which listed Knossos as a World Heritage Site in 2025, notes that the reconstructions are well documented and that large portions of the site remain authentic.","The same goes for the famous paintings. Many survive only as fragments, and a familiar picture may combine ancient pieces with modern additions. Many of the most important finds, fresco fragments among them, are in the Heraklion Archaeological Museum in town."]},{"name":"The bull","minutes":1,"stand":"Walk to the North Entrance of the palace, where a plaster relief of a bull is shown.","paras":["Bulls turn up again and again at Knossos. Here at the North Entrance, a painted plaster relief shows one.","Evans found so many bull scenes on these walls that he decided the Minoans worshipped the bull.","The most completely restored is the Bull-Leaping Fresco. It was once high up on the east wall of the palace, on an upper floor. In these scenes, a leaper goes over the bull upside down. How they did it, and why, nobody knows for sure, even a century after the discovery."]},{"name":"The Labyrinth","minutes":2,"stand":"Stay near the North Entrance, or find a quiet spot nearby.","paras":["And now the legend. The story goes that King Minos had the craftsman Daedalus build a Labyrinth to hold the Minotaur, a creature with a man's body and a bull's head. Athens had to send young men and women as tribute, until the hero Theseus, helped by Minos's daughter Ariadne, killed the Minotaur and found his way out by following a thread.","Where did the monster come from? In the myth, Minos asked the sea god Poseidon for a snow-white bull to sacrifice, but it was so beautiful that he kept it. To punish him, Poseidon made Minos's wife, Pasiphaë, fall in love with the bull, and she gave birth to the Minotaur. Some versions say Athens sent seven young men and seven young women every seventh or ninth year; others say every year.","So was this palace the Labyrinth? Evans suggested so. Some scholars link the word labyrinth with labrys, the double axe, but that's debated. And you can see how a building of courts, corridors, stairways and levels could grow, in Greek memory, into a maze. Not everyone agrees, though. One Oxford geographer says Evans's idea must be treated skeptically.","The link is old, either way. Ancient Knossos put the Labyrinth on its coins. And UNESCO says the legend of the Minotaur and the Labyrinth is directly tied to these palaces."]},{"name":"Closing","minutes":1,"stand":"On your way out, pause for a last look back over the ruins.","paras":["Around the palace you'll find more of the ancient town, including the Theatral Area and the Royal Road, which linked the palace with the town around it.","Take one last look. Strip away the concrete and the legends, and what's left is people: people who stored their oil, kept their lists, and painted their walls with bulls. The monster is a story. They were real."]}] },
  { id: "heraklion-koules", v: "1-a9973c", city: /Heraklion|Iraklion|Iraklio/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(koules|castello a mare|rocca a mare)\b/i, title: "The Venetian Harbor and Koules Fortress", where: "The Venetian Harbor and Koules Fortress", checked: "2026-11-22",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Koules Fortress","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Koules_Fortress","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Heraklion","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Heraklion","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Heraklion","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heraklion","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Siege of Candia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siege_of_Candia","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Arrival","minutes":1,"stand":"On the harbor breakwater, facing the fortress at the entrance of the old port.","paras":["This is the Koules, the Venetians' Castello a Mare, the Fort on the Sea. It guards the entrance to the old port of Heraklion, and the Republic of Venice built it in the early 16th century.","Mind your footing out here. If waves are breaking over the breakwater, don't go out along it. Stay by the fortress.","Venice bought its claim to this city in 1204 and called it Candia. To hold it, over the centuries that followed, the Venetians built a giant wall with seven bastions, and a fortress in the harbor. This one.","There may have been a fort here since Arab times, and later there was a Byzantine tower. The Venetians knocked it down in 1523. To make the platform bigger, they filled old ships with stone and sank them as a breakwater. The fortress was finished in 1540."]},{"name":"The fortress","minutes":1,"stand":"At the fortress entrance, looking at the thickness of the walls.","paras":["The fortress comes in two parts: a tall rectangular block and a lower, rounded section. In places, the walls are 8.7 meters thick.","Inside, on two floors, there were 26 rooms: barracks, a prison, storerooms, a water reservoir, a church, a mill and a bakery. A small town, sealed in stone.","In 1630 it carried 18 cannons on the ground floor, and 25 more along the way up to the roof.","If you get up to the parapet, look out to sea toward the islet of Dia. Jacques Cousteau found many sunken remains of Cretan sea trade out there. On the northern part of the fort is a lighthouse tower; a lighthouse was built here under Egyptian rule, between 1830 and 1840."]},{"name":"The siege of Candia","minutes":1,"stand":"On the breakwater, looking back toward the city.","paras":["This fort's great test was the siege of Candia. The Ottomans besieged the city from 1648 to 1669. That's 21 years, one of the longest sieges in history.","The fort didn't count for much in the siege. Ottoman guns easily put its firepower out of action. In the end, on 27 September 1669, the Venetian commander, Francesco Morosini, with only 3,600 fit men left, accepted terms and surrendered.","The cost was terrible. By one account, in the final 22 months alone, some 70,000 Turks, 38,000 Cretans and slaves, and 29,088 of the city's Christian defenders died.","Under the terms, all Christians could leave Candia with whatever they could carry. And Morosini? He'd surrendered without the Venetian Senate's permission, and for years he was a controversial figure back home. One more strange detail from the Venetian archives: a plan to break the siege by infecting the Ottoman soldiers with plague. It was never carried out."]},{"name":"Closing","minutes":1,"stand":"If the sea is calm, walk a little way out along the breakwater and look back; if not, stay where you are and look back at the fortress.","paras":["The Ottomans changed little here, adding some battlements. They built a small fort on the land side, Little Koules, but it was torn down in 1936, when the city was being modernized. Today the fortress is restored and open to visitors.","The breakwater runs on for almost 2 kilometers, with views back over the city and the ferry port.","Look at the fort against the city. For 21 years, it was part of a defense that people would not give up. Take a moment before you walk back."]}] },
  { id: "heraklion-lions", v: "1-08e548", city: /Heraklion|Iraklion|Iraklio/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(lions square|morosini fountain|eleftheriou venizelou)\b/i, title: "Lions Square and 25th August Street", where: "Lions Square and 25th August Street", checked: "2026-11-22",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Lions Square","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Morosini_Fountain","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Heraklion","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heraklion","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Saint Mark's Basilica, Heraklion","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basilica_of_St_Mark%2C_Heraklion","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Heraklion","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Heraklion","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Lions Square","minutes":1,"stand":"Beside the fountain in the middle of the square, where you can see the lions.","paras":["This is Lions Square. Its official name is Eleftheriou Venizelou Square, after the Cretan statesman Eleftherios Venizelos. But everyone calls it after the fountain in the middle, the Morosini Fountain, where four lions hold up the main basin.","The square goes back to Venetian times, and the fountain dates from 1628. It's one of several Venetian fountains still standing around the city.","Back then this city was Candia, seat of the Venetian Duke of Candia. From 1212 Venice settled its own families on Crete. Two cultures side by side, plus the spark of the Italian Renaissance, gave rise to a flowering of writing and art now called the Cretan Renaissance."]},{"name":"Saint Mark's Basilica","minutes":1,"stand":"Face the church with the columned porch, facing the fountain.","paras":["That church with the porch of columns is the Basilica of Saint Mark. The Venetians built it in 1239, in Gothic style, for the Duke and his officials.","During the long war with the Ottomans, its bell tower served as an observation post, and its bells rang every time the bombardment began.","After the city fell in 1669, it became a mosque, named for its founder, Defterdar Ahmet Pasha, and it stayed a mosque until 1915. Later it was used as a cinema. Restoration began in 1956, and since 1960 it has been the city's art gallery.","If it's open, step inside and look at the green granite columns. Each one is a different height, and nobody knows exactly why. One theory says they came from older buildings. One scholar thinks they reflect centuries of repairs. And the porch out front? Merchants once sold grain there."]},{"name":"The Loggia","minutes":1,"stand":"Walk from the square along Twenty-Fifth of August Street to the Venetian Loggia.","paras":["This elegant building is the Venetian Loggia, on 25th August Street, right next to Lions Square. It's a Renaissance building, first built from 1626 to 1628 as a meeting place for the city.","Stand here for a minute and watch the people pass. Venetians, Ottomans and modern Cretans have all walked this street. Cities change hands. Meeting places stay."]}] },
  { id: "heraklion-minas", v: "1-0aa0cd", city: /Heraklion|Iraklion|Iraklio/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(agios minas|saint minas|st minas|saint menas)\b/i, title: "Agios Minas Cathedral", where: "Agios Minas Cathedral", checked: "2026-11-22",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Agios Minas Cathedral","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agios_Minas_Cathedral","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"King James Version (public domain), via bible-api.com","title":"","url":"https://bible-api.com/titus%201%3A5-12?translation=kjv","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Gortyn","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gortyn","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Arrival","minutes":1,"stand":"In the square in front of the cathedral, facing its main front.","paras":["This great church is Agios Minas, the cathedral of Saint Menas, and the seat of the Archbishop of Crete. It's the largest cathedral on Crete, and one of the largest in Greece.","Saint Menas is the patron saint of Heraklion. Tradition remembers him as a martyr and a wonderworker, and gives his dates as 285 to 309 AD.","Building it took a long time. The foundation stone was laid in 1862, on a garden bought from a Turkish owner, to designs by Athanasios Moussis. Work stopped during the Cretan Revolution of 1866 to 1869, started again in 1883, and was finished in 1895.","Take in the shape. It's laid out as a cross, with a central dome, and there are two bell towers. The whole church is 43.2 meters long and 29.5 meters wide."]},{"name":"Little Agios Minas","minutes":1,"stand":"Look for the small old church close by the cathedral.","paras":["Close by stands the small old church of Agios Minas, built in 1735. Locals call it little Agios Minas. As the Christian community grew, it became too small, and that's why the big cathedral was built."]},{"name":"Inside","minutes":1,"stand":"Inside, a little way in from the door, facing the icon screen.","paras":["If a service is going on, keep your voice low and stay toward the back.","The side aisles have their own dedications: one to the Apostle Titus, the other to the Ten Martyrs of Crete.","Titus brings the Bible into this church. Paul's letter to Titus, chapter 1, verse 5, in the King James Version, says: \"For this cause left I thee in Crete, that thou shouldest set in order the things that are wanting, and ordain elders in every city, as I had appointed thee.\" That's Scripture. Church tradition honors Titus as Bishop of Crete, and a saint.","The icon screen in front of you is marble. Following plans by the architect Anastasios Orlandos, it replaced an earlier carved wooden one, and the bishop's seat was redone in marble too. The paintings follow the principles of Byzantine iconography."]},{"name":"Closing","minutes":1,"stand":"On your way out, at the door.","paras":["Before you go, think about how this church was built. People started it, stopped during a revolution, and came back years later to finish it. Some things are worth waiting for."]}] },
  { id: "heraklion-phaistos", v: "1-bbea4f", city: /Heraklion|Iraklion|Iraklio/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(phaistos)\b/i, title: "Phaistos", where: "Phaistos", checked: "2026-11-22",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Phaistos","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phaistos","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","title":"Minoan Palatial Centres - UNESCO World Heritage Centre","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1733/","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Phaistos Disc","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phaistos_Disc","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Knossos","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knossos","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Arrival","minutes":1,"stand":"At the top of the site, where you can look down over the palace ruins.","paras":["This is Phaistos, a Minoan palace and town on a hill in south central Crete, about 62 kilometers from Heraklion. It was one of the 3 largest cities of Minoan Crete, along with Knossos and Malia.","On a hot day, take it slowly, drink water, and watch your step on the old stones and stairs.","We call it a palace, but it was much more than a royal home. Like other Minoan palaces, it seems to have had many uses: religion and politics, storage, housing, even a theater.","Since 2025 it has been a UNESCO World Heritage Site, together with Knossos. And here's the difference you'll notice. UNESCO says most of these sites kept their original forms, thanks to a light touch in conservation, while Knossos was heavily rebuilt in the early 20th century."]},{"name":"A palace rebuilt","minutes":1,"stand":"Stay at the top, looking over the layers of walls.","paras":["People settled here around 3600 BC, and the first palace went up around 2000 BC.","It was destroyed and rebuilt three times in about three centuries, each new palace shifting slightly east of the first. Then, around 1400 BC by one dating, it was destroyed for good. A large earthquake may have been the cause.","That's partly why finds are scarce here: the Minoans thoroughly cleared away the old ruins before they rebuilt. People lived here again in Greek times, and the town struck its own coins, until the Gortynians destroyed it around the end of the 3rd century BC."]},{"name":"The theater area","minutes":1,"stand":"Go down to the theater area, by the staircases that lead up into the palace.","paras":["This was the theater area, and it was larger than the one at Knossos, even though the palace as a whole was only about two thirds the size.","From here, two staircases led up through large doors into the main entrance hall. The palace rose in levels. The theater and shrines were at the bottom, then rooms for servants and guests. Above them were the royal apartments, and at the top the Hall of the State, with balconies looking out over it all."]},{"name":"The central court","minutes":1,"stand":"In the central court, facing north toward the mountains.","paras":["From this central court, a way led north to the royal apartments, and from there you could see the tops of Psiloritis, Mount Ida.","By the northern edge of the palace, in a basement room, the Phaistos Disc was found in 1908, together with a Linear A tablet. It's now in the archaeological museum in Heraklion. And in the southern part of the palace, excavators found a temple to the goddess Rhea, built later, after the Geometric age.","Phaistos was first located in 1853 by a ship's captain, Thomas Spratt. Then came the excavators: Federico Halbherr and his student Luigi Pernier dug here from 1900 to 1904, and also at Hagia Triada, a smaller palace about 3 kilometers away. Italian teams have come back many times since."]},{"name":"Closing","minutes":1,"stand":"Find a quiet spot at the edge of the hill, looking out.","paras":["The Greeks told stories about this place. In legend, according to the historian Diodorus, King Minos founded Phaistos, along with Knossos and Kydonia. Others said the founder was Phaestos, a son of Hercules, in one telling. And Homer, in the Iliad, calls it well populated.","Look out from here for a moment. The people who lived in these rooms looked at the same mountains. We can't read their writing. But we know they chose a beautiful place to live."]}] },
  { id: "kusadasi-artemis", v: "1-d3e888", city: /Kusadasi|Kuşadası|Ephesus|Selcuk|Selçuk|Izmir/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(artemis|artemision|temple of diana)\b/i, title: "Temple of Artemis", where: "Temple of Artemis", checked: "2026-11-22",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Temple of Artemis","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Artemis","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","title":"Ephesus - UNESCO World Heritage Centre","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1018/","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Herostratus","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Herostratus","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Selçuk","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Sel%C3%A7uk","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"King James Version (public domain), via bible-api.com","title":"","url":"https://bible-api.com/acts%2019%3A1-41?translation=kjv","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"The lone column","minutes":1,"stand":"On the path at the edge of the field, facing the single tall column.","paras":["That one column, standing in a marshy field, is nearly all that's left of the Temple of Artemis, one of the Seven Wonders of the ancient world. It's not even an original column: it was put together from fragments found here.","The goddess here was a local form of Artemis, whom the Romans called Diana. Keep that name in mind; we'll meet it again in the Bible.","Notice how wet the ground is. This was always a swampy site, and the high water table has made digging hard.","After rain the ground here can be soft and muddy. Mind your footing."]},{"name":"Temples, fire and fame","minutes":2,"stand":"Stay facing the column and picture a forest of marble columns around it.","paras":["There were several temples here. An early one was wiped out by a flood in the 7th century BC. From around 550 BC the architect Chersiphron and his son Metagenes built a new one, 377 feet long and 151 feet wide, said to be the first Greek temple built of marble. King Croesus helped pay for it.","The Roman writer Pliny claims they chose this marshy ground on purpose, as a precaution against earthquakes.","In 356 BC the temple here burned. As the story goes, a man named Herostratus set fire to the roof beams for one reason: to be famous. The Ephesians executed him and banned anyone from saying his name. It didn't work. His name became a word for someone who commits a crime just to be known.","Was it really him? Scholars aren't sure. He confessed his motive under torture, and some suggest the temple's own managers may have set the fire, because they knew the foundation was sinking.","Ancient tradition added a flourish: the fire happened on the very day Alexander the Great was born. Plutarch remarks that Artemis was too busy with the delivery to save her temple. Years later Alexander offered to pay for the rebuilding, and the Ephesians tactfully refused. It would be improper, they said, for one god to build a temple to another.","They rebuilt it themselves from 323 BC, even bigger: 450 feet long, 225 wide, 60 feet high, with more than 127 columns. Antipater of Sidon, who drew up a list of the Seven Wonders, wrote that when he saw the house of Artemis, the other wonders lost their brilliancy."]},{"name":"Great is Diana","minutes":1,"stand":"Stay by the column.","paras":["This is the temple behind the riot in the book of Acts. A silversmith named Demetrius made silver shrines for Diana, and he warned the craftsmen that because of Paul, the temple of the great goddess Diana might be despised, whom all Asia and the world worshippeth. They cried out, Great is Diana of the Ephesians.","The town clerk who calmed them asked who did not know that Ephesus was a worshipper of the great goddess Diana, and of the image which fell down from Jupiter. That detail, an image fallen from the sky, is known for this temple from Acts alone.","In the Ephesus Museum in Selçuk you may see her statues, covered in oval shapes. They were long read as many breasts, a sign of fertility. Modern scholars doubt it."]},{"name":"How it disappeared","minutes":1,"stand":"Look around the field at the scattered stones.","paras":["According to the historian Jordanes, Goths set fire to the temple in 268 AD. How badly it was damaged, nobody knows. Christian writers mention it being closed in the 5th century, and some of its stone was carried off for other buildings.","There's a medieval legend that columns from here hold up the Hagia Sophia in Istanbul. A nice story, but there's no truth to it.","The site was lost so completely that it took an expedition led by John Turtle Wood, for the British Museum, six years of searching to find it again, in 1869.","So here is one of the wonders of the world, reduced to a single column. And as the story goes, a man burned it just to be remembered. Stand here a moment and ask yourself the better question: what would you want to be remembered for?"]}] },
  { id: "kusadasi-ephesus", v: "1-50a88a", city: /Kusadasi|Kuşadası|Ephesus|Selcuk|Selçuk|Izmir/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(ephesus|efes|celsus|curetes|terrace houses)\b/i, title: "Ancient Ephesus", where: "Ancient Ephesus", checked: "2026-11-22",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Ephesus","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ephesus","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","title":"Ephesus - UNESCO World Heritage Centre","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1018/","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Ephesus","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Ephesus","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Library of Celsus","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Library_of_Celsus","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Temple of Artemis","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Artemis","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"King James Version (public domain), via bible-api.com","title":"","url":"https://bible-api.com/acts%2019%3A1-41?translation=kjv","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"King James Version (public domain), via bible-api.com","title":"","url":"https://bible-api.com/acts%2020%3A17-38?translation=kjv","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Council of Ephesus","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Council_of_Ephesus","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Paul the Apostle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paul_the_Apostle","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"King James Version (public domain), via bible-api.com","title":"","url":"https://bible-api.com/revelation%202%3A1-7?translation=kjv","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"The upper city","minutes":1,"stand":"Just inside the upper gate, at the edge of the open market square, facing downhill.","paras":["Welcome to Ephesus. Under the Romans this was the capital of their province of Asia, and the geographer Strabo ranked it second only to Rome itself.","First, the heat. Summer noons here push 36 or 37 degrees Celsius, and there's little shade. Wear a hat, carry water, and watch your feet on the old marble.","The Greek city was built in the 10th century BC, and since 2015 the ruins have been a UNESCO World Heritage Site. We'll walk the way most people do, north and downhill, so the slope works for us.","Around you is the upper city: a market square, the scrappy remains of the Baths of Varius, a colonnaded walkway, and the Odeon, a little roofed theater for plays and concerts, with seats for about 1,500 people. A citizen named Publius Vedius Antoninus and his wife built it around 150 AD.","How many people lived here? Older books said up to 225,000. Scholars today call that unrealistic. Mountains, the coast and the quarries hemmed the city in. Recent estimates differ widely; one puts the population between 33,600 and 56,000."]},{"name":"Curetes Street","minutes":1,"stand":"At the Gate of Hercules, at the top of the marble street that slopes away downhill.","paras":["This is Curetes Street, the main boulevard of the ancient city, leading downhill toward the library.","UNESCO calls it an old processional way, lined with grand Roman monuments. Along this stretch, watch for a big public fountain, the Fountain of Trajan. A leading citizen, Claudius Aristion, paid for it and the water system from 102 AD, and the inscription makes very sure you know who paid."]},{"name":"The Temple of Hadrian","minutes":1,"stand":"Farther down Curetes Street, in front of the small Temple of Hadrian, with the roofed Terrace Houses across the street behind you.","paras":["This compact little building is the Temple of Hadrian, from the 2nd century, stood up again from its own surviving pieces. Look for Medusa among the carvings on its porch.","The carved panels up top are casts; the originals are in the Ephesus Archaeological Museum. One shows the city's founder from legend, Androklos, a prince of Athens, with his dog. The story goes that an oracle told him a fish and a boar would show him the way, and he founded Ephesus where that came true.","Was it Hadrian's temple from the start? Nobody's sure. The dedication to the emperor may have come later."]},{"name":"The Terrace Houses","minutes":1,"stand":"Turn around to face the slope with the big protective roof, across the street from the temple.","paras":["Up on this slope lived the rich. The Terrace Houses are six luxury Roman homes, with mosaics on the floors and paintings on the walls, kept under that big roof.","The oldest goes back to the first century BC, and some were still lived in until the seventh century AD. UNESCO singles out Terrace House 2, with its wall paintings, mosaics and marble paneling, as a picture of how the upper levels of society lived.","If you go inside, take the stairs and walkways slowly, and hold the rails.","And everyone else? Farther along the main street, by a monument called the Octagon, is the public latrine, a marble 48-seater. Only the people in these houses and a handful of other very rich families had their own toilets. The latrine was roofed, and used water from the baths ran beneath the seats."]},{"name":"The Library of Celsus","minutes":2,"stand":"At the foot of Curetes Street, in front of the library steps, facing the two-story façade.","paras":["And here it is, the Library of Celsus. A Roman consul named Aquila ordered it in the 110s as a monument to his father, Celsus, a former governor of Asia. It was finished after Aquila's own death. Celsus himself lies in a marble sarcophagus in a crypt beneath it.","It was a working library too, believed to have held around 12,000 scrolls, third in the Greek and Roman world after Alexandria and Pergamum. It faces east, so the reading room caught the morning light.","In 262 a fire gutted the inside, books and all. Nobody knows whether an earthquake or a Gothic invasion started it; the city seems to have suffered both that year. The façade stood alone until an earthquake in the 10th or 11th century brought it down, and archaeologists put it back up between 1970 and 1978.","Look at the four niches on the lower level, between the columns. Each holds a woman standing for a virtue: Sophia, wisdom; Episteme, knowledge; Ennoia, intelligence; and Arete, excellence. The statues there now are not the originals. And the whole front uses false perspective, so it looks bigger and grander than it really is.","One small thing most people miss: on the second step from the top of the staircase, someone carved a seven-branched menorah, about 8 inches across. Nobody knows exactly when."]},{"name":"The Great Theater","minutes":2,"stand":"Walk on from the library to the Great Theater, and stand at the bottom, looking up at the seats.","paras":["This is the Great Theater. Acts, chapter nineteen, tells how the whole city was filled with confusion, and a crowd rushed with one accord into the theatre, having caught two of Paul's companions, Gaius and Aristarchus. Later, all with one voice, about the space of two hours, they cried out, Great is Diana of the Ephesians.","It's set into the hillside, built from 200 BC, enlarged in the 1st and 2nd centuries AD, and never roofed. Figures for its size differ: around 24,000 seats, or 25,000.","How did the riot start? Acts says Paul preached in this city, in the synagogue for three months, then daily in the school of one Tyrannus for two years, so that all they which dwelt in Asia heard the word.","Then a silversmith named Demetrius, who made silver shrines for Diana, the goddess the Greeks called Artemis, gathered the craftsmen. Sirs, he said, ye know that by this craft we have our wealth. This Paul hath persuaded and turned away much people, saying that they be no gods, which are made with hands. Not only this our craft is in danger, but also that the temple of the great goddess Diana should be despised.","And Paul? He wanted to go in to the people, but the disciples suffered him not, and friends among the chief men of Asia begged him not to adventure himself into the theatre. Acts adds a very human detail: the more part knew not wherefore they were come together.","In the end the town clerk calmed them. These men, he said, are neither robbers of churches, nor yet blasphemers of your goddess. We are in danger to be called in question for this day's uproar. And he dismissed the assembly.","If you climb the seats for the view, go slowly. The stone steps are steep and uneven."]},{"name":"Harbor Street and farewell","minutes":1,"stand":"At the top of the broad avenue running west from the theater, looking down it toward the old harbor.","paras":["This avenue ran down to the harbor, the life of the city. But the river slowly silted it up, and today the old harbor lies about 5 kilometers inland.","Paul left Ephesus after the riot. Later, Acts, chapter twenty, says, he sent from Miletus to Ephesus and called the elders of the church. He told them they would see his face no more, and reminded them of the words of the Lord Jesus: It is more blessed to give than to receive. And they all wept sore, and fell on Paul's neck, and kissed him, and accompanied him unto the ship.","Near the lower gate is the Church of Mary, probably where the Council of Ephesus met in 431. About 250 bishops condemned the teaching of Nestorius and declared Mary Theotokos, the Mother of God.","One more voice. In Revelation, chapter two, Ephesus is the first of the seven churches addressed: I know thy works, and thy labour, and thy patience. Nevertheless I have somewhat against thee, because thou hast left thy first love.","Take a last look down this street before you go. And carry that letter's question with you: what was your first love, and have you left it?"]}] },
  { id: "kusadasi-marydouse", v: "1-398ffc", city: /Kusadasi|Kuşadası|Ephesus|Selcuk|Selçuk|Izmir/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(virgin mary|meryem ana|meryemana|mary's house)\b/i, title: "House of the Virgin Mary", where: "House of the Virgin Mary", checked: "2026-11-22",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"House of the Virgin Mary","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House_of_the_Virgin_Mary","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"King James Version (public domain), via bible-api.com","title":"","url":"https://bible-api.com/john%2019%3A25-27?translation=kjv","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Ephesus","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ephesus","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Anne Catherine Emmerich","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anne_Catherine_Emmerich","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Arrival","minutes":1,"stand":"On the path just below the little stone house, where you can see its front.","paras":["This small stone chapel on Mount Koressos, the Mount of the Nightingale, is the House of the Virgin Mary. Christian and Muslim pilgrims come here because of a tradition that Saint John brought Mary to this house, and that she lived here for the rest of her life.","Did you notice the keyhole-shaped baptismal pool you passed on the way up? Some think early Christians may have been baptized here in secret.","The paths here slope a little. Take your time, and keep your voice low near the chapel; people come here to pray."]},{"name":"Scripture and tradition","minutes":1,"stand":"Stay outside the door for a moment, before you go in.","paras":["Let's keep two things apart: what the Bible says, and what tradition says. The Bible, in the Gospel of John, says that from the cross Jesus told his mother, Woman, behold thy son! and told the disciple, Behold thy mother! And from that hour that disciple took her unto his own home. That is all Scripture tells us. It never says where that home was.","Even in ancient times people noticed the gap. The church writer Epiphanius pointed out that the Bible says John went to Asia, but not that Mary went with him, and he later said she was buried in Jerusalem.","So two traditions grew up. The older one places the end of Mary's life in Jerusalem. The idea of Ephesus is old, but as a settled tradition it dates from the 12th century, and it has always competed with the older Jerusalem one. So where Mary spent her last years is uncertain. One scholarly assessment puts it plainly: the evidence that Mary stayed here is not very strong, and the indications for Jerusalem are better."]},{"name":"The visions and the discovery","minutes":1,"stand":"Stand to one side of the path with a view of the whole house.","paras":["How was this house found? Through the visions of a German nun, Anne Catherine Emmerich, who lived from 1774 to 1824. Bedridden in Germany, she described the house John had built for Mary near Ephesus.","The poet Clemens Brentano wrote them down from his visits with her, and his book came out in 1852. Neither of them had ever been to Ephesus.","Later scholars found maps and travel guides among Brentano's papers that could have been used to embellish her accounts, and a Vatican official called the writings Brentano's artistic fantasy, which played no part in her beatification. Pope John Paul II beatified her in 2004.","Then came the search. On October 18, 1881, a French priest, Julien Gouyet, followed the book and found a small stone building on this mountain. Few took him seriously. Ten years later, on July 29, 1891, two missionary priests, urged on by Sister Marie de Mandat-Grancey, found it again.","And here's the part that moves me. They learned that villagers from Şirince had honored this roofless ruin for a long time. They called it Panaya Kapulu, the Doorway to the Virgin, and every year on August 15 they came here on pilgrimage."]},{"name":"Inside the chapel","minutes":1,"stand":"Outside the chapel door, before you join the line to go in.","paras":["When you go in, you'll find one large room, with an altar and a statue of Mary in the center. The smaller room on the right is the one tradition says was Mary's bedroom. It's small inside, so keep moving gently with the line.","Once you're back outside, look along the walls for a line painted in red. It separates the restored part of the building from the original remains."]},{"name":"The wishing wall","minutes":1,"stand":"Outside the shrine, at the wall covered in paper and cloth.","paras":["This is the wishing wall. Pilgrims tie their prayers to it on paper and cloth. Nearby is the fountain; some pilgrims believe its water heals.","What does the Church say? The Catholic Church has never ruled on whether this is really Mary's house, for lack of scientifically acceptable evidence. But in 1951 Pope Pius XII declared it a Holy Place, and three popes have come here: Paul VI in 1967, John Paul II in 1979, and Benedict XVI in 2006. Every August 15 a liturgy is held here for the Assumption, and Muslims come to honor Mary as well.","So nobody knows for certain that Mary lived here. But look at this wall. Strangers from everywhere, of different faiths, have left a prayer for someone they love. Behold thy mother, Jesus said. People still come here to be looked after."]}] },
  { id: "kusadasi-pigeon", v: "1-390abe", city: /Kusadasi|Kuşadası|Ephesus|Selcuk|Selçuk|Izmir/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(pigeon island|guvercinada|guvercin adasi)\b/i, title: "Pigeon Island Castle", where: "Pigeon Island Castle", checked: "2026-11-22",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Kuşadası","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ku%C5%9Fadas%C4%B1","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Kuşadası","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Ku%C5%9Fadas%C4%B1","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"The causeway","minutes":1,"stand":"At the town end of the causeway, looking out toward the island and its walls.","paras":["Ahead of you is Pigeon Island, Güvercinada in Turkish, joined to the town by a causeway about 200 meters long. This little island gave the whole town its name. Kuşadası means bird island, because from the sea the island has the shape of a bird's head.","In ancient times this was a minor port, overshadowed by Ephesus, until Ephesus lost its harbor to silt.","The causeway is open to the sea and the wind. Hold on to hats and little hands."]},{"name":"The castle","minutes":1,"stand":"On the island, in front of the castle walls.","paras":["When Byzantine, Venetian and Genoese traders worked this coast, they re-founded the port as Scala Nova, the New Port, and put a garrison here on the island. In 1834 the castle and garrison were rebuilt and expanded, and the island became the focus of the town, so much so that in time people started calling the whole place Kuşadası.","Today the castle has been restored as a museum. Who first built walls here, and when, we don't know."]},{"name":"The view back","minutes":1,"stand":"Find a safe spot on the island where you can look back across the bay toward the harbor, well away from the rocks at the water's edge.","paras":["Offshore, about 16 kilometers from this coast, lies the Greek island of Samos.","Back near the docks is another old strongbox of a building, the Öküz Mehmed Pasha Caravanserai, built in 1618 to keep sailors' goods safe.","Stand here a minute with the town in front of you. A whole town took its name from this small island. Sometimes the little place at the edge is the one people remember."]}] },
  { id: "kusadasi-stjohn", v: "1-41cd3d", city: /Kusadasi|Kuşadası|Ephesus|Selcuk|Selçuk|Izmir/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(saint john|st john|st\. john|ayasuluk|isa bey|selcuk castle)\b/i, title: "Basilica of Saint John", where: "Basilica of Saint John", checked: "2026-11-22",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Basilica of Saint John the Theologian","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basilica_of_St._John","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Ayasuluk Hill","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ayasuluk_Castle","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Selçuk","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sel%C3%A7uk","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"King James Version (public domain), via bible-api.com","title":"","url":"https://bible-api.com/john%2019%3A25-27?translation=kjv","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"John the Apostle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_the_Apostle","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Ephesus","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ephesus","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Selçuk","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Sel%C3%A7uk","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","title":"Ephesus - UNESCO World Heritage Centre","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1018/","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"İsa Bey Mosque","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C4%B0sa_Bey_Mosque","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Arrival on the hill","minutes":1,"stand":"Just inside the entrance, where you can look along the ruins of the basilica.","paras":["You're standing on Ayasuluk Hill, among the ruins of the Basilica of Saint John, just below the old fortress. This was the church built over the place where, by tradition, the apostle John was buried.","This hill is much older than the church. People lived up here in the Bronze Age, and archaeologists think it may have been Apasa, the capital of a kingdom called Arzawa. Today it's part of the Ephesus World Heritage Site.","Even the name tells the story. Medieval Greeks called the hill Theologos, after Saint John the Theologian, and over the centuries that wore down to Ayasuluk.","The ground here is uneven and there's little shade. Watch your step among the fallen stones."]},{"name":"John and Mary","minutes":1,"stand":"Walk on through the church to the place of the altar, where the tomb is.","paras":["Here is the tomb. First, what the Bible says. The Gospel of John tells how there stood by the cross of Jesus his mother. When Jesus therefore saw his mother, and the disciple standing by, whom he loved, he saith unto his mother, Woman, behold thy son! Then saith he to the disciple, Behold thy mother! And from that hour that disciple took her unto his own home.","Notice what the Bible does not say. It doesn't name that disciple, and it doesn't mention Ephesus. Many traditions identify him as John, but scholars debate it.","Now tradition. The early church writer Irenaeus said John stayed with the church of Ephesus until the times of the emperor Trajan. John is said to have died here of natural causes, sometime after AD 98, the only apostle who wasn't martyred. Modern scholars are divided on how much of that to accept.","In the great church, the tomb lay in a crypt directly beneath the altar. Words from Psalm 132 were inscribed on the altar: This is my resting place forever, here will I dwell."]},{"name":"Justinian's church","minutes":1,"stand":"Turn back to face down the length of the church, toward the way you came in.","paras":["Picture this whole space roofed over. Over the grave that tradition gives to John, the emperor Justinian built this church in the 6th century, finished by 565, modeled on the Church of the Holy Apostles in Constantinople. It measured 428 by 213 feet, laid out as a cross and roofed with domes.","He wasn't the first to build here. A small mausoleum that served as a church came first, then a bigger basilica. When that one was built, the sources differ: one account says the 4th century, another the 5th.","The historian Procopius called it the most holy sanctuary there, held in very high honour. And the main entrance had a striking name: the Gate of Persecution.","Look for the baptistery, a large eight-sided room on the north side of the church. If you can't spot it, ask the staff."]},{"name":"Legends of the tomb","minutes":1,"stand":"Stay near the tomb.","paras":["The tomb gathered legends. It was said that John was not dead but sleeping beneath it, and that each time he breathed, the dust around the altar stirred. That dust, called manna, was said to cure the sick, and the miracle came every year on 8 May.","Pilgrims carried the dust home in little flasks stamped with the saint's image.","What's really in the tomb? Nobody knows for sure. One account says that when it was opened in Constantine the Great's time it held no bones. Another says the relics were moved to Constantinople in the 6th century, and a Greek archaeologist who dug here last century found the grave empty."]},{"name":"The fortress and the later centuries","minutes":1,"stand":"Climb to the higher ground near the fortress walls, if it's open, and look back down over the church.","paras":["Above us is the fortress. It was built after the Arab invasions of the 7th century to protect the basilica, and later Seljuk and Ottoman rulers restored it.","In the early fourteenth century Turkish rulers took the area, and the church was turned into a mosque; accounts differ on exactly who and when. Then, sources say, an invasion led by Timur destroyed it in 1402. Parts of the church were rebuilt after 1923, with money from religious foundations in the United States. Pope Paul VI came here, and prayed.","From up here, think about the plain below. UNESCO notes that the great Temple of Artemis drew pilgrims from all over the Mediterranean, until in the 5th century Christian pilgrimage to the Church of Mary and this basilica eclipsed it."]},{"name":"The İsa Bey Mosque","minutes":1,"stand":"Walk to the mosque next door, and stand facing its western wall.","paras":["Right next to the basilica is the İsa Bey Mosque, built in 1374 and 1375 for Isa Bey, a ruler of the Aydinid family. Its plan is based on the Great Mosque of Damascus.","Look at this western wall, faced in marble and engraved with inscriptions and geometric shapes. If you go inside, look up at the rims of the domes, decorated with İznik tiles. The brick minaret has lost its top, above the balcony.","The mosque was in ruins by 1829, and its minaret fell by 1842. For a time it was even used as a caravanserai, an inn for travelers. It was rebuilt in 1975. UNESCO sees the mosque and the medieval town on this hill as marking the arrival of the Seljuk and Ottoman Turks.","So many centuries on one small hill. Before you go, take a moment with the words from the cross: Behold thy mother. Whatever else happened here, it began with someone being asked to take care of someone else."]}] },
  { id: "messina-annunziata", v: "1-c569eb", city: /Messina|Taormina/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(annunziata dei catalani|chiesa dei catalani|santissima annunziata dei catalani)\b/i, title: "Santissima Annunziata dei Catalani", where: "Santissima Annunziata dei Catalani", checked: "2026-11-23",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Church of the Santissima Annunziata dei Catalani","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Santissima_Annunziata_dei_Catalani","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Messina","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Messina","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Messina","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Messina","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Arrival","minutes":1,"stand":"On the street above the church, looking down at it.","paras":["Look down. This small church is Santissima Annunziata dei Catalani. Because of the earthquake of 1908, it now sits 3 meters below the rebuilt street. And it's one of the few buildings in Messina that came through that earthquake at all.","If you go down to it, go slowly and watch your footing.","It's a Norman church. The Normans held Messina from 1060, and the church is usually dated to the 1100s; one listing says the late 1100s into the 1200s, so sources differ slightly.","And the ground under it may be older still. It is said to stand on the ruins of an older temple of Neptune."]},{"name":"A mix of worlds","minutes":1,"stand":"Wherever you get the clearest view of the church walls.","paras":["What makes it special is the mix. It's Sicilian Norman architecture, with influences from Arab and Byzantine building, and Roman elements too.","Look for the apse. That's the part people single out, because it's unusually well preserved.","Why such a mix? Think of who had ruled here before the Normans came. Messina was Byzantine from 535, then, in the Islamic period, under the Aghlabids and the Fatimids, then Byzantine again from 1038. That's the mix you see in the walls."]},{"name":"The name","minutes":1,"stand":"Stay where you are.","paras":["The name tells its own story. It used to be called the Annunziata di Castellammare, after a medieval fortress nearby that guarded the entrance to the port and the shipyard.","Its later name, dei Catalani, comes from merchants from Catalonia, who set up in Messina in the 1500s.","One more Catalan echo in Messina: in the days before August 15, two giant statues on horseback, Mata and Grifone, are paraded through the streets. In legend they're the founders of the city, and the giants come from the old Catalan tradition of processional giants.","The city around you was rebuilt from 1912, mostly in the Liberty style. This little church is far older than all of it. It simply stayed where it was, three meters down, while Messina rose up around it."]}] },
  { id: "messina-duomo-me", v: "1-332b03", city: /Messina|Taormina/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(duomo di messina|messina cathedral|astronomical clock of messina|messina astronomical clock|fontana di orione|fountain of orion)\b/i, title: "Messina Cathedral and the Astronomical Clock", where: "Messina Cathedral and the Astronomical Clock", checked: "2026-11-23",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Messina Cathedral","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Messina_Cathedral","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"1908 Messina earthquake","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1908_Messina_earthquake","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Orion (mythology)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fountain_of_Orion","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Messina","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Messina","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Messina","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Messina","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Messina astronomical clock","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Astronomical_clock_of_Messina","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Reggio Calabria","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reggio_Calabria","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"King James Version (public domain), via bible-api.com","title":"","url":"https://bible-api.com/acts%2028%3A11-14?translation=kjv","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Arrival","minutes":1,"stand":"In the cathedral square, facing the front of the cathedral.","paras":["This is Messina Cathedral, built by the Normans and consecrated in 1197. But most of what you see was rebuilt in the twentieth century, after the earthquake of 1908 and the bombs of World War Two.","Its full name is the cathedral of Santa Maria Assunta. The consecration was a royal occasion: Archbishop Berardo led it, and the Emperor Henry the Sixth and Constance of Sicily were there to watch.","So why does a cathedral that old look so new? Hold on to that question. This square is a story of things knocked down and stubbornly put back up."]},{"name":"The Fountain of Orion","minutes":1,"stand":"Walk over to the big fountain beside the cathedral, in the same square.","paras":["This is the Fountain of Orion. The sculptor Montorsoli built it in 1547, and the giant hunter Orion stands at its heart.","Why Orion? The story goes that he helped Zanclus, the founder of the city, by building the promontory that forms the harbor. So here he is, with his dog Sirius, as one of Messina's legendary founders.","The harbor gave the city its first name, too. It was Zancle, and Thucydides said the word meant scythe, from the curved shape of the natural port.","The design came from Francesco Maurolico, a Renaissance historian and mathematician from Messina. In his plan, Orion stands for the Emperor Charles the Fifth, a master of the sea and the restorer of Messina. Maurolico also claimed to have identified the remains of a temple of Orion near this cathedral. One guidebook adds that Montorsoli had studied under Michelangelo."]},{"name":"The night of 1908","minutes":1,"stand":"Turn back to face the front of the cathedral.","paras":["Early in the morning of December 28, 1908, an earthquake hit the strait. Around 120,000 people died. Of this cathedral, only the outer walls, the Gothic portal and an apse were left standing.","It came at twenty past five, and the ground shook for 37 seconds. Most people were asleep, and their houses fell on them. The city was even fuller than usual: visitors had come in for Verdi's opera Aida, staged the evening before.","Then, about ten minutes later, the sea pulled back from both shores of the strait, and a tsunami twelve meters high swept in. Here in the city and its suburbs, some 75,000 people were killed, and at least 91 percent of the buildings were destroyed or damaged beyond repair.","The rebuilt cathedral had one more ordeal to come. In 1943, incendiary bombs fell on the restored roof and destroyed much of the interior. Only an original mosaic and a statue survived.","A small mercy: the tower also has two older bells, cast by Michael Salicula in 1400. They were saved from the earthquake of 1908."]},{"name":"The clock tower","minutes":2,"stand":"Step back into the square and face the side of the bell tower that looks onto the square.","paras":["This bell tower holds Messina's astronomical clock, built by the Ungerer company of Strasbourg in 1933. It's considered the largest and most complex of its kind in the world.","The tower is new as well. It was rebuilt on its old site after 1908, to the original design, and it rises over 65 meters. Archbishop Angelo Paino ordered the clock to mark that rebuilding. Perhaps the idea came from Pope Pius the Eleventh, who had given him a working model of the famous clock in Strasbourg.","Let's read this side from the bottom up. Near the bottom is the carousel of the days of the week: each day has its classical god in a chariot pulled by an animal. On Sunday it's Apollo with a horse; on Wednesday, Mercury with a panther.","Above that, the ages of life: a child, a young man, a warrior and an old man. Every quarter hour one gives way to the next, passing in front of Death, a skeleton waving a scythe.","Next comes the sanctuary of the Madonna of Montalto. At midday a dove flies in a circle and the little church rises from the ground, recalling how the sanctuary was founded, back in the 1200s.","Then a Bible scene that changes with the church year. From Christmas to Epiphany, the shepherds bow before the infant Jesus. From Easter to Pentecost, two soldiers keep watch at the tomb, and Jesus rises.","Higher still are two local heroines, Dina and Clarenza. Legend says that during the Sicilian Vespers, in 1282, Dina drove off an attack by rolling rocks, while Clarenza rang the bells to warn the city. Up there, they ring the quarter hours.","That's a lot of looking up. Step back far enough to see the top without craning, and keep a hand on your bag when the square fills up."]},{"name":"The sky side","minutes":1,"stand":"Walk around to the side of the bell tower that faces the cathedral.","paras":["This side is about the sky: a calendar dial below, a planetarium above, and a turning sphere that shows the phase of the moon.","On the calendar, a marble angel points an arrow at the date, and moving panels mark the feasts that shift from year to year.","In the planetarium, the Sun sits at the center and the planets circle it through the signs of the zodiac. The moon above is half gold and half black, 1.2 meters across."]},{"name":"Noon, and the letter","minutes":2,"stand":"Go back into the square and face the bell tower again.","paras":["At midday the tower comes alive. The lion at the top waves its flag and roars three times, and the rooster crows three times.","Watch for the Madonna of the Letter, the patron of Messina. Legend says the city sent ambassadors to Jerusalem to honor the Virgin Mary, and she sent back a letter blessing the city. At noon an angel hands her the letter, and Saint Paul and the ambassadors pass by and bow.","Tradition even gives the letter a date, 42 AD, and its words: We bless you and your city. Out at the tip of the sickle-shaped harbor, a tall gilded statue of the Madonna of the Letter stands on top of the tower of Forte San Salvatore.","Now let me keep Scripture and tradition apart. The Bible does reach this strait. Across the water is Reggio Calabria, which in Latin was Rhegium, and the apostle Paul passed through it on his last voyage toward Rome. Acts, chapter twenty-eight, verse thirteen, puts it like this: \"And from thence we fetched a compass, and came to Rhegium: and after one day the south wind blew, and we came the next day to Puteoli.\"","Notice that Acts names Rhegium, not Messina. The story of the letter and the ambassadors is Messina's own legend, and the clock tells it, with Paul in the procession.","This city has been shaken, flooded and bombed. And still, every day at noon, a lion on a rebuilt tower lifts a flag and roars. A city that keeps on saying it's alive."]}] },
  { id: "messina-museo-me", v: "1-7425f6", city: /Messina|Taormina/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(museo regionale di messina|regional museum of messina|museo regionale interdisciplinare|mume)\b/i, title: "Regional Museum of Messina", where: "Regional Museum of Messina", checked: "2026-11-23",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Regional Museum of Messina","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Regional_Museum_of_Messina","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Antonello da Messina","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antonello_da_Messina","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Messina","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Messina","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Scylla","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scylla","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Caravaggio","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caravaggio","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Arrival","minutes":1,"stand":"Outside the museum entrance, before you go in.","paras":["This is the Regional Museum of Messina. It tells the story of art in this city from the 1100s to the 1700s, and much of it was pulled from the ruins of the 1908 earthquake.","The museum itself is a survivor. It first opened in 1806, to end, as the phrase went, the despoliation of art. In 1908 the building collapsed in the earthquake, and some works were lost. The major pieces were brought indoors; the rest waited in makeshift shelters and out on the open esplanade.","Then came the long wait for a proper home. Until 2017 the collection lived in a former silk mill. Building work began in 1984, and the museum only opened fully on June 17, 2017. Along the way, in 1951, someone discovered that paintings had been stolen and replaced with fakes.","It's a big place. Take your time, and sit down whenever a painting asks you to."]},{"name":"The old city in pieces","minutes":1,"stand":"In the medieval galleries; ask the staff if you can't find them.","paras":["A lot of what's here is old Messina in pieces: paintings, sculpture and decoration from buildings the earthquake damaged or destroyed.","Look for the painted wooden beams from the cathedral ceiling, from the 1200s and 1300s. Their scenes include warrior angels, Saint George, and Jonah coming out of the whale's mouth.","And look for the colored marble inlay panels, gathered here from the city's churches after 1908. Centuries earlier, Messina was probably the first Sicilian city to host Byzantine artisans skilled in this kind of inlay.","Before we get to Antonello, a word of warning. Wikipedia reports that on August 15, 2026, three panels of Antonello da Messina's San Gregorio Polyptych, and another panel attributed to him, were stolen from this museum overnight. We don't know whether they've come back, so ask the staff what's on display today.","The museum's director at the time, Marisa Mercurio, called it a great loss for the museum, for the city, the community and the art world."]},{"name":"Antonello da Messina","minutes":1,"stand":"In the Antonello room; ask the staff if you can't find it.","paras":["Antonello da Messina is this city's own painter of the early Renaissance, born here around 1429 to 1431. His San Gregorio Polyptych, from 1473, is the Antonello this museum is known for, though some of its panels were reported stolen in 2026, so you may find gaps.","His work shows a strong influence from Netherlandish painting, though there's no record that he ever traveled beyond Italy. The old biographer Vasari even credited him with bringing oil painting to Italy, but historians now regard that as wrong.","He was also a pioneer of the portrait. The art historian John Pope-Hennessy called him the first Italian painter for whom the individual portrait was an art form in its own right.","He spent 1475 and 1476 in Venice, and his work influenced painters there. Then he came home, and he died here in Messina in 1479. His son Jacobello finished some of his last works."]},{"name":"Neptune and Scylla","minutes":1,"stand":"At Montorsoli's Scylla; ask the staff where it's shown.","paras":["This Scylla is by Montorsoli, the sculptor of the fountains by the cathedral and on Via Garibaldi. The museum also lists the original of his Neptune fountain, so look for that too.","Scylla belongs to the strait outside. In Greek myth she's a man-eating monster living on one side of a narrow channel, opposite the sea-swallowing monster Charybdis. Virgil, in the Aeneid, linked that channel with the Strait of Messina.","In the myth as Homer's Odyssey tells it, Odysseus gets through the strait, but Scylla snatches six of his sailors off the deck. Out on Via Garibaldi, the fountain still shows Neptune with Scylla and Charybdis."]},{"name":"Caravaggio","minutes":1,"stand":"In the Caravaggio room.","paras":["Here are two paintings by Caravaggio from his time in Sicily: the Raising of Lazarus and the Adoration of the Shepherds.","He came here a man on the run. In Rome he had killed Ranuccio Tomassoni in a brawl and been sentenced to death. He fled to Naples, then Malta, then Sicily, hoping for a pardon from the pope.","With his old friend Mario Minniti, he made what amounted to a triumphal tour from Syracuse to Messina, winning prestigious, well-paid commissions. Look at how he paints: his great Sicilian altarpieces isolate their shadowy, pitifully poor figures in vast areas of darkness.","In the Lazarus, look at the body. It's still stiff with death, but the hand, turned toward Christ's, is alive.","He wasn't easy company. Reports from the time describe him sleeping fully armed and in his clothes, ripping up a painting at a slight word of criticism, and mocking local painters. After only nine months in Sicily he went back to Naples, in the late summer of 1609. He died the next year, and how he died is still uncertain."]},{"name":"Before you leave","minutes":1,"stand":"Anywhere quiet before the exit.","paras":["Keep an eye out for two paintings that echo the cathedral square: Mattia Preti's Madonna of the Letter, and a painting called Orion, Founder of Messina.","So much in this building was dug out of rubble, sheltered, moved and, more than once, stolen. What's on these walls is here because people kept digging it out and putting it back."]}] },
  { id: "messina-taormina", v: "1-c18bd5", city: /Messina|Taormina/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(taormina)\b/i, title: "Taormina", where: "Taormina", checked: "2026-11-23",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Taormina","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taormina","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Taormina","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Taormina","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Ancient theatre of Taormina","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ancient_theatre_of_Taormina","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","title":"Mount Etna - UNESCO World Heritage Centre","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1427/","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Mount Etna","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Etna","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Isola Bella (Sicily)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isola_Bella_(Sicily)","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Arrival","minutes":1,"stand":"On the main street of the old town, somewhere out of the flow of people.","paras":["Welcome to Taormina. The old town sits roughly 200 to 250 meters above the sea, on Sicily's east coast, and people have come here as tourists since the 1800s. You'll soon see why.","Its history reaches back before the first Greek colony on Sicily, in 734 BC. The Greeks called it Tauromenion; in Latin it became Tauromenium.","After Italy was unified, well-off travelers from northern Europe found it, and it became known as a welcoming haven for gay men and artists.","Its patron is Saint Pancras. Tradition says Saint Peter sent him to Sicily in AD 40 as the first bishop here; that's tradition, not something the Bible tells. And on his feast day, July 9, 1943, with the German command headquartered in town, Allied planes bombed Taormina twice."]},{"name":"The ancient theatre","minutes":2,"stand":"High up in the theatre's seating, facing the stage.","paras":["This is the ancient theatre. A Greek theatre stood here from around the third century BC, and the Romans rebuilt it. It's the second largest of its kind in Sicily, after the one at Syracuse.","Careful on the old steps and stone. And in July and August it can get very hot and busy, so carry water and look for shade.","So how Greek is it? Most of what stands is brick, so it's probably Roman work, laid out on a Greek plan. The Roman rebuilding probably came around the time of the emperors Trajan or Hadrian.","Look at the stage end. The back wall of the stage building survives, which is rare: in most ancient theatres only traces are left. Most of the original seats are gone, but the wall around the seating is still there.","It's big. One source gives its diameter as 107 meters, another as 109; sources differ. It could hold around 10,000 people.","In the third century AD the Romans turned the orchestra into an arena and took the stage out, though the wall behind it still stands. Fragments of the decoration show it was richly ornamented, in the Corinthian style. And it's still a stage today: opera, plays and concerts, and every year the Taormina Film Festival."]},{"name":"Etna from the square","minutes":1,"stand":"On Piazza IX Aprile, at the balcony, looking out to sea.","paras":["From this balcony you look out over the sea and, on a clear day, to Mount Etna in the south. UNESCO calls it the most active stratovolcano in the world.","Its height was given as 3,403 meters in September 2024, but that changes with eruptions at the summit. Its eruptive history goes back 500,000 years, and people have recorded its activity for at least 2,700 years.","The ancients had their own explanations. In myth, the blacksmith god Hephaestus, Vulcan to the Romans, kept his forge under the mountain, and Zeus trapped the monster Typhon beneath it.","Wikipedia records that a new eruptive phase began at the Voragine crater on August 6, 2026, with lava fountains and ash that cancelled or rerouted hundreds of flights at Catania. Etna joined UNESCO's World Heritage list in June 2013."]},{"name":"Isola Bella","minutes":1,"stand":"At a terrace where you can look down at the coast just south of town.","paras":["Down below is Isola Bella, a small island just south of Taormina, called the Pearl of the Ionian Sea. A narrow path often links it to the beach.","King Ferdinand gave the island to Taormina in 1806. In 1890 Florence Trevelyan, an English conservationist, bought it, built a small house facing the sea, and brought in exotic plants, which thrived.","She left her mark up here too: the town's public gardens, the Parco Duca di Cesarò, are her creation. She settled and married here, and she's buried in nearby Castelmola.","In 1990, after a later owner went bankrupt, the Region of Sicily bought the island and made it a nature reserve. Keep an eye on the sky: the reserve's wildlife includes the peregrine falcon. If you go down, there's a cable car to the beaches, but the beach here is sharp volcanic shingle.","People have looked out from this hill since before the first Greek colony on Sicily: Greeks, Romans, a long parade of travelers, and now you. The view that drew all of them is the one in front of you."]}] },
  { id: "mykonos-chora", v: "1-dae7d3", city: /Mykonos/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(chora|mykonos town|manto|mavrogenous|petros|tria pigadia)\b/i, title: "Mykonos Town (Chora)", where: "Mykonos Town (Chora)", checked: "2026-11-22",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Mykonos","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mykonos","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Mykonos","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Mykonos","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Manto Mavrogenous","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manto_Mavrogenous","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Petros (pelican)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Petros_the_pelican","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"The harbor square","minutes":2,"stand":"In Manto Square on the harbor front, facing her bust.","paras":["Welcome to Mykonos Town. Islanders call it Chora, which simply means the Town. And the larger-than-life bust in this square is the woman this square is named for: Manto Mavrogenous, a heroine of the Greek War of Independence. She was extremely wealthy, and she gave her fortune to the cause.","A little background first. For almost three centuries, from 1537 to 1830, Mykonos was part of the Ottoman Empire. When the Greek Revolution broke out in 1821, this island played an important role, and Manto led it.","She was born in Trieste, in what's now Italy, and grew up educated: ancient Greek philosophy and history, and fluent French, Italian and Turkish. When the fighting began, she came to Mykonos, the island of her origin, and asked its leaders to join.","Then she paid for it. At her own expense she equipped and manned two ships to chase the pirates raiding these islands. And on the 22nd of October 1822, under her leadership, the Mykonians drove off Ottoman troops who had landed here.","It cost her more than money. She sold some of her jewelry to equip 200 men. And she was despised even by her own mother for the choices she made.","After the war, Ioannis Kapodistrias gave her the rank of Lieutenant General. Greece became independent in 1830. She died on Paros in July 1848. And from 1988 to 2001, her face was on the two-drachma coin.","This island saw war again in living memory. In September 1944, Greek commandos of the Sacred Squadron raided the German garrison. The remaining German troops surrendered, and the island was free on the 30th of September."]},{"name":"Looking for Petros","minutes":1,"stand":"Stroll along the harbor front, with the water beside you.","paras":["Keep an eye out along the water. Mykonos has a mascot, a pelican called Petros, and today's Petros can sometimes be found at the waterfront, or even up in town.","The first Petros was a great white pelican. Accounts differ on when he arrived: one says he settled here after a storm in 1954, another that a local fisherman found him wounded off the coast in 1958. Either way, he was nursed back to health, the locals looked after him, and the name Petros started as a joke among them.","Sadly, he was hit and killed by a car in December 1985. Afterwards three new pelicans settled around town, and one of them was given his name. So the Petros you meet today is carrying on a title.","If you do meet him, enjoy the moment, but give him space."]},{"name":"Into the lanes","minutes":2,"stand":"Turn away from the water and step into the narrow lanes behind the harbor.","paras":["Now the town itself: a maze of tiny whitewashed streets, houses and churches, wrapped around the harbor. Cars aren't allowed in here, so this is a place for walking, and for getting a little lost.","A quick word of care. The lanes are narrow, there are steps where you don't expect them, and the paving can be slick. In the midday heat, take the shady side and keep some water with you.","Notice how many little churches you pass. Wikivoyage counts about 400 on the island. The usual explanation is that for many years islanders had to build a church on their land before they could build a house.","The town has grown busy and stylish, but look up from the shop windows and the old Cycladic style is still here, white walls and all.","Some history in these walls. Mykonos came under direct Venetian rule in 1390. Barbarossa's Ottoman fleet attacked in 1537. And right up to the end of the 18th century this was a trading town, drawing newcomers from nearby islands, and drawing pirates too, raid after raid. In June 1794, British and French ships even fought a battle in the island's main harbor.","Even the name is a puzzle, and nobody knows for sure. One theory says it comes from an ancient Greek word that roughly means a pile of stones. Another ties it to a mythological hero named Mykonos. And myth said the great battle between Zeus and the Giants was fought here, and that the big rocks all over the island are the giants themselves, turned to stone."]},{"name":"The three wells","minutes":1,"stand":"In the middle of town, beside the three wells standing in a row.","paras":["These three matching wells are Tria Pigadia. They were built in 1722 to give the town its water.","They're shallow, only five or six meters deep, because they were dug into sand, where the water was easier to reach.","So as you wander on, remember what's said about the churches here: a church first, then a house. True or not, there are a great many of them. If a door is open, step inside for a quiet minute, away from the crowds."]}] },
  { id: "mykonos-delos", v: "1-214ddb", city: /Mykonos/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(delos)\b/i, title: "Delos", where: "Delos", checked: "2026-11-22",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Delos","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Delos","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","title":"Delos - UNESCO World Heritage Centre","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/530/","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Delos","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Delos","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Delian League","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Delian_League","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Archaeological Museum of Delos","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Archaeological_Museum_of_Delos","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Leto","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leto","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"The Sacred Harbor","minutes":1,"stand":"On the quay where the boat lands, facing inland toward the ruins.","paras":["Welcome to Delos. It's a small island, but it's one of the most important places in all of Greek myth and history. Today the entire island is an archaeological site.","Before we walk: there's no protection from the sun out here, and the ground is rough, ancient stone. Wear a hat, watch your feet, and drink plenty of water as you go.","Nobody lives here now. Overnight stays are forbidden, and the only residents are security staff and archaeologists. UNESCO added Delos to the World Heritage List in 1990. Digging began in 1872, and it's still going on."]},{"name":"The Sanctuary of Apollo","minutes":1,"stand":"Walk inland from the harbor into the Sanctuary of Apollo, among the temple foundations.","paras":["This was Apollo's sanctuary. It drew pilgrims from all over Greece, and around it Delos grew into a prosperous trading port.","The sanctuary was here at least from the 9th century BC, and it was at its height in the Archaic and Classical periods.","Look beside the Temple of the Delians. A colossal statue of Apollo once stood there, from the 6th century BC. Parts of the upper body and hips still lie on the spot. One hand is in the island's museum. A foot is in the British Museum.","This is also where the Delian League began, in 478 BC: an alliance of Greek cities, led by Athens, to keep fighting Persia. Its congresses met in the sanctuary of Apollo's temple. Members dropped lumps of iron into the sea, a sign that the alliance was meant to be permanent.","The league's treasury was kept here until 454 BC, when Pericles moved it to Athens. Athens spent the money on its navy, and on art and buildings for the city.","Delos was also made ritually pure. In the fifth century BC the whole island was cleared of the dead, and a decree said no one could be buried here, or give birth here."]},{"name":"The Terrace of the Lions","minutes":1,"stand":"At the Terrace of the Lions, facing the row of marble lions.","paras":["Here are the famous lions of Delos, given to Apollo by the people of Naxos shortly before 600 BC. What stands here now are exact copies; the originals are in the museum.","There were once nine to twelve of these crouching, snarling marble guardians along the Sacred Way. To protect the marble from the north wind, the originals were moved indoors. Only seven of the original lions survive.","Picture the whole row, like an Egyptian avenue of sphinxes. One of the missing lions was carried off, and today it stands over the main gate of the Venetian Arsenal, in Venice."]},{"name":"The Sacred Lake","minutes":2,"stand":"Beside the round, dry hollow of the Sacred Lake.","paras":["This round hollow was the Sacred Lake. Near this lake, myth says, Leto labored before giving birth to the twins Apollo and Artemis.","The myth goes like this. Leto was pregnant by Zeus, and Zeus's wife Hera was jealous. Hera ordered every land on earth to turn Leto away. But Delos, the story goes, wasn't joined to the mainland or fixed to the sea floor. It didn't count as land. So it could take her in.","For nine days and nine nights, the myth says, she suffered near this lake. The goddess of childbirth, Eileithyia, was not there. Unaware, she was up on Olympus with Hera. On the ninth day the messenger goddess Iris won her over with a necklace and brought her here.","According to a Homeric hymn, the island had been floating until then, and now it was anchored to the ocean floor. The newborn Apollo threw off his swaddling clothes, bathed the universe in light, and began walking with his lyre and his bow.","Like most myths, this one comes in versions. In some, Artemis was born first, on another island called Ortygia. One local legend even put Apollo's birth in Boeotia, on the mainland.","Why is it dry? The caretakers keep it that way on purpose, to stop malaria mosquitoes from breeding."]},{"name":"The museum","minutes":1,"stand":"Inside the site museum, if it's open, in front of the original lions.","paras":["Here, out of the wind, are the original Naxian lions.","Not everything found on Delos is here, though. A large share of the finds is in the National Archaeological Museum in Athens."]},{"name":"The merchant town","minutes":1,"stand":"Among the walls of the ancient houses.","paras":["Now the other Delos: a boom town. After 167 BC Delos became a free port, and the trade of the eastern Mediterranean poured in. An ancient writer called it the greatest commercial center of the whole world.","Rich merchants, bankers and ship-owners settled here and built luxurious houses, decorated with frescoes and mosaic floors. Look for the House of Dionysus, named for its mosaic of the god riding a panther, and the House of the Dolphins, with its mosaic of erotes riding dolphins.","There is a darker side, and we should say it plainly. Roman traders came here to buy tens of thousands of slaves. Delos became the center of the slave trade, with the largest slave market in the region.","People came from everywhere, and they brought their gods: temples to Sarapis, Isis and Anubis, to Syrian gods, and even a synagogue near the stadium. Nobody's sure about that building; its ruins were once believed to be a Samaritan synagogue. Separately, inscriptions record a community calling itself the Israelites on Delos. And an inscription honoring Herod the Great, King of Judaea, was found here."]},{"name":"The end of Delos, and the view","minutes":1,"stand":"Wherever you can look back over the ruins to the sea, or on Mount Kynthos if you climb it.","paras":["Its wealth, and its friendship with Rome, made Delos a target. It was attacked and looted twice: in 88 BC by Mithridates, King of Pontus, and in 69 BC by pirates allied with him. After that it declined, and was slowly abandoned.","Later the island became a quarry, its temple columns burnt for lime. The last people left somewhere around the 7th or 8th century AD; the sources differ.","If you have the time and the legs for it, climb Mount Kynthos. It's crowned with a sanctuary of Zeus, and from the top you get a panoramic view of the islands around you. The Cyclades took their name from the rough circle they form around Delos.","The path up can be rough, and there's no shade. Go slowly, and watch your footing on the way down.","The ancient Greeks had a saying for someone happy and light-hearted: you sing as if sailing into Delos. People still make this crossing today. I hope you sail home singing."]}] },
  { id: "mykonos-littlevenice", v: "1-f19f98", city: /Mykonos/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(little venice|alefkandra)\b/i, title: "Little Venice", where: "Little Venice", checked: "2026-11-22",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Mykonos","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mykonos","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Mykonos","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Mykonos","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Mykonos windmills","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Windmills_of_Mykonos","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"On the waterfront","minutes":1,"stand":"At the water's edge in Little Venice, looking along the row of houses.","paras":["This is Little Venice, also called Alefkandra, at the far western edge of town. Look at the houses: rows of them right on the waterfront, with their balconies hanging over the sea.","Mind your step along the edge. When the wind is up, waves can splash over, and wet stone gets slippery.","They're two and three stories high and stand like a wall above the sea. Pick out the colorful wooden balconies.","How old are they? The first went up in the mid-18th century, though you'll see them loosely called medieval."]},{"name":"Captains and pirates","minutes":1,"stand":"Stay where you can see the foot of the houses, where the walls meet the water.","paras":["These houses first belonged to rich merchants and sea captains. Down at the bottom, little doors opened straight onto the sea and into storage below.","And that started a story. With doors like that, the story goes, people believed the owners were secretly pirates. It's a rumor, not a record, but you can see how it began.","It wasn't a wild idea for its time. Up to the end of the 18th century, Mykonos was a thriving trading town, and pirates raided it again and again."]},{"name":"Sunset row","minutes":1,"stand":"Turn and look back up the hill toward the windmills.","paras":["Up on the hill above the harbor stand the windmills. Today some of these old houses are bars, cafes, little shops and galleries.","This is said to be one of the most romantic spots on the island. In the evening, people gather here to watch the sunset, and artists come to paint this coastline.","The waterfront wasn't always this lively. The island's economy faded in the late 19th century, especially after the Corinth Canal opened, and many Mykonians left to find work, many of them in the United States. Then the visitors came. In the 1960s, the international jet set discovered Mykonos, and by the 2000s it was one of the most expensive islands in Greece.","Picture a captain's family at one of these windows, watching the sea for a sail coming home. People still stand here looking out at the same water. Only now, most of us are waiting for the sun."]}] },
  { id: "mykonos-paraportiani", v: "1-935b42", city: /Mykonos/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(paraportiani)\b/i, title: "Panagia Paraportiani Church", where: "Panagia Paraportiani Church", checked: "2026-11-22",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Church of Panagia Paraportiani","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Panagia_Paraportiani","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Mykonos","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Mykonos","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Mykonos","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mykonos","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"The white church","minutes":1,"stand":"In the open space beside the church, where you can take in its whole shape.","paras":["This soft, white, almost sculpted building is Panagia Paraportiani, the Church of Our Lady. Its odd, lovable shape, Wikipedia says, makes it one of the most photographed places in the world.","The whitewash can be dazzling in full sun. Sunglasses help, and the stone underfoot can be uneven, so look down now and then as you move along.","Wikivoyage goes further: of all the churches on Mykonos, it calls this the most impressive.","And there's a lot of competition. Mykonos is crowded with churches. The usual explanation is that for many years islanders had to build a church on their land before they could build a house. Wikivoyage calls this one the most photographed of them all."]},{"name":"Five churches in one","minutes":1,"stand":"Move along the outside as far as the path allows, looking up at the upper level.","paras":["Here's the secret of that shape. It isn't one church, it's five, joined together. Four sit at ground level, and the fifth is built on top of them.","The four below are dedicated to Saint Eustathios, Saint Sozon, the Saints Anargyroi, and Saint Anastasia. They form the base, and the fifth church rests above them. As you move along, see if you can tell where one ends and the next begins.","It went up slowly. Construction began in 1425 and wasn't finished until the 17th century, around 200 years from start to finish."]},{"name":"By the castle gate","minutes":1,"stand":"Stand where you can see the lanes of the Kastro quarter around the church.","paras":["This quarter is Kastro, the oldest part of town. The church's name, Paraportiani, means Our Lady of the Side Gate, because its entrance was at the side gate into the Kastro.","A gate into what? A medieval castle. Here's how it came about. After Constantinople fell in the Fourth Crusade in 1204, Mykonos passed to Andrea Ghisi. The castle was built here in 1207 by the Ghisi family, who controlled the island at the time.","The castle didn't last. It was destroyed in the 16th century, and when the town grew in the 18th century, new buildings covered what was left. The church outlived the walls it was named for.","Just a few steps away is the Folklore Museum, set in an 18th-century sea captain's house, if you'd like to see how island families once lived.","The first stones were laid here in 1425, and the last around two hundred years later. Whoever started it never saw the shape you're looking at now."]}] },
  { id: "mykonos-windmills", v: "1-c1e483", city: /Mykonos/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(kato mili|windmills of mykonos|mykonos windmills)\b/i, title: "Kato Mili Windmills", where: "Kato Mili Windmills", checked: "2026-11-22",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Mykonos","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mykonos","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Mykonos","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Mykonos","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Mykonos windmills","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Windmills_of_Mykonos","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"The row of mills","minutes":1,"stand":"On the windmill hill, well back from the edge, with the row of mills in front of you and the sea beyond.","paras":["Here they are: Kato Mili, which is Greek for the lower mills. A row of white windmills on a hill above the sea, set here to catch the strong north wind.","It can blow hard up here. Hold on to your hat, keep children close, and stay back from the drop on the sea side.","Look at their shape. Each one is round and white, with a pointed roof and very small windows, and the roofs are capped with wood and straw. Most windmills on the island face north, because that's where the strongest wind comes from most of the year.","There are 16 windmills on Mykonos today, and seven of them are on this hill in Chora, Wikipedia says. They stand out above the town, and they're the first thing you see coming into the harbor of Alefkandra below."]},{"name":"Wind and wheat","minutes":1,"stand":"Stay where you are and feel the wind on your face.","paras":["Mykonos has a nickname: the Island of the Winds. These mills put that wind to work. They ground wheat, and they were an important source of income for the people here.","Most of them were built by the Venetians in the 1500s. Venice had ruled Mykonos directly since 1390, until the Ottomans took the island in 1537. But the building didn't stop with them. New mills were still going up into the early 20th century. Once, Wikivoyage says, this island was a great producer of wheat and bread.","When did the sails stop turning? Accounts differ. One says the mills were used until the early 1900s. Another says production ended in the middle of the century.","Many have since been restored, some as homes, others as safe stores for the island's old documents. And you'll find mills like these on almost every island in the Cyclades.","In July and August, a wind called the meltemi blows steadily through these islands. It takes a little of the edge off the summer heat."]},{"name":"Looking down","minutes":1,"stand":"Turn to face the sea and the waterfront houses below.","paras":["From here you look over Little Venice. Wikivoyage calls this the most popular place on the island to watch the sun go down.","Somewhere off this coast, near the mills, is a formation called Camel Rock. It's named for its shape, like a camel sitting in the shallow blue and green water.","These are the lower mills, and there are upper ones too. Above the town, in Ano Myloi, the Upper Windmills, one old mill now holds the Agricultural Museum, also called the Bonis Mill.","When you're ready, the stairs lead down from the windmills into Little Venice.","Take the steps slowly. They can be steep and worn.","Nobody built these to be pretty. They were built for wheat and bread, and for a living. Yet here we all are, at the end of the day, watching them against the sky."]}] },
  { id: "naples-capri", v: "1-fd7d81", city: /Naples|Napoli|Pompeii|Sorrento|Capri|Pozzuoli|Salerno/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(capri)\b/i, title: "Capri", where: "Capri", checked: "2026-11-23",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Capri","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Capri","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Capri","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Capri","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Blue Grotto (Capri)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue_Grotto_(Capri)","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Villa Jovis","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Villa_Jovis","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Faraglioni","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Faraglioni","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Arrival at Marina Grande","minutes":1,"stand":"On the quay at Marina Grande, out of the way of the crowds, looking up at the town on the hill.","paras":["Welcome to Capri. Italians say it KAH-pree. You've landed at Marina Grande, the main harbor. From here the funicular climbs to Capri town, and boats leave for the Blue Grotto.","This island has been a resort since the days of the Roman Republic. It's small and made of rugged limestone, about 5 kilometers off the tip of the Sorrento Peninsula, with sea caves, sea stacks and high cliffs. There are two towns: Capri, and Anacapri, higher up on the slopes of Monte Solaro, the island's highest point at 589 meters.","Where does the name come from? Nobody knows for sure. It may be Greek for wild boar, or Latin for goats. Boar fossils have turned up on the island, which helps the Greek idea. And in one telling of the Greek myth, this was the island of the sirens."]},{"name":"The Blue Grotto","minutes":2,"stand":"At Marina Grande or by the cave, while you wait your turn for a rowboat.","paras":["This is the Blue Grotto. The opening is only about a meter high at low tide, which is why you'll lie flat while the boatman pulls the boat in on a chain. Inside, the sunlight comes up from an opening under the water, and the red is filtered out, so the whole cave glows blue.","Keep your head and hands inside the boat until the boatman says it's clear.","Once you're inside and the boatman says it's fine, dip a hand in the water. Things placed in it look silver, and your own hand will seem to glow. The cave often closes when the tide or the waves are up, so if you got in today, you were lucky.","The Romans knew this place well. It was the emperor Tiberius's swimming hole, and a temple of the sea. Statues of the sea gods Neptune and Triton were raised from the floor in 1964, and they're now in a museum in Anacapri.","In the 1700s, sailors and islanders kept away. The cave was said to be home to witches and monsters. Then in 1826 a local fisherman, Angelo Ferraro, took two German visitors to the cave, the writer August Kopisch and the painter Ernst Fries, and after their visit the wider world discovered it. Though an old Neapolitan song from the 1700s shows the locals knew it all along."]},{"name":"The Piazzetta","minutes":1,"stand":"Up in Capri town, in the little main square where the funicular brings you.","paras":["This is the Piazzetta, Piazza Umberto the First, the living room of Capri town. Everyone passes through here sooner or later.","The first emperor to fall for Capri was Augustus. He made it his private resort, with villas, temples, aqueducts and gardens. The historian Suetonius says his workmen dug up giant bones and stone weapons, and Augustus put them on display. Modern experts think they may have been fossils of huge extinct animals.","The island's later history is rougher. In the 1500s Ottoman admirals captured it twice, Barbarossa in 1535 and Turgut Reis in 1553. Then from the later 1800s came artists and writers, drawn by the beauty and by Capri's name as a tolerant place. In 1908 the writer Maxim Gorky even hosted Lenin here."]},{"name":"Villa Jovis","minutes":2,"stand":"At the top of Monte Tiberio, at the entrance to the ruins of Villa Jovis.","paras":["This is Villa Jovis, the Villa of Jupiter, the largest of the twelve villas that, according to the Roman historian Tacitus, the emperor Tiberius built on Capri. In AD 27 he moved here for good, and he ruled the Roman Empire from this island until he died in AD 37.","You'll have earned this view. The only way up is on foot, about two kilometers uphill from town, so bring water and take the climb slowly.","Why here, in the far north-east corner of the island? Probably because he was wary of the politics in Rome, and afraid of being murdered. His own rooms were hard to reach and heavily guarded.","The palace steps down several terraces, some 40 meters from top to bottom, and covers about 7,000 square meters. The north wing held the living quarters, the south wing the offices, and the east wing was for receptions. On the west side was an open hall looking toward Anacapri. Water was the hard part up here, so the engineers caught rain from the roofs in a large cistern.","South of the main building are the remains of a watch tower. From there, messages could be sent to the mainland, by fire or by smoke.","You may hear darker stories. The Roman writer Suetonius said Tiberius lived in wild debauchery up here, and the story goes that people who displeased him were thrown from the cliffs. Most modern historians think those tales were sensationalized."]},{"name":"The Faraglioni","minutes":1,"stand":"Wherever you get a clear view of the three great rocks off the south-east coast.","paras":["Those three giant rocks are the Faraglioni, sea stacks carved by the waves.","Stella, joined to the island, is 109 meters high; Mezzo is 82; and Scopolo, also called Fuori, 106.","On Scopolo, think small. A rare blue lizard is found on that rock. And the word faraglione perhaps comes from the old Greek and Latin words for a lighthouse."]},{"name":"Closing","minutes":1,"stand":"Before you head back down to the harbor, find one last spot looking out to sea.","paras":["Take one more look. An emperor came here, perhaps to hide from the world, and governed it from this island all the same. Today the island belongs to whoever is standing still long enough to look."]}] },
  { id: "naples-duomo-na", v: "1-332c7c", city: /Naples|Napoli|Pompeii|Sorrento|Capri|Pozzuoli|Salerno/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(duomo di napoli|naples cathedral|cattedrale di san gennaro|tesoro di san gennaro|santa restituta)\b/i, title: "Naples Cathedral and the Chapel of San Gennaro", where: "Naples Cathedral and the Chapel of San Gennaro", checked: "2026-11-23",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Naples Cathedral","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Naples_Cathedral","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","title":"Historic Centre of Naples - UNESCO World Heritage Centre","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/726/","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Santa Chiara, Naples","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Santa_Chiara%2C_Naples","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Naples","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Naples","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Januarius","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Januarius","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Giuseppe Sanmartino","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giuseppe_Sanmartino","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"The front","minutes":1,"stand":"On the sidewalk across the street from the cathedral, where you can take in the whole front.","paras":["This is the cathedral of Naples. Officially it's the Cathedral of the Assumption of Mary, but it's widely known as the cathedral of San Gennaro, Saint Januarius, the city's patron saint.","The church itself is Gothic. King Charles the First of Anjou commissioned it, and it was finished in the early 1300s under King Robert, the same Robert who built Santa Chiara. The front you see is newer, reworked in the late 1800s by Enrico Alvino, but the main doorway is from the 1400s, and it includes sculptures by Tino da Camaino.","And underneath, it's older still. The cathedral stands on the foundations of two early Christian basilicas, and digs under the building have turned up Greek and Roman remains.","The street out front carries traffic. Stay on the sidewalk while you look.","This is a working church. If a Mass is on, keep your voice low and stay toward the back."]},{"name":"Who was San Gennaro?","minutes":1,"stand":"Step inside and stop in the nave, facing the main altar.","paras":["So who was Januarius? Honestly, very little is known. No records from his own lifetime survive. Later sources and legend say he was a bishop, martyred in the Great Persecution under the emperor Diocletian, who retired in 305.","The legend goes further. He was condemned to be thrown to bears in the amphitheater at Pozzuoli, but in the end he was beheaded near the Solfatara crater. Other versions say the beasts refused to touch him, or that he walked out of a furnace unharmed.","The first real historical mention is a letter from 432. It calls him simply bishop as well as martyr, an illustrious member of the Neapolitan church.","His body was brought to Naples in 1497, and a richly decorated crypt was built for it beneath the cathedral, the Succorpo, finished in 1506. His bones are kept in a crypt under the main altar, ahead of you.","Naples turns to him in hard times. By tradition, he was called on to stop the eruption of Vesuvius in 1631, and, the tradition goes, the lava heading for the towns slowed down."]},{"name":"The Chapel of the Treasure","minutes":1,"stand":"At the entrance to the Royal Chapel of the Treasure of San Gennaro.","paras":["This is the Royal Chapel of the Treasure of San Gennaro. Look for the bronze railing by Cosimo Fanzago. Inside are frescoes by Domenichino and Giovanni Lanfranco, and a rich high altar by Francesco Solimena.","The treasure is a collection of gifts gathered over seven centuries, from popes, kings, emperors and ordinary people. And it's been kept, not by the clergy, but by the Deputation, a secular body set up in 1527 by a vote of the city of Naples. According to one group of experts who studied it, it's worth more than the crowns of the late Queen Elizabeth the Second and the Tsar of Russia.","Look for the almost life-size silver group of Tobias and the Angel, made in 1797 from designs by Giuseppe Sanmartino, the sculptor of the Veiled Christ.","The Museum of the Treasure has its entrance on the right side of the cathedral, under the arcades. Through it you can reach this chapel even when the cathedral is closed."]},{"name":"The blood","minutes":2,"stand":"Stay near the Treasure chapel, or find a seat in the nave.","paras":["And now the famous part. Naples keeps what is believed to be the blood of San Gennaro, sealed in two small vials. Three times a year it's brought out: on the Saturday before the first Sunday in May, on September 19, and on December 16. Usually, the dried blood appears to turn liquid. And legend says that if it fails, disaster will fall on Naples.","When did this begin? A chronicle of Naples from 1382 describes his cult in detail, and doesn't mention the blood at all. The first certain date is 1389, when it was found to have melted. The legend says a woman named Eusebia saved his blood just after his death.","Here's how it goes. The archbishop holds up the reliquary and tilts it, to show the contents are solid. Then the faithful pray, among them the people known as the relatives of San Gennaro. Usually the larger vial appears to turn liquid. Sometimes it happens almost at once; sometimes it takes hours, or even days.","So what's actually in it? Nobody knows for sure. The Church has never made an official statement, and it doesn't allow the vials to be opened. Some researchers suggest a gel that turns liquid when it's moved. A biologist in Naples tested a different old blood relic in 2010, saw the same kind of change, and concluded: there's blood, no miracle. A historian argues it's all about temperature. Scholars still disagree.","It's watched closely. In 2015, during a visit by Pope Francis, the archbishop announced the blood had half liquefied. For Benedict the Sixteenth, in 2007, it didn't. A failure is said to be a bad omen, and some people even link the failed years with wars and pandemics."]},{"name":"Santa Restituta and the baptistery","minutes":1,"stand":"Find the way into Santa Restituta, the older church reached from inside the cathedral.","paras":["This is Santa Restituta, the original early Christian church, next to the cathedral and reached from inside it. The cathedral also keeps an early Christian baptistery, with mosaics from the 300s.","Down in the excavations you can read the city in layers: a Greek wall from a temple of Apollo, the courtyard of a late Roman house, a stretch of Roman aqueduct, and a piece of Greek road climbing a slope.","Two more things to look for in the cathedral: an Assumption by Perugino, and the Minutolo Chapel, with frescoes from the 1300s. Boccaccio mentions that chapel in the Decameron."]},{"name":"Closing","minutes":1,"stand":"Back in the nave, take a seat if you can.","paras":["Whatever you make of the blood, think about the people. Thousands fill this church three times a year, praying, waiting, watching one small vial. A whole city holding its breath together. That, more than any chemistry, is what you're standing in."]}] },
  { id: "naples-museonaz", v: "1-a00baa", city: /Naples|Napoli|Pompeii|Sorrento|Capri|Pozzuoli|Salerno/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(museo archeologico nazionale|national archaeological museum of naples|national archaeological museum, naples|farnese bull|alexander mosaic)\b/i, title: "National Archaeological Museum of Naples", where: "National Archaeological Museum of Naples", checked: "2026-11-23",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"National Archaeological Museum, Naples","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Archaeological_Museum%2C_Naples","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Pompeii","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Pompeii","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Pompeii","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pompeii","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Farnese Bull","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Farnese_Bull","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Alexander Mosaic","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexander_Mosaic","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Arrival","minutes":2,"stand":"Outside the main entrance, looking up at the long front of the building.","paras":["Welcome to the National Archaeological Museum of Naples. Greek, Roman and Renaissance works fill it, and above all, finds from Pompeii, Herculaneum and Stabiae. If you're going to Pompeii, this is the other half of the story: most of the best-preserved mosaics and finds from Pompeii are kept here, not at the site.","The building has had several lives. It dates from 1585, and began as cavalry barracks. From 1616 to 1777 it was the University of Naples. Then it became a museum, known from 1816 to 1861 as the Royal Bourbon Museum.","Why is it all here? In 1738, workers digging foundations for a summer palace for the King of Naples, Charles of Bourbon, rediscovered buried Herculaneum. Digging at Pompeii began in 1748, before anyone knew which city it was. An inscription found in 1763 settled it: Pompeii. And Charles cared, because displaying antiquities boosted the prestige of Naples.","One honest note before we go in. Collections move. In recent years some of the biggest Farnese sculptures, sometimes even the Farnese Bull, have been shown at the Capodimonte museum across the city, and the Alexander Mosaic has been under restoration, with a facsimile covering its place. If something isn't where you expect it, ask a guard.","It's a big building. Pace yourself, and sit down whenever you find a bench."]},{"name":"The Farnese sculptures","minutes":2,"stand":"In the hall of the great Farnese sculptures, in front of the huge marble group with the bull.","paras":["If it's here today, this is the Farnese Bull, the largest single sculpture yet recovered from the ancient world. It stands more than 4 meters high and weighs 21.8 tonnes.","It tells a brutal myth. Dirce, wife of King Lykos of Thebes, had mistreated a woman named Antiope. Antiope's sons, Amphion and Zethus, punished her by tying her to a wild bull.","It's a Roman copy of a Greek work, dated between 222 and 235 AD. It was dug up in 1546 in the Baths of Caracalla in Rome, in excavations Pope Paul the Third ordered to decorate the Farnese family palace. Michelangelo even planned to turn it into a fountain.","Is this the very sculpture the Roman writer Pliny the Elder described, by two brothers from Rhodes? Scholars disagree. Some say it's a later Roman version made just for the baths; others dispute that. It's also much restored: the child, the dog and the other animals around the base perhaps weren't in the original at all.","Most of the classical sculpture here comes from the same Farnese collection: Roman copies that are often the only record of lost Greek masterpieces. Look for the Farnese Hercules, found in the same excavation. It fixed the image of Hercules in the European imagination.","And find the Farnese Atlas, the oldest surviving figure of Atlas, and it carries the oldest view of the Western constellations, perhaps based on the star catalog of the astronomer Hipparchus."]},{"name":"The Alexander Mosaic","minutes":2,"stand":"In the mosaic rooms, in front of the great battle scene, or the facsimile that covers its place if it is still being restored.","paras":["This is the Alexander Mosaic, from the floor of the House of the Faun in Pompeii, usually dated to around 120 to 100 BC. It once lay on a floor; here it hangs on the wall. It shows Alexander the Great charging the Persian king, Darius the Third. It's traditionally believed to be the Battle of Issus.","Find Alexander on the left: no helmet, lance in his right arm, riding his horse Bucephalus, his eyes fixed on Darius. Then look right. Darius, in his chariot, reaches toward a dying soldier, while his driver whips the horses to flee. Look at the fear in his face.","Now look closer. More than 50 men are packed in. In the foreground, a Persian soldier sees his own death throes reflected in a shield. And in the middle, there's a horse seen from behind, a daring bit of foreshortening.","How many tiny stones went into it? Nobody knows exactly. Estimates run from about one and a half million to more than four million, each around 2 millimeters wide.","It's probably a copy of a Greek painting from the late 300s or early 200s BC, perhaps by Philoxenus of Eretria, or Apelles. It was rediscovered in 1831 and brought to Naples in September 1843. Since 2005, a modern copy has lain in the House of the Faun."]},{"name":"Pompeii and Herculaneum","minutes":1,"stand":"In the fresco galleries.","paras":["These rooms hold a large collection of wall paintings from Pompeii and Herculaneum, towns buried in ash and pumice when Vesuvius erupted in 79 AD.","Look elsewhere in the museum for the bronzes from one great villa at Herculaneum, the Villa of the Papyri: a seated Hermes, a sprawling drunken satyr, and a pair of wonderfully lively runners. The villa takes its name from the scrolls found there after 1752, carbonized by the eruption.","If you have time, find the Egyptian collection: 2,500 objects, one of the largest in Italy, behind only Turin, Florence and Bologna."]},{"name":"The Secret Cabinet","minutes":1,"stand":"At the entrance to the Secret Cabinet.","paras":["This is the Secret Cabinet. The Bourbon kings gave that name to the rooms where they kept erotic art, mostly from the digs at Pompeii and Herculaneum. Only people of mature age and known morals were let in.","One account says that in 1819, a royal visitor, Francis, came with his wife and daughter and was so embarrassed he had it all locked away. By 1851 even nude statues of Venus were shut in, and the entrance walled up, in the hope the collection would be forgotten. When Garibaldi's forces took Naples in 1860, he ordered it opened. The royal butler with the key was gone, so they broke in. It only opened fully to the public in April 2000."]},{"name":"Closing","minutes":1,"stand":"Wherever you'd like to end, ideally back near the mosaics.","paras":["Remember, the Alexander Mosaic lay on a floor where its owner received guests; it was the first thing a visitor saw on entering that room. That's the thing about this museum. So much here was once part of someone's home, until one day in 79 AD, life stopped. Carry that with you to Pompeii."]}] },
  { id: "naples-plebiscito", v: "1-b6fdb9", city: /Naples|Napoli|Pompeii|Sorrento|Capri|Pozzuoli|Salerno/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(plebiscito|palazzo reale di napoli|royal palace of naples|san francesco di paola|teatro san carlo)\b/i, title: "Piazza del Plebiscito and the Royal Palace", where: "Piazza del Plebiscito and the Royal Palace", checked: "2026-11-23",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Piazza del Plebiscito","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piazza_del_Plebiscito","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Naples","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Naples","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Naples","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Naples","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"San Francesco di Paola","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_Francesco_di_Paola","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Royal Palace of Naples","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Palace_of_Naples","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","title":"Historic Centre of Naples - UNESCO World Heritage Centre","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/726/","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"The square","minutes":2,"stand":"In the middle of the square, facing the domed church with the curving colonnades.","paras":["This is Piazza del Plebiscito, the main square of Naples. In front of you is the church of San Francesco di Paola, with its colonnades reaching out on both sides. Behind you is the Royal Palace.","The name means the Square of the Plebiscite. It remembers the vote of October 21, 1860, that brought Naples into the new, united Kingdom of Italy.","But the square was planned for an emperor. In the first years of the 1800s, Joachim Murat, King of Naples and Napoleon's brother-in-law, planned it as a tribute to Napoleon. Then Napoleon fell, the Bourbons came back, and King Ferdinand carried on the building, but turned it into a church.","He dedicated it to Saint Francis of Paola, who had stayed in a monastery on this very spot in the 1400s. Does the church remind you of the Pantheon in Rome? Look at the portico on six columns and two Ionic pillars. Inside it's round, under a dome 53 meters high.","Francis of Paola himself was a friar who lived from 1416 to 1507 and founded the Order of Minims.","It hasn't always looked this grand. From 1963 the square was a parking lot. Only in 1994, when it was renovated for the G7 summit, was it closed to traffic entirely. Now it hosts open-air concerts, from Pino Daniele to Bruce Springsteen in 2013.","If the sun is strong, step into the shade of the colonnade while you listen."]},{"name":"The Royal Palace","minutes":2,"stand":"Turn around and face the long front of the Royal Palace.","paras":["This is the Royal Palace. It was begun in 1600, designed by Domenico Fontana, for the Spanish viceroys who governed Naples. From 1734, Charles of Bourbon made it the main home of the Bourbon kings for more than a hundred years.","Officially it was built to welcome King Philip the Third of Spain on a royal visit. He never came. Historians today think the viceroy knew that perfectly well, and built it to please himself. In fact the only Spanish king who ever stayed here was Philip the Fifth, in 1702.","Now look at the niches along the front. In 1888 eight statues of kings were set into them. From left to right: Roger the Second, Frederick the Second, Charles of Anjou, Alfonso the Fifth of Aragon, Charles the Fifth, Charles of Bourbon, Joachim Murat, and Victor Emmanuel the Second. The idea was to show the new royal house, the Savoys, as heirs to every dynasty before them. Never mind that Victor Emmanuel the Second never held the title of king of Naples.","Those niches were once open arches. Fontana gave the palace a portico along its whole length, so people could walk under cover in bad weather. But the pillars were being crushed, and in 1753 the arches were walled up, to a design by Luigi Vanvitelli.","The front is nearly a hundred and seventy meters long: grey stone picking out the architecture against reddish brick, a two-tone style that caught on all over Naples. In 1837 a fire destroyed much of the eastern half, and the architect Gaetano Genovese rebuilt the palace and tied it all together.","The future king Victor Emmanuel the Third was born here in 1869, and in 1919 he gave the palace to the state. Since 1924 the eastern half has held the National Library. If you go in, the grand staircase is the showpiece. Montesquieu called the earlier one the most beautiful in Europe, in 1729. What you climb today was rebuilt after the fire, in rich marbles."]},{"name":"The opera house and the Galleria","minutes":1,"stand":"Walk to the Teatro San Carlo, beside the palace, watching the traffic as you leave the square.","paras":["This is the Teatro San Carlo, part of the royal palace complex. It opened on November 4, 1737, for the king's name day. It's said to be the oldest continuously active opera house in Europe.","Once you leave the square, there's traffic again. Use the crossings and keep to the sidewalk.","Rossini and Donizetti both ran it for years, and Donizetti's Lucia di Lammermoor had its first production here. The theater even connects straight into the palace, through two vestibules, one of them private.","Directly across from the opera house is the Galleria Umberto the First, designed by Emanuele Rocco. It was meant to mix shops, cafes and street life with apartments upstairs."]},{"name":"Closing","minutes":1,"stand":"Come back to the middle of the square.","paras":["Think about this square for a moment. It was planned to glorify an emperor, turned into a church, spent years as a parking lot, and it's named after a vote. Today it belongs to the people walking across it, you included."]}] },
  { id: "naples-pompeii", v: "1-3c0e12", city: /Naples|Napoli|Pompeii|Sorrento|Capri|Pozzuoli|Salerno/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(pompeii|pompei)\b/i, title: "Pompeii", where: "Pompeii", checked: "2026-11-23",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Pompeii","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pompeii","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","title":"Archaeological Areas of Pompei, Herculaneum and Torre Annunziata - UNESCO World Heritage Centre","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/829/","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Pompeii","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Pompeii","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Eruption of Mount Vesuvius in 79 AD","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eruption_of_Mount_Vesuvius_in_79_AD","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Giuseppe Fiorelli","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giuseppe_Fiorelli","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Arrival","minutes":1,"stand":"Just inside the site, step to the side of the path, out of the stream of visitors.","paras":["Welcome to Pompeii. In 79 AD, Mount Vesuvius buried this whole town under 4 to 6 meters of ash and pumice.","Watch your step on these old streets: cobblestones, uneven ground, and grooves worn by cart wheels. In summer it's often 32 to 35 degrees Celsius out here, so wear good shoes and a hat, and carry plenty of water.","This was a wealthy Roman town of somewhere around 10,000 to 20,000 people, though newer counts of the houses suggest up to 11,500. UNESCO calls it the only archaeological site in the world that gives a complete picture of an ancient Roman city.","This town is much older than the Romans. The first settlements here date to the 8th century BC, when a people called the Oscans founded five villages. The name Pompeii perhaps comes from their word for five, pompe. Then in 89 BC the Roman general Sulla besieged the town, and afterwards it became a Roman colony.","And the volcano itself? Vesuvius is about 8 kilometers away, some five miles. The ground under your feet is a plateau of lava from its earlier eruptions. The people here were living on the volcano's own work."]},{"name":"The Forum","minutes":1,"stand":"Walk on to the Forum, the big open square, and stop near the middle.","paras":["This is the Forum, the center of public life in Pompeii, ringed by the Basilica, the Capitolium and temples.","Much of this went up in the 2nd century BC, when Pompeii grew rich on Rome's conquests in the east. That's when the Temple of Jupiter and the Basilica were built.","On the west side of the Forum stood the Basilica. It was the most important public building in town. Justice was handed out there, and business was done there too. North of it, on the same western side, is the Temple of Apollo. It holds some of the oldest remains found here, going back to 575 BC.","Notice how bare it all feels. Where are the statues, the marble? Probably carried off after the eruption, by survivors and by robbers digging down through the ash. In one house, archaeologists even found the robbers' note scratched on the wall: \"house dug.\""]},{"name":"The eruption","minutes":2,"stand":"Stay in the Forum, in a patch of shade if you can find one.","paras":["So what happened here? The only eyewitness account that survives comes from a 17-year-old, Pliny the Younger, watching from across the bay. Here in town, pumice rained down for about 18 hours, and most people got away. Then, in the night or early the next day, fast, scorching clouds of ash came down from the volcano.","Pliny watched from Misenum, about 29 kilometers, or 18 miles, away. He saw a dense cloud rise above the mountain that, in his words, \"more closely resembled a pine-tree than anything else.\" And a study published in 2010 concluded that heat, not suffocation, was the main killer here.","His uncle, Pliny the Elder, commanded the Roman fleet at Misenum. He launched the galleys to take people off the coast. When his helmsman urged him to turn back, he said, \"Fortune favors the brave,\" and sailed on to Stabiae. He never came home. His nephew blamed poisonous fumes, but a stroke or a heart attack is more likely; nobody knows for sure. His body was found the next day with no apparent injuries.","The part of his letters I always remember is this. His mother begged him to leave her behind and save himself. Instead he took her hand and led her away as best he could. Afterwards he wrote that the ash lay like a blanket of snow.","Two odd things about those letters. He wrote them some 25 years later, for the historian Tacitus. And Pompeii itself is never mentioned in them. Still, volcanologists honor his account by calling eruptions like this one \"Plinian.\"","And the date? UNESCO gives the 24th of August, 79. But the copies of Pliny's letter may have been corrupted over the centuries, and in 2018 archaeologists found a charcoal scribble dated to what we would call October 17. Scholars still argue about it, because nobody knows which day that note was talking about."]},{"name":"The streets","minutes":1,"stand":"Leave the Forum along one of the paved streets and stop beside a row of stepping stones.","paras":["Look down at the street. Those big blocks are stepping stones. Water and waste ran down these streets, so the sidewalks were built high, and the stepping stones match them. The grooves? Worn by the carts.","Now picture it busy. There were nearly 100 snack bars in town, thermopolia, selling hot food and drink over the counter. There were at least 31 bakeries, each with its ovens, millstones and a counter. And an aqueduct fed more than 25 street fountains.","Read the walls as you go. An election was coming when the volcano erupted, and the walls are full of political slogans, plus plenty of personal remarks, often insulting ones. Those scrawls also preserve the everyday Latin people actually spoke.","The digging still turns things up. In December 2020 a snack bar was uncovered with bright paintings of the food on offer. Its pots still held the remains of meals: duck, goat, pig, fish, and snails."]},{"name":"The plaster casts","minutes":2,"stand":"Go to the plaster casts of the victims: some are kept in the Forum Granary, back near the Forum, and others in the Garden of the Fugitives on the south-east side of the site.","paras":["These are the people of Pompeii, or rather the spaces they left. Giuseppe Fiorelli, who took charge here in 1863, realized that the hollows in the ash were left by bodies that had decayed. Plaster was poured in, and when the ash was cleared away, there was the shape of a person at the moment of death.","Please go gently here. Many casts still hold the person's bones. And the twisted poses are not signs of a long struggle; scientists explain them as a spasm caused by the shock of extreme heat. Clear resin has also been used, because it lasts longer and doesn't destroy the bones.","In the Garden of the Fugitives, the casts lie where the people fell, and some of them are children. About 1,150 bodies have been found so far, which suggests most people got away.","Fiorelli also gave the city its addresses. He divided it into nine regions, split those into blocks, and numbered the doorways of the houses. Archaeologists still use those addresses.","It took a long time to find this place again. In 1592 an architect, Domenico Fontana, dug an aqueduct right through the buried town and hit painted walls, and he kept it secret. Only on 20 August 1763 did an inscription prove that this was Pompeii."]},{"name":"Closing","minutes":1,"stand":"On your way out, pause in any quiet doorway along the street.","paras":["Pompeii is famous for how it ended. But try to walk out remembering how it lived: slogans for an election that was coming, bakeries with their ovens, and, across the bay, a young man leading his mother by the hand. Ordinary days, a lot like ours."]}] },
  { id: "naples-pozzuoli", v: "1-cc3458", city: /Naples|Napoli|Pompeii|Sorrento|Capri|Pozzuoli|Salerno/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(pozzuoli|puteoli)\b/i, title: "Pozzuoli", where: "Pozzuoli", checked: "2026-11-23",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Pozzuoli","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pozzuoli","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"King James Version (public domain), via bible-api.com","title":"","url":"https://bible-api.com/acts%2028%3A11-15?translation=kjv","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Macellum of Pozzuoli","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Macellum_of_Pozzuoli","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Bradyseism","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bradyseism","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Flavian Amphitheater (Pozzuoli)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flavian_Amphitheater_(Pozzuoli)","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Pozzuoli","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Pozzuoli","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"The harbor","minutes":2,"stand":"Down by the waterfront, looking out over the bay.","paras":["This is Pozzuoli, the Roman Puteoli. At its peak it was Rome's main commercial port, even though Rome was 150 miles away, and the grain ships from Alexandria came in here.","And one ship of Alexandria brought the apostle Paul. Here's how the book of Acts, chapter 28, tells it, in the King James Version: \"after one day the south wind blew, and we came the next day to Puteoli: Where we found brethren, and were desired to tarry with them seven days: and so we went toward Rome.\"","Think about that word, brethren. Paul is on his way to Rome, and there are already brethren, fellow believers, here to take him in. This was a city of many peoples: Greeks, Jews, Nabataeans, Phoenician merchants, and early Christians.","Acts keeps a few small details. The ship had wintered on an island, and its sign was Castor and Pollux. And after his week here, when the brethren heard he was coming, they came out to meet him as far as Appii Forum and The three taverns. Acts says that when Paul saw them, he thanked God, and took courage.","The road he took north is usually given as the Appian Way, the great Roman highway to the capital. Acts itself just says, \"so we went toward Rome.\"","The town is much older than Paul. It began in 531 BC as a Greek colony called Dicaearchia, founded by refugees from the island of Samos. The Roman name may come from the Latin for a well, or, some say, from the word for stink, because of the sulfur fumes around here. Nobody knows which.","Two more things this bay gave the world. The local volcanic sand, pozzolana, the dust of Puteoli, was the secret of Roman concrete, the stuff of the Pantheon's dome in Rome. And the ancient writers say the emperor Caligula once strung together a floating bridge of trading ships, over two miles long, from here across to Baiae, and rode his horse over it."]},{"name":"The Macellum","minutes":1,"stand":"Where you can look over the Macellum, the Roman market square with three tall marble columns.","paras":["In front of you is the city's symbol. People call it the Temple of Serapis, but it was really the Roman food market, the macellum. A statue of the god Serapis found there gave it the wrong name.","Picture shops all around a colonnade of 34 granite columns. In the center of the square stood a round building on a raised platform, ringed by sixteen marble columns under a dome. Across from the main entrance was a shrine with a porch of four big marble columns.","Now look at those three tall columns, partway up. See the band of little holes? Those were bored by sea creatures, shellfish that live in salt water. So this market sank below the sea, stayed there a long time, and then rose back up, with the columns still standing.","How high do the holes go? Accounts differ: up to about 6 meters from the floor by one measurement, 7 by another."]},{"name":"The ground that breathes","minutes":1,"stand":"Stay where you can see the three columns.","paras":["The name for this is bradyseism, from the Greek for slow movement: the ground slowly rises and falls as the magma and hot water below fill up or drain away.","You are near the center of a volcanic crater here, the Campi Flegrei. These columns puzzled the early geologists for years, and in the end they helped prove that the ground can rise and sink without being shaken to pieces. Charles Lyell put a picture of them at the front of his Principles of Geology in 1830.","It isn't only ancient history. From 1982 to 1984 the seabed in the bay rose by almost 2 meters. Tens of thousands of people had to leave their homes; accounts differ between 30,000 and 36,000. And the ground here is still restless today; the earthquakes came back in 2023 and since."]},{"name":"The Flavian Amphitheater","minutes":1,"stand":"Inside the Flavian Amphitheater, on the edge of the arena floor.","paras":["This is the Flavian Amphitheater, the third-largest Roman amphitheater in Italy. Only the Colosseum in Rome and the one at Capua are bigger. By one count it could hold up to 50,000 people.","Watch your footing here, on the worn stone and anywhere you go down into the passages below.","It was begun under the emperor Vespasian and probably finished under his son Titus. Historians think the same architects may have built the Colosseum.","The underground here is the real treasure. Its chambers, cages and passageways are among the best preserved of any Roman amphitheater. You can still see parts of the gear that lifted the animal cages up to the arena floor.","Local tradition holds that in 305 two Christians were thrown to the wild beasts in this arena: Proculus, now patron saint of Pozzuoli, and Januarius, San Gennaro, patron saint of Naples. The tradition says they survived the beasts, and were beheaded nearby, at the Solfatara crater.","Later the Solfatara's eruptions partly buried the arena, and it was abandoned. In the Middle Ages its outer marble was stripped away, but the inside was left as you see it."]},{"name":"Closing","minutes":1,"stand":"On your way out, look back toward the bay.","paras":["In time, the emperors enlarged the port of Ostia, closer to Rome, and Puteoli began to fade. But for a week, this busy harbor was where a man named Paul came ashore and found friends. Sometimes that is all a place needs to be remembered for."]}] },
  { id: "naples-sansevero", v: "1-eb86dc", city: /Naples|Napoli|Pompeii|Sorrento|Capri|Pozzuoli|Salerno/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(sansevero|veiled christ|cristo velato|pietatella)\b/i, title: "Sansevero Chapel and the Veiled Christ", where: "Sansevero Chapel and the Veiled Christ", checked: "2026-11-23",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Cappella Sansevero","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sansevero_Chapel","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Naples","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Naples","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Raimondo di Sangro","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raimondo_di_Sangro","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Veiled Christ","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Veiled_Christ","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Giuseppe Sanmartino","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giuseppe_Sanmartino","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Outside the chapel","minutes":1,"stand":"In the narrow street outside the chapel entrance, out of the way of the line.","paras":["This is the Sansevero Chapel, properly the Chapel of Santa Maria della Pietà. Inside are almost thirty works of art, and one of them is the Veiled Christ. It's a small place, which is why the line here can be longer than at other sights in Naples.","It began in 1590 as a private chapel. John Francesco di Sangro, Duke of Torremaggiore, had it built after he recovered from a serious illness, in what were then the gardens of his family's palace nearby. In 1613 Alessandro di Sangro made it the family's burial chapel. That's recorded on the marble over the entrance.","It's also called the Pietatella. The story goes that an unjustly arrested prisoner spotted a painting of the Virgin Mary, a Pietà, here, and the name grew from that painting. The tale was written down in 1623.","But the chapel you're about to see is really one man's creation: Raimondo di Sangro, Prince of Sansevero, who lived from 1710 to 1771. Nobleman, soldier, inventor, writer, scientist, alchemist and freemason. He gave the chapel its final form, and he built Masonic symbols into it.","Inside it's one quiet room. Take your time, and give the people around the sculpture a little space."]},{"name":"The Veiled Christ","minutes":2,"stand":"Inside, a step back from the sculpture lying in the center of the nave, near Christ's feet.","paras":["Here he is. The Veiled Christ, finished in 1753 by the Neapolitan sculptor Giuseppe Sanmartino, from a single block of white marble. The dead Christ lies on a couch, under a thin shroud that clings to his body. Look at the face. You can see the signs of pain right through the veil.","Now look down by his feet. Sanmartino carved the instruments of the Passion there: the pliers, the shackles, and the crown of thorns.","It wasn't meant to be Sanmartino's job. The prince first gave it to the Venetian sculptor Antonio Corradini, who made a clay model and then died. The commission passed to Sanmartino, with a demanding brief: a marble statue of Our Lord Jesus Christ in death, covered by a transparent shroud carved from the same block of stone as the statue.","And here's where the legend comes in. Some visitors decided the prince, a known alchemist, had laid a real veil over the figure and turned it to marble by a secret process. It's a good story. In fact, veiled statues go back to classical times. This is carving, not chemistry.","The sculptor Antonio Canova tried to acquire it, and said he would gladly give ten years of his life to have made something as good. As for Sanmartino, he was born in Naples in 1720, and little is known about his early life. This is his first dated work, and it helped launch his career."]},{"name":"Modesty and Disillusion","minutes":1,"stand":"Walk along the chapel to find the other veiled statue and the one wrapped in a marble net.","paras":["Two more marble tricks complete the set. Modesty, by Antonio Corradini, finished in 1752, is another figure under a see-through veil. It's a tomb monument to Raimondo's mother, Cecilia, who died shortly after he was born.","The Veiled Christ shows the influence of this veiled Modesty. Now find the Release from Deception, Il Disinganno, by Francesco Queirolo of Genoa, a monument to Raimondo's father. Look for the fisherman's net, carved in marble."]},{"name":"The prince's chapel","minutes":1,"stand":"Stand beside the Veiled Christ again and look up at the ceiling.","paras":["Above you is the Glory of Paradise, painted by Francesco Maria Russo in 1749.","Now look down. The original floor was a black and white labyrinth. It is said the colors stood for good and evil, and the labyrinth is a Masonic symbol of initiation. Most of the floor you see today dates from 1901.","Raimondo was a restless mind. Among his inventions were colored fireworks, and a carriage with wood and cork horses that could travel on land and on water. He led the Masonic lodge of Naples until the Church excommunicated him. Pope Benedict the Fourteenth later lifted that, probably thanks to his family's influence. Before he died he destroyed his own scientific archive, and his descendants, under threat of excommunication, destroyed the rest.","His family palace nearby had a darker past. At the end of the 1500s, the composer Carlo Gesualdo caught his wife and her lover there, and killed them both."]},{"name":"The anatomical machines","minutes":1,"stand":"Down in the basement, in front of the two figures.","paras":["Take the steps down slowly. And a word of warning: what's down here is unsettling.","These are the anatomical machines: a man and a pregnant woman, their skeletons wrapped in a web of arteries and veins, colored red and blue. They were made around 1760 by the anatomist Giuseppe Salerno, for Raimondo.","Naples told dark stories about the prince. The legend said he could make blood out of nothing, and even copy the miracle of San Gennaro's blood. Many Neapolitans believed these two were his servant and a pregnant woman, whose veins had been injected with some substance. The latest research says otherwise: there's no sign of injection, and the vessels are made of beeswax, iron wire and silk. Even so, nobody knows exactly how they were made.","Raimondo died in 1771, his death hastened by the dangerous chemicals he worked with. In 1794 a Swedish naturalist named a plant genus after him: Sansevieria."]},{"name":"A last look","minutes":1,"stand":"Back upstairs, one last time beside the Veiled Christ.","paras":["For all the prince's secrets, the real marvel here is in plain sight. Sanmartino's veil doesn't hide the suffering, it shows it. Take one more look at that face before you go."]}] },
  { id: "naples-sorrento", v: "1-0fbd1d", city: /Naples|Napoli|Pompeii|Sorrento|Capri|Pozzuoli|Salerno/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(sorrento|surrentum)\b/i, title: "Sorrento", where: "Sorrento", checked: "2026-11-23",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Sorrento","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sorrento","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Sorrento","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Sorrento","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Piazza Tasso","minutes":1,"stand":"In Piazza Tasso, the main square, at the foot of Viale Enrico Caruso.","paras":["This is Piazza Tasso, the main square of Sorrento, named for the poet Torquato Tasso, who was born here. Now walk a few steps up Viale Enrico Caruso to the railing and look down.","That deep gorge is the Valley of the Mills. At the bottom are the ruins of an old water mill. Gorges like this protected the ancient town, and the streets of the old town still follow its plan.","Hold on to the railing and keep children close; it's a long drop.","The Romans called this place Surrentum, but it's older than Rome's rule here. The oldest ruins are Oscan, from about 600 BC."]},{"name":"The old streets","minutes":1,"stand":"Walk along Corso Italia to the cathedral, then into the narrow lanes of the old town toward Via San Cesareo.","paras":["Along Corso Italia is the cathedral. It goes back to the 14th century, though the front was rebuilt in 1924, and its doors, from the 11th century, are said to have come from Constantinople.","Via San Cesareo is one of the oldest streets in town, and now the main shopping street. You'll see lemons and limoncello everywhere, made from lemon rind, alcohol, water and sugar. Here's the surprise: in Roman times Surrentum was famous for its wine, not for lemons. The lemon groves came much later.","This town has had hard days too. On 13 June 1558 an Ottoman fleet sacked Sorrento, and about 2,000 captives were reportedly carried off. New walls went up afterwards."]},{"name":"Villa Comunale","minutes":1,"stand":"In the Villa Comunale park, at the edge of the terrace, facing the bay.","paras":["And here's the view. The coast below you, the Bay of Naples, and Vesuvius in the distance. On a clear day you can see across the bay toward Naples.","This coast has a myth. Since ancient times, the Sirens have been linked with Sorrento: three of them, the story goes, lived on the little Li Galli islands near Punta Campanella, enchanting passing sailors. Legend even says Ulysses founded a great sanctuary on this peninsula, first devoted to the Sirens, and that this is where the name Sorrento comes from.","Ancient Surrentum's most important temples really were to Athena and to the Sirens. Athena's stood out at the tip of the peninsula. No trace of either survives.","Look across at Vesuvius again. An inscription shows that the emperor Titus restored the town's timepiece, its horologium, the year after the earthquake of 79 AD, the same disaster year as Pompeii.","Writers and artists kept coming. Byron, Keats, Goethe, Dickens, Wagner and Ibsen all visited; Ibsen wrote parts of Peer Gynt here. Nietzsche stayed six months in 1876 and worked on Human, All Too Human."]},{"name":"Closing","minutes":1,"stand":"Stay at the terrace edge a moment longer.","paras":["There's an old song, Torna a Surriento: Come Back to Sorrento. Standing here, you can see why someone wrote it."]}] },
  { id: "naples-spaccanapoli", v: "1-6e5def", city: /Naples|Napoli|Pompeii|Sorrento|Capri|Pozzuoli|Salerno/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(spaccanapoli|gesù nuovo|gesu nuovo|santa chiara|centro storico di napoli|historic centre of naples)\b/i, title: "Spaccanapoli and the Historic Center", where: "Spaccanapoli and the Historic Center", checked: "2026-11-23",
    sources: [{"pub":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","title":"Historic Centre of Naples - UNESCO World Heritage Centre","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/726/","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Historic Centre of Naples","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Historic_Centre_of_Naples","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Naples","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Naples","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Santa Chiara, Naples","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Santa_Chiara%2C_Naples","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Gesù Nuovo","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ges%C3%B9_Nuovo","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Naples","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Naples","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Giuseppe Sanmartino","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giuseppe_Sanmartino","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Piazza del Gesù Nuovo","minutes":2,"stand":"In the middle of Piazza del Gesù Nuovo, beside the tall carved spire, facing the church with the studded stone front.","paras":["Welcome to the old center of Naples, a UNESCO World Heritage Site since 1995. The street that runs past Santa Chiara, across the square, is Spaccanapoli. The name literally means Naples splitter.","Three landmarks share this square: the spire beside you, the church of Gesù Nuovo in front of you, and Santa Chiara across the way. And Spaccanapoli is only a nickname: on the signs you'll read Via Benedetto Croce, and further on, Via San Biagio dei Librai.","Look up at the spire. It's the Guglia dell'Immacolata, the tallest and most ornate of three plague columns in Naples. Tradition says it was built to ask the Virgin Mary for protection from the plague. It took its time, too: begun in the 1600s, and finished only in 1750, after decades of pauses.","Now look at the church front, covered in stone points. That's Gesù Nuovo, the New Jesus. And those diamond-shaped stones were never meant for a church. This was a palace, built in 1470 for Roberto Sanseverino, Prince of Salerno. His family got tangled in political intrigue, the property was confiscated, and in the 1580s the Jesuits bought it for 45,000 ducats. They turned it into a church, and kept the palace front.","If you step inside, turn around once you're through the door. On the inside of the front wall is a great Baroque painting, The Expulsion of Heliodorus from the Temple, by Francesco Solimena, from 1725. And in the chapel of the Visitation, under the altar, lies a doctor: Saint Joseph Moscati, who taught at the University of Naples and was head physician of the hospital of the Incurables. He died in 1927, and Pope John Paul the Second made him a saint in 1987.","Before we walk: Spaccanapoli is narrow and busy. Keep to the side, watch for scooters, mind the uneven paving, and keep your bag in front of you."]},{"name":"Santa Chiara","minutes":2,"stand":"Leave the square by Via Benedetto Croce and go through the gateway to the front of Santa Chiara, diagonally across from Gesù Nuovo.","paras":["Santa Chiara looks severe after the Gesù. It's Gothic, built by King Robert of Naples and his queen, Sancha of Majorca. The exact dates differ from one account to another: 1310 to 1328 in one, 1313 to 1340 in another. Either way, it was the largest church of the Poor Clares ever built.","Inside, walk up toward the high altar. Right behind it is the tomb of King Robert of Anjou himself, carved by Pacio and Giovanni Bertini in 1343. And behind the tomb is a wall. It hides the nuns' choir, with three screened grilles. Through them the nuns could follow the Mass, while nobody in the church could see them.","Much of what you see in here is a rebuilding. In 1943, Allied bombing and the fire that followed destroyed part of the church and much of its decoration. It was brought back to a Gothic look by a restoration finished in 1953, and that restoration is still argued over. But for Naples, rebuilding Santa Chiara became the symbol of the city's rebirth.","In the side chapels are royal tombs: the Bourbon king Francis the Second, and his queen, Maria Sophie. And there's a quieter grave: Salvo D'Acquisto, a carabiniere who gave his life to save 22 civilian hostages during the Nazi occupation. He's remembered as a national hero.","If it's open, go round to the cloister at the back. In 1742 Domenico Antonio Vaccaro covered the nuns' cloister in majolica tiles, bright flowers on octagonal columns. It seems almost too cheerful for an enclosed convent, and that's part of the surprise."]},{"name":"Along Spaccanapoli","minutes":2,"stand":"Walk east along Via Benedetto Croce and stop where you can look straight down the length of the street.","paras":["Look down the street. Spaccanapoli is long and narrow, and it crosses the old center from east to west, in a city of small, irregular alleys. Around you, the rectangular grid of ancient Greek Neapolis, the New City, can still be traced, and it still gives the old center its basic shape.","When was Neapolis founded? UNESCO dates it to 470 BC, refounded from an older settlement called Parthenope. Other accounts put the refounding in the 500s BC. Historians differ on the detail, but either way, the history of this old center spans more than 2,700 years.","Now look up. The buildings are tall and pressed together. In the Middle Ages the city was closed in by its walls, and building outside them was forbidden. So Naples grew upward instead of outward. Look at the stone, too: yellow tufa, white marble, and a grey stone called piperno, all local. The old ways of working them are still used in restoration today.","Under your feet there's a second city. Naples sits on tuff, and from its very beginning builders quarried their stone from underneath it. Later those hollows became part of the water system, then rubbish pits, and in the war, shelters. You can visit parts of them on underground tours.","The numbers in this old core are hard to take in. More than 200 historic churches. Their story is tied to artists like Giotto, Caravaggio, Donatello and Giuseppe Sanmartino. You can meet Sanmartino's most famous piece, the Veiled Christ, in the Sansevero Chapel."]},{"name":"San Domenico Maggiore","minutes":1,"stand":"In Piazza San Domenico Maggiore, facing the church.","paras":["This is San Domenico Maggiore, a Gothic church founded in 1283 around a smaller church from the 10th century. The great theologian Thomas Aquinas taught theology in the monastery here in 1272, and the philosopher Giordano Bruno lived here too.","Kings are here as well. In the sacristy are 45 tombs of the royal Aragonese family."]},{"name":"Via Duomo","minutes":1,"stand":"Keep walking east until the street meets the major cross street, Via Duomo. Stop on the sidewalk and turn to look back the way you came.","paras":["Here at Via Duomo, our street changes its name to Via Vicaria Vecchia and loses much of its character. So this is a good place to turn around and look back.","Via Duomo carries traffic. Stay on the sidewalk, and don't stop in the crossing.","UNESCO says what makes Naples special isn't only its monuments. It's that the city kept living in them: the same street pattern, the same buildings, many of the same everyday uses. Greek settlers drew the grid of this old center. And the people of Naples have kept walking it ever since."]}] },
  { id: "rome-colosseum", v: "1-b23082", city: /Rome|Roma|Civitavecchia|Vatican/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(colosseum|colosseo|roman forum|foro romano|palatine|arch of titus|arch of constantine|mamertine)\b/i, title: "Colosseum and Roman Forum", where: "Colosseum and Roman Forum", checked: "2026-11-22",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Rome","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Rome","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Colosseum","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colosseum","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Arch of Constantine","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arch_of_Constantine","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Arch of Titus","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arch_of_Titus","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Roman Forum","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Forum","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Palatine Hill","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palatine_Hill","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Mamertine Prison","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mamertine_Prison","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"King James Version (public domain), via bible-api.com","title":"","url":"https://bible-api.com/acts%2028%3A14-31?translation=kjv","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Outside the Colosseum","minutes":2,"stand":"Stand on the north side of the Colosseum, where the outer wall still rises to its full height, and look up at it.","paras":["Here it is, the Colosseum. Begun under the emperor Vespasian in 72, and finished in AD 80 under his son Titus, it's the largest amphitheatre the ancient world ever built.","A word before we start: expect lots of walking on uneven stone, often in full sun. Good shoes, slow steps, and water.","In summer especially, the heat makes long walks in the middle of the day hard going; early morning and evening are kinder. The old street fountains, the nasoni, give free spring water that's safe to drink, so fill a bottle.","This side, the north side, is the part of the outer wall still standing. The sloping brick wedges at each end aren't Roman; they were added in the early 1800s to hold it up.","And see all those holes pocking the stone? The blocks were once held together with iron clamps. In the Middle Ages people hacked the iron out, and these scars are what's left.","Here's an uncomfortable fact. The building was paid for with spoils taken from the Temple in Jerusalem, after the city fell in AD 70. A reconstructed inscription found here says Vespasian built it from his general's share of the booty. Keep that in mind; we'll see the same story carved in stone later on.","The site itself made a point. Nero had taken this ground for a private lake beside his palace. Vespasian filled the lake in, which can be seen as handing the land back to the people. Even the name Colosseum is believed to come from a giant bronze statue of Nero that stood next to it.","Why is so much of it missing? The great earthquake of 1349 brought down the outer south side, and the fallen stone was carted off to build palaces and churches all over Rome."]},{"name":"Inside the arena","minutes":2,"stand":"Go inside and find a spot along the rail where you can look down into the maze of walls under the old arena floor.","paras":["Imagine it full. An estimated 50,000 to 80,000 people sat around you, watching gladiators, animal hunts, executions, even re-enacted battles.","Those walls below you were hidden under a wooden floor covered in sand. The Latin word for sand is arena. The emperor Domitian added this underground world: two levels of tunnels and cages where gladiators and animals waited. Lifts and pulleys hoisted animals and scenery up into the light.","The scale of the killing is hard to take in. The historian Dio Cassius says over 9,000 wild animals died in the opening games alone.","On hot days there was shade. Sailors from the navy worked a vast awning, the velarium, to keep sun and rain off the crowd.","Ancient writers also say the arena was flooded for mock sea battles in the early years. Historians still debate how that could have worked.","Now the cross you may see standing here. Were Christians martyred in this arena? In 1749 Pope Benedict XIV declared that they were, and set up the Stations of the Cross. But historians find no evidence for it, beyond the plausible idea that some martyrs may have died here. Every Good Friday the pope still leads a torchlit Way of the Cross at the Colosseum.","The gladiator fights did end. The emperor Honorius banned gladiator fights in 399 and again in 404, and the last mention of them is around 435."]},{"name":"The Arch of Constantine","minutes":1,"stand":"Come back out and walk to the great three-way arch that stands between the Colosseum and the Palatine Hill.","paras":["This is the Arch of Constantine, the largest Roman triumphal arch. The Senate put it up for Constantine's victory over his rival Maxentius at the Milvian Bridge in 312, and dedicated it in 315.","Look closely at the carvings. Much of it is recycled: reliefs taken from monuments of Trajan, Hadrian and Marcus Aurelius, with the old emperors' heads recut to look like Constantine.","Up top, the inscription says he won inspired by the divine. Which divine? It doesn't say. Scholars read that vague phrase as a sign of his shifting faith, and it may have been worded to please every reader. Christian writers tell of a vision of God during the campaign."]},{"name":"The Arch of Titus","minutes":1,"stand":"Walk up the Via Sacra to the single arch; if you can, step into its passage, otherwise look in from the side.","paras":["This is the Arch of Titus, built by his brother Domitian around AD 81, to honor Titus and the crushing of the Jewish revolt in Judaea.","Look at the carved panel on the south wall of this passage. A procession carries the treasures of the Temple in Jerusalem through Rome in triumph: the golden menorah, the trumpets, and the Table of Showbread. The procession took place in AD 71.","On the opposite wall, the other panel shows Titus himself in his four-horse chariot, crowned by a winged Victory.","In 2012, researchers found traces of yellow ochre paint on the menorah. The treasures were probably once painted gold.","For Jews, this arch became a symbol of exile, and its menorah became the model for the emblem of the State of Israel. Rome's Chief Rabbinate once forbade Jews to walk underneath it. That ban was lifted after Israel was founded, and made public at Hanukkah in 1997."]},{"name":"The Roman Forum","minutes":2,"stand":"Walk on down the Via Sacra into the Forum and stop where you can see the long open valley of ruins ahead.","paras":["This valley, between the Palatine and Capitoline hills, was the Forum, for centuries the center of daily life in Rome: elections, speeches, trials, markets.","The stone road under your feet is the Via Sacra, where victorious generals paraded into the Forum. Out in the open square of the Forum, the last travertine paving, still visible, dates from Augustus. The ground is uneven everywhere here, so watch your step.","Hard to believe, but this was once a marsh, drained by Rome's great sewer, the Cloaca Maxima.","Somewhere ahead of you, perhaps the most famous funeral in Rome's history took place. In 44 BC Mark Antony spoke over Julius Caesar's body from the speakers' platform, the Rostra, and Caesar was burned on a spot across from it. Augustus later built the Temple of the Deified Caesar there.","Look for the eight columns standing together: they're all that's left of the Temple of Saturn, one of the oldest buildings here. It doubled as a bank. And at the far end, by the Capitoline, is the white marble Arch of Septimius Severus, dedicated in 203.","Why is so little standing? The worst of it came between 1540 and 1550, when Pope Paul III mined the Forum for stone to build the new Saint Peter's."]},{"name":"The Palatine Hill","minutes":1,"stand":"Find a spot where you can look up at the Palatine Hill rising above the Forum, or climb it if your ticket allows.","paras":["Above you is the Palatine, the middle one of Rome's seven hills. The emperors built their palaces up there, starting with Augustus, and our word palace comes from its name.","People have lived on it since the 900s BC. And in Roman myth, this is where the she-wolf found the twins Romulus and Remus in a cave, the Lupercal, and where Romulus later founded the city. It's legend, but it's the story Rome told about itself."]},{"name":"The Mamertine Prison","minutes":1,"stand":"At the far end of the Forum, look to the slope of the Capitoline Hill facing the old Senate House, where two churches stand over the ancient prison.","paras":["Under those churches is the Mamertine Prison, the old Tullianum. It held prisoners briefly before execution; the Gaulish leader Vercingetorix was executed here.","Tradition says Peter was held here, and that a spring rose miraculously so he could baptize. But, as the Catholic Encyclopedia points out, the spring was there long before, and there's little first-hand evidence for Peter's stay. Paul is said to have been held here too.","The Bible tells only this of Paul's time in Rome. Acts says: Paul dwelt two whole years in his own hired house, and received all that came in unto him."]},{"name":"Closing","minutes":1,"stand":"Turn and look back down the Forum toward the Colosseum.","paras":["Here's one last thing to carry home. When a death sentence anywhere in the world is lifted, or a country ends the death penalty, Rome lights the Colosseum gold instead of white. A building raised for killing now keeps watch for lives spared."]}] },
  { id: "rome-navona", v: "1-fbc670", city: /Rome|Roma|Civitavecchia|Vatican/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(navona)\b/i, title: "Piazza Navona", where: "Piazza Navona", checked: "2026-11-22",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Piazza Navona","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piazza_Navona","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Stadium of Domitian","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stadium_of_Domitian","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Sant'Agnese in Agone","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sant'Agnese_in_Agone","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Fontana dei Quattro Fiumi","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fontana_dei_Quattro_Fiumi","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Rome","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Rome","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"The stadium","minutes":1,"stand":"Stand in the middle of the square and look along its length.","paras":["Notice the shape of this square: long and narrow, like a racetrack. That's because you're standing in a Roman stadium. Piazza Navona sits on the Stadium of Domitian, begun in AD 80, and the square still follows its oval.","It was Rome's first permanent stadium for athletics, and it seated approximately 30,000 people. The buildings around you are built on the stadium's original lower arcades.","Even the name is a leftover. The Romans called the place in agone, the site of the games, and over time in agone became Navona.","One word of caution: if someone starts tying a bracelet on your wrist, pull your hand back. It's a known trick to ask for money."]},{"name":"The Fountain of the Four Rivers","minutes":2,"stand":"Walk to the great fountain in the center with the obelisk on top.","paras":["This is Bernini's Fountain of the Four Rivers, from 1651. Four giant river gods stand for four continents.","They are the Nile for Africa, the Danube for Europe, the Ganges for Asia, and the Río de la Plata for the Americas. On top of the obelisk sits a dove with an olive twig, the emblem of the pope's family, the Pamphili. Their palace faces the square, and it was Pope Innocent X who made this square a Baroque showpiece.","Walk around and find them. The Nile has his head covered, because back then nobody knew where the Nile began. The Río de la Plata sits on a pile of coins. The Ganges holds a long oar. And the Danube touches the pope's coat of arms.","Bernini almost didn't get the job. According to his biographer Baldinucci, he was left out of the competition, so a prince married to the pope's niece persuaded him to make a model, and had it placed secretly in a room the pope had to walk through. The pope stopped, almost in ecstasy, and said that whoever doesn't want to use Bernini's designs had better not look at them.","Not everyone loved it. It was built at public expense during a terrible famine, and protesters pinned notes on the obelisk's stone blocks: we don't want obelisks and fountains, we want bread."]},{"name":"Sant'Agnese in Agone","minutes":1,"stand":"Turn to face the church with the curved front and the dome, right behind the fountain.","paras":["Behind the fountain is the church of Sant'Agnese in Agone. Christian tradition says the martyr Agnes died here in the stadium.","Building began in 1652. The architect Borromini took over in 1653, but he resigned in 1657. Look at the curved front, with the dome framed between twin towers. Inside is a shrine of Saint Agnes said to hold her skull.","Now a famous story you'll hear from guides. Bernini and Borromini were bitter rivals, and the legend goes that the Nile on the fountain covers his eyes so he won't have to look at Borromini's church. It's a good story, but it's only a legend: the fountain was finished years before Borromini started on the church.","The real rivalry was sharp enough. When Bernini's bell towers at Saint Peter's ran into trouble, Borromini was one of the harsh critics, and the towers were torn down."]},{"name":"The two end fountains","minutes":1,"stand":"Look toward each end of the square in turn.","paras":["At the southern end is the Fountain of the Moor, and at the northern end the Fountain of Neptune.","On the Moor fountain, Bernini added the figure of a Moor wrestling a dolphin. Neptune, at the other end, only arrived in 1878.","This square has always been a stage. The city market moved here at the end of the 1400s and stayed until 1869. And from 1652 until 1866, the square was flooded every weekend in August for the Pamphili family's festivities. Today there's still a Christmas market here each year."]},{"name":"Closing","minutes":1,"stand":"Find a bench or a spot by the fountain and just watch the square for a moment.","paras":["Long ago, athletes competed here in front of a packed stadium. Today people stroll, eat ice cream, and argue about Bernini. The stadium is gone, but the crowd never really left."]}] },
  { id: "rome-pantheon", v: "1-4a3b59", city: /Rome|Roma|Civitavecchia|Vatican/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(pantheon|santa maria ad martyres|santa maria rotonda)\b/i, title: "The Pantheon", where: "The Pantheon", checked: "2026-11-22",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Pantheon, Rome","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pantheon%2C_Rome","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"The porch","minutes":1,"stand":"Stand in the square in front, where you can see the whole porch of columns and the words carved above them.","paras":["This is the Pantheon, a Roman temple first built under Augustus and rebuilt under Hadrian. Since AD 609 it has been a church, and that's why it's still standing.","Read the big letters above the columns. They say Marcus Agrippa, son of Lucius, made this when consul for the third time. But Agrippa's temple burned. This building is Hadrian's, probably dedicated around AD 126. Hadrian simply kept the old inscription, which is why the date is uncertain.","The name means temple of all the gods, though scholars aren't sure it ever really was one; it may have been just a nickname.","Those granite columns came from Egypt. Each is 39 feet tall and weighs 60 tonnes. They were dragged on sledges to the Nile, floated down by barge, shipped across the sea, and pulled up the Tiber.","Now the doors as you go in. These bronze doors are the originals, the oldest doors in Rome."]},{"name":"Under the dome","minutes":1,"stand":"Go in and stand in the middle of the floor, under the round opening, and look straight up.","paras":["Look up. Almost two thousand years old, this is still the largest unreinforced concrete dome in the world. From the floor to the opening is about 142 feet, 43 meters, exactly the width of the room, so a giant ball would fit inside perfectly.","That opening, the oculus, has never had glass. It and the door are the only natural light. Look for the circle of sunlight; through the day it travels round the room like a sundial in reverse.","And when it rains, it rains inside. Look down: the floor slopes about 12 inches toward drains. On Pentecost, firefighters on the roof pour red rose petals through the oculus.","How does it stay up? The concrete gets lighter as it rises, ending in light volcanic stone near the top, and the shell thins from 21 feet at the base to about 4 feet at the rim of the opening. Count the sunken squares, the coffers: five rings of 28. They may once have held bronze rosettes, like stars."]},{"name":"A temple becomes a church","minutes":1,"stand":"Stay inside, under the dome.","paras":["In 609 the emperor gave this building to Pope Boniface IV, who dedicated it on May 13 to Saint Mary and the Martyrs. Becoming a church saved it, when most of ancient Rome was being torn apart.","Even so, it was robbed. In 663 the emperor Constans II stripped the bronze roof tiles and shipped them off to Constantinople.","Later, Pope Urban VIII melted down the bronze beams of the porch. People said it went into Bernini's canopy at Saint Peter's, but his accounts show about 90 percent went into cannon, so that story is probably wrong. A Roman wit summed it up: what the barbarians did not do, the Barberini did. Barberini was the pope's family name.","It's still a working church, with Mass on Sundays, so keep your voice low."]},{"name":"Raphael's tomb","minutes":1,"stand":"From the entrance, walk round to your left until you are just past the tomb of King Umberto; Raphael is in the next niche.","paras":["Here lies the great painter Raphael. Beside him, to the right, is Maria Bibbiena, his fiancée, who died before they could marry.","His epitaph was written by the poet Pietro Bembo. It says that while Raphael lived, Nature feared he would outdo her, and when he died, she feared she would die with him.","Two kings of Italy are buried here too, Victor Emmanuel II and Umberto I.","There's a legend about this dome. In 1536 the emperor Charles V, whose armies had sacked Rome, wanted to climb up and look down through the oculus. His guide, the keeper's son, later said he felt a sudden urge to push him in."]},{"name":"Closing","minutes":1,"stand":"On your way out, stop in the doorway and look back up at the light.","paras":["Romans built this for their gods. Christians have prayed in it for over fourteen centuries. And the rain still comes straight in through the roof, as it always has. Stand here a moment and let the sky in."]}] },
  { id: "rome-stpaulwalls", v: "1-08625a", city: /Rome|Roma|Civitavecchia|Vatican/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(saint paul outside|st paul outside|san paolo fuori)\b/i, title: "Basilica of Saint Paul Outside the Walls", where: "Basilica of Saint Paul Outside the Walls", checked: "2026-11-22",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Basilica of Saint Paul Outside the Walls","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basilica_of_Saint_Paul_Outside_the_Walls","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","title":"Historic Centre of Rome, the Properties of the Holy See in that City Enjoying Extraterritorial Rights and San Paolo Fuori le Mura - UNESCO World Heritage Centre","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/91/","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"King James Version (public domain), via bible-api.com","title":"","url":"https://bible-api.com/acts%2028%3A14-31?translation=kjv","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Paul the Apostle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paul_the_Apostle","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"King James Version (public domain), via bible-api.com","title":"","url":"https://bible-api.com/2%20timothy%204%3A6-8?translation=kjv","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Saint Peter's tomb","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Peter's_tomb","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Arriving","minutes":1,"stand":"Stand in the great courtyard in front of the basilica, facing its façade.","paras":["This is the Basilica of Saint Paul Outside the Walls, one of the four great papal basilicas of Rome. It stands outside the old city walls on the spot that tradition holds to be the grave of the apostle Paul.","UNESCO lists it with the historic center of Rome as a World Heritage site.","How did Paul come to Rome? As a prisoner. The book of Acts, in its very last chapter, tells how believers came out to meet him. And when Paul saw them, Acts says, he thanked God, and took courage.","In Rome, Acts says, Paul was suffered to dwell by himself with a soldier that kept him. He called the chief of the Jews together and told them: for the hope of Israel I am bound with this chain.","And then the book closes with these words: And Paul dwelt two whole years in his own hired house, and received all that came in unto him, preaching the kingdom of God, and teaching those things which concern the Lord Jesus Christ, with all confidence, no man forbidding him."]},{"name":"Scripture and tradition","minutes":1,"stand":"Stay in the courtyard, or step under the porch, and take a moment here.","paras":["Here's the important part. Acts ends with Paul alive and preaching. It does not tell us how he died. Everything after that comes from tradition.","Writers like Eusebius say he was killed under the emperor Nero, and Jerome says he was beheaded in Rome and buried on the Ostian Way, the road this church stands on. It's believed to have happened sometime between the Great Fire of AD 64 and 68.","There's also a later legend that when Paul was beheaded his head bounced three times, and a spring rose at each spot. That's how the place got its name, Saint Paul at the Three Fountains.","There is a letter that speaks as if from Paul's last days, the second letter to Timothy. Many scholars think it was written in Paul's name by someone else. But listen to its words: I am now ready to be offered, and the time of my departure is at hand. I have fought a good fight, I have finished my course, I have kept the faith."]},{"name":"The nave","minutes":1,"stand":"Go inside and stand at the back of the central aisle, looking down the rows of columns toward the altar.","paras":["Constantine built the first church over Paul's grave, consecrated in 324. Then from 386 the emperor Theodosius built a far bigger one. For a time it was larger even than the old Saint Peter's.","What you see, though, is mostly new. On July 15, 1823, a workman repairing the roof gutters started a fire that nearly destroyed the whole basilica. It was rebuilt with gifts from all over: the Viceroy of Egypt sent alabaster columns, the Emperor of Russia malachite and lapis lazuli. It was reconsecrated in 1854, and it's only broadly like the original.","It's about 432 feet long, the second largest church in Rome, with 80 columns in the nave.","Look at the windows. They look like stained glass, but they're thin translucent alabaster.","And look above the columns: a long row of portraits, one for every pope. These are 19th-century mosaic versions of an old painted series."]},{"name":"The arch and the altar","minutes":1,"stand":"Walk down the nave to the great arch in front of the altar and look up at its mosaics.","paras":["The mosaics on this great arch are from the 400s, one of the few things the fire spared, though heavily reworked. Find Peter on the right and Paul on the left. Paul is pointing downward, probably to his tomb.","The canopy over the altar is by Arnolfo di Cambio, from 1285.","Below the altar lies the tomb. On a marble slab are the Latin words Paulo Apostolo Mart: to Paul, apostle and martyr. One round hole in the stone leads down to the tomb, perhaps for pouring perfume or offerings, as Romans did."]},{"name":"What the archaeologists found","minutes":1,"stand":"Go down to the level beneath the altar, if it is open, where the side of the sarcophagus can be seen through a window.","paras":["In 2006, Vatican archaeologists confirmed a white marble sarcophagus beneath the altar, which may hold the apostle's remains. A window was cut so pilgrims can see it.","In 2009 Pope Benedict XVI announced that bone fragments inside had been carbon dated to the first or second century. But the head of the Vatican Museums' laboratory urged caution: the dating, he said, neither confirms nor rules out that they are Paul's. So nobody knows for sure.","Church tradition adds that Paul's head is kept separately, at the Lateran.","The memory of this grave is very old. Around the year 200, a Roman Christian named Caius wrote that he could point out the trophies of the apostles, at the Vatican and on the Ostian Way. Tradition says the burial ground belonged to a Christian woman named Lucina, and that a memorial, a trophy, stood here and quickly became a place of veneration."]},{"name":"The cloister and closing","minutes":1,"stand":"If you have time, follow the signs to the cloister and stand among its columns.","paras":["The cloister, built between 1205 and 1241, is often called one of the most beautiful of the Middle Ages. Look at the columns: paired, in different shapes, some inlaid with gold and colored glass.","A prisoner came to Rome in chains, and Acts leaves him in a rented house, welcoming everyone, no man forbidding him. Whatever the bones may be, that is how the Bible chose to leave him: still talking, still open-handed."]}] },
  { id: "rome-trevi", v: "1-7d4076", city: /Rome|Roma|Civitavecchia|Vatican/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(trevi|fontana di trevi)\b/i, title: "Trevi Fountain", where: "Trevi Fountain", checked: "2026-11-22",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Trevi Fountain","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trevi_Fountain","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Rome","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Rome","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Arriving","minutes":1,"stand":"Come into the little square and find a spot with a clear view of the fountain.","paras":["And suddenly, there it is: the Trevi Fountain, tucked into this small square among the side streets. It's the largest Baroque fountain in the world, 86 feet high and about 161 feet wide, finished in 1762.","Crowds gather here, and so do pickpockets. Keep your bag zipped and in front of you.","To go down to the water's edge and throw a coin, visitors now usually need a 2-euro ticket during the day; late at night the barriers open. Check on the day.","The name comes from the Latin trivium, the meeting of three streets."]},{"name":"Reading the fountain","minutes":1,"stand":"Stay facing the center of the fountain.","paras":["The theme is the taming of the waters. In the central niche stands Oceanus, by the sculptor Pietro Bracci, on a shell chariot, with tritons wrestling sea horses below him.","On either side of him are two figures. One is Abundance, pouring water from her urn. The other is Salubrity, health, holding a cup that a snake drinks from.","This water has a long history. The fountain is the end of an aqueduct that goes back to 19 BC, the Aqua Virgo, and it is still in use today.","Look up at the carved panels above: they show how the aqueduct began. Legend has it that a virgin helped Roman engineers find the spring."]},{"name":"How it was built","minutes":1,"stand":"Step a little to one side so you can see the whole width of the palace behind the fountain.","paras":["The fountain is built right onto the back of a palace, the Palazzo Poli. It took more than a century of tries to get it built.","In 1629 Pope Urban VIII asked Bernini for ideas, but the pope died and the plan was dropped. Then in 1730 there was a contest. Nicola Salvi lost, to a man from Florence, and Romans were so outraged that Salvi got the job anyway. Work began in 1732. Salvi died in 1751 with the fountain half done, and Giuseppe Pannini finished it.","Most of it is travertine, a pale stone quarried near Tivoli, about 22 miles east of Rome. Pannini changed the plan at the end: instead of statues of Agrippa and of Trivia, the Roman virgin, he put in the figures you see now. Pope Clement XIII opened it on May 22, 1762.","A barber's ugly shop sign would have spoiled the effect, so Salvi hid it behind a big sculpted vase. Romans call it the Ace of Cups."]},{"name":"The coin","minutes":1,"stand":"Turn so your back is to the fountain.","paras":["Now the tradition. Throw a coin into the fountain before you leave Rome, and it's believed you'll come back. You throw it with your back to the water, right hand over your left shoulder.","By one estimate, some 3,000 euros go in every day, and the money is given to the charity Caritas. So your wish helps someone else.","Some people try to fish the coins out. It's illegal, and it happens regularly anyway.","The night lighting owes something to the fashion house Fendi, which paid for a 20-month restoration, the most thorough in the fountain's history. It was finished in November 2015 and added more than 100 LED lights.","And it has its own music. Respighi's 1916 piece Fountains of Rome includes a movement called The Trevi Fountain at Noon.","The fountain is a film star too: in Roman Holiday, in Three Coins in the Fountain, and in La Dolce Vita, where Anita Ekberg wades in with Marcello Mastroianni."]},{"name":"Closing","minutes":1,"stand":"Take one last look before you go.","paras":["Everyone here is hoping for the same small thing: to come back. Whether or not the coin works, it's a good wish to make."]}] },
  { id: "rome-vatican", v: "1-b101df", city: /Rome|Roma|Civitavecchia|Vatican/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(st peter|st\. peter|saint peter|san pietro|vatican)\b/i, title: "Saint Peter's Basilica and Square", where: "Saint Peter's Basilica and Square", checked: "2026-11-22",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"St. Peter's Square","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St._Peter's_Square","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"St. Peter's Basilica","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St._Peter's_Basilica","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Rome/Vatican","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Vatican_City","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","title":"Vatican City - UNESCO World Heritage Centre","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/286/","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Pietà (Michelangelo)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piet%C3%A0_(Michelangelo)","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"St. Peter's Baldachin","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St._Peter's_Baldachin","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Paul the Apostle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paul_the_Apostle","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Saint Peter's tomb","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Peter's_tomb","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Vatican Museums","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vatican_Museums","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Sistine Chapel","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sistine_Chapel","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Sistine Chapel ceiling","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sistine_Chapel_ceiling","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"The square","minutes":1,"stand":"Stand near the obelisk in the middle of the square and face the basilica.","paras":["Here we are in Saint Peter's Square. Those great curving rows of columns on both sides are Bernini's. He called them the maternal arms of Mother Church, reaching out to embrace whoever arrives.","Count how deep they are: four columns. And the square had a practical purpose too. It was laid out so the greatest number of people could see the Pope give his blessing.","Now the obelisk beside you. It's Egyptian red granite, with no hieroglyphs on it, first raised in Heliopolis by a pharaoh nobody can name. The emperor Caligula brought it to Rome in AD 37 for the Circus of Nero, whose ruins lie under the basilica. It was moved to this spot in 1586.","Tradition says Peter was crucified near this obelisk, so it's revered as a witness to his death.","Walk toward the steps. Saint Peter stands on the left of the stairs and Saint Paul on the other side, the two apostles of Rome. Up along the top of the façade are Christ, John the Baptist and eleven apostles. Peter isn't up there; he's down here with you.","One practical word: shoulders and knees need to be covered to go in."]},{"name":"The basilica and the Pietà","minutes":2,"stand":"Go in through the doors and turn right to the first chapel on that side.","paras":["You're now inside the largest church in the world, measured inside.","The first church here was Constantine's, in the fourth century. This one was begun in 1506 and finished in 1626.","Its main designers were Bramante, Michelangelo and Maderno, with Bernini doing the square and much of the furnishing. And before you came in, if you glanced at the door at the northern end of the porch, the far right as you face the church, that's the Holy Door. By tradition it stays walled up and opens only in a jubilee year.","Part of the money came from indulgences granted in return for donations. One preacher, Johann Tetzel, pushed them so hard that his salesmanship caused a scandal, and that scandal helped set off the Protestant Reformation.","Here in the first chapel on the right is Michelangelo's Pietà: Mary holding her son, just taken down from the cross.","He carved it in 1498 and 1499, for a French cardinal's funeral chapel in the old basilica.","Look at Mary's face. She looks younger than her son, and nobody knows for sure why. Michelangelo told his biographer it showed her purity; others point to a line in Dante: Virgin mother, daughter of your son.","Now find the band across her chest. That's his signature, the only work he ever signed. According to the writer Vasari, he heard visitors credit it to another sculptor and carved his name into it. Vasari adds that he later regretted the pride, and swore never to sign another.","The glass is there because in 1972 someone attacked Mary's figure."]},{"name":"Under the dome","minutes":2,"stand":"Walk up the middle of the nave and stop in the open space under the dome, facing the bronze canopy.","paras":["Near the end of the nave, against the great pillar on your right as you reach the dome, sits a bronze Saint Peter on a throne. Scholars disagree about its age; some say the late 1200s, some the 400s. Look at his foot, worn smooth by centuries of pilgrims' kisses.","Now look up. Michelangelo took charge of this building in 1547, in his seventies. He wrote that he did it only for the love of God and in honor of the Apostle. He died in 1564, and others finished the dome in 1590.","Around the inside of the dome runs a line in Latin from Matthew chapter 16. In English it says: you are Peter, and on this rock I will build my church, and I will give you the keys of the kingdom of heaven. The name Peter itself means rock; it's the Greek for Kepa, the name Jesus gave to Simon.","Beneath it stands Bernini's bronze canopy over the high altar, built to mark Saint Peter's tomb below. Pope Urban VIII commissioned it, and it took from 1623 to 1634.","It's about 94 feet tall, nearly 29 meters, and claimed to be the largest piece of bronze in the world. Look for bees and laurel leaves on it: the emblems of the pope's family, the Barberini.","The twisted columns copy ancient marble ones from the old church, which by the Middle Ages people believed had come from the Temple in Jerusalem. In fact they're Greek marble. Eight of the originals are still here, in pairs halfway up the great pillars around you.","And the bronze? People believed it was stripped from the Pantheon. But the pope's accounts say most of the Pantheon's bronze went into cannon, and the canopy's came from Venice. So the old story is probably wrong.","If you're going to climb the dome, be ready for a lot of stairs; the last stretch is a spiral staircase barely as wide as your shoulders."]},{"name":"Peter's tomb","minutes":2,"stand":"Go to the rail in front of the high altar and look down into the sunken space below it.","paras":["Below you, two curving staircases lead down to the Confession, a chapel just above the place where Peter is said to be buried.","Let's be careful here. The New Testament does not mention Peter's death in Rome. That comes from tradition, from the early Church writers, and tradition holds that his tomb lies right below this altar.","The Bible does show us the man himself: the book of Acts has Peter, the fisherman from Galilee, leading the first followers of Jesus.","Tradition also says he died under the emperor Nero, after the Great Fire of Rome, and Jerome says Peter asked to be crucified head downwards, feeling unworthy to die the way Jesus did.","The memory is old. Around the year 200, a Roman Christian named Caius wrote that he could show the trophies, the memorials, of the apostles, at the Vatican and on the Ostian Way. The Ostian Way is where Saint Paul's church stands today.","Then the archaeologists came. From 1939, digs under this floor found a shrine from the second century, and bones. In 1950 Pope Pius XII said none could be confirmed as Peter's with absolute certainty. In 1968 Pope Paul VI called the identification of other bones convincing. But Antonio Ferrua, who led the dig, said he was not convinced. Historians still disagree."]},{"name":"The Vatican Museums and the Sistine Chapel","minutes":1,"stand":"If your visit includes the museums, play this as you set off; the Sistine Chapel is the last room on the route.","paras":["The Vatican Museums hold roughly 70,000 works, with about 20,000 on show, and the Sistine Chapel comes at the very end of the route.","The chapel is named for Pope Sixtus IV, who built it between 1473 and 1481, and it's still where cardinals elect a new pope. White smoke from the chimney means a pope; black means nobody has two-thirds of the vote yet.","Inside, keep quiet, and no photos; the guards will remind you.","Look up at the middle of the ceiling: nine scenes from Genesis, among them the Creation of Adam, painted by Michelangelo between 1508 and 1512. He painted standing, his head tilted back, not lying down.","Now the wall behind the altar: The Last Judgment, from 1535 to 1541. Christ is high on the wall. At the bottom left the dead rise from their graves; on the right the damned are dragged down.","Two faces to find. The papal master of ceremonies, Biagio da Cesena, called all the nudes disgraceful, so Michelangelo gave his face to Minos, judge of the underworld. It is said the pope told Biagio his authority didn't reach as far as hell. And Michelangelo painted himself on the flayed skin held by Saint Bartholomew."]},{"name":"Closing","minutes":1,"stand":"Wherever you finish your visit, pause for a moment before you leave.","paras":["Ancient Christians chose to bury their dead here because they believed Peter lay here, long before this dome. Everything you've seen today grew from that remembering."]}] },
  { id: "santorini-akrotiri", v: "1-042154", city: /Santorini|Thira|Fira/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(akrotiri)\b/i, title: "Akrotiri Prehistoric Town", where: "Akrotiri Prehistoric Town", checked: "2026-11-22",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Akrotiri (prehistoric city)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Akrotiri_(prehistoric_city)","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Santorini","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Santorini","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Santorini","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Santorini","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Minoan eruption","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minoan_eruption","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Akrotiri Boxer Fresco","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frescoes_of_Akrotiri","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Spyridon Marinatos","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spyridon_Marinatos","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Arrival","minutes":1,"stand":"Just inside the covered site, at the first point where you can look down over the buried streets.","paras":["Welcome to Akrotiri, a Bronze Age town buried in volcanic ash about 3,600 years ago. Like Pompeii, it's remarkably well preserved.","Here's something to start with: nobody knows what its people called it. Akrotiri is just the name of the modern village nearby.","The huge roof over your head covers the whole site, which is why you can visit even in the summer heat. And what you see is only the southern tip of a much larger town. The rest is still under the ground around you.","Watch your step on the walkways and any stairs, and take your time."]},{"name":"A town that traded","minutes":1,"stand":"Move along the walkway until you can see down into a street lined with walls.","paras":["This was a busy port. It sat on the main sailing route between Cyprus and Minoan Crete, and it became a center for working copper.","It started small. People lived here as early as the fifth millennium BC, in a fishing and farming village. Over time it grew into one of the major Bronze Age ports of the Aegean. Objects found here came from Crete, from Anatolia, Cyprus, Syria and Egypt.","Strictly, this was a Cycladic town, not a Cretan one. But its writing, Linear A, and the style of its art tie it closely to the Minoan civilization of Crete."]},{"name":"Walking the streets","minutes":1,"stand":"Stay where you can see down into the streets and the house walls.","paras":["Look down at the houses. Some stood three stories high, and walls here still stand as tall as eight meters. Many of the stone staircases are still intact.","This was a sophisticated place: paved streets and an extensive drainage system. There were pipes with running water, and water closets. And here's the remarkable bit: the pipes run in pairs, which suggests both hot and cold water. The hot water probably came from the volcano itself.","Inside the houses, the excavators found huge storage jars, called pithoi, along with mills and pottery. Where wooden furniture rotted away inside the ash, it left a hollow, a perfect mold. Pour in liquid plaster, and out comes a cast of a bed, a table or a stool.","Some things remain a mystery. In one building, the House of the Benches, they found between 300 and 500 animal horns, painted with ochre patterns. What they were used for in worship, nobody knows."]},{"name":"Where did everyone go?","minutes":1,"stand":"Pause anywhere along the walkway with a view down into the houses.","paras":["Now the question everyone asks. Where are the people? Unlike Pompeii, no skeletons lie in these streets, and only a single gold object has been found. It looks like the town was evacuated in an orderly way.","Scholars think the volcano gave a few months' warning. Earthquakes may have damaged the town first. They had time to get out, but not to take much: only their most valuable things. That's why so much pottery stayed behind.","There's even evidence that survivors came back afterward, to recover their belongings and perhaps to bury their dead.","And when did it happen? The exact year isn't known. Radiocarbon dating places it most probably between 1620 and 1530 BC."]},{"name":"The painted walls","minutes":1,"stand":"Stand where you have a good view of the larger houses.","paras":["The ash also saved its wall paintings, far better preserved than those from Knossos on Crete. They're no longer on these walls: the finds were taken to a museum away from the site, the Museum of Prehistoric Thera, in Fira.","Their scenes are full of life: two antelopes, a man holding a catch of fish, a flotilla of boats with leaping dolphins, and girls gathering saffron for a seated woman, perhaps a goddess.","The painters worked in white, yellow, red, brown, blue and black, colors that seem to come from minerals. In 1969, the Blue Monkeys were found in a room the excavators called Beta 6. The rocks the monkeys are climbing look just like the volcanic rocks around this site. In 1970, the Spring Fresco turned up in Room Delta 2, the first fresco here found perfectly preserved and still standing where it was painted.","There's also a fresco of two young boys wearing boxing gloves, found in Building Beta. Was it a real match, or just practice? Nobody knows."]},{"name":"The man who found it","minutes":2,"stand":"Stay where you are.","paras":["The great excavation here was begun in 1967 by the Greek archaeologist Spyridon Marinatos.","He wasn't the first to dig on the island. The French geologist Fouque dug on Santorini in 1867, after local people found old objects in a quarry.","Marinatos came with a theory: that this eruption had destroyed the Minoan palace civilization on Crete. He wanted to test it. The town lay under about 46 meters of pumice, so he tunneled in from a gully.","Within five days of starting on 24 May, he found the remains of a building in the Minoan style, with forty local pumice miners helping him dig.","History has been mixed on his theory. Scholars now think the eruption came several generations before the end of Minoan palace society on Crete. And his other famous idea, that the eruption lies behind Plato's Atlantis, isn't supported by current scholarship either.","He was a complicated man, too. He was an enthusiastic supporter of Greece's military junta, and his leadership of the Archaeological Service was criticized for cronyism.","In 1974, Marinatos died here, while excavating. Nobody's sure how: reports at the time said he fell, and a later story said a wall collapsed on him. Christos Doumas took over the dig.","Marinatos was first buried on the site, near where he died. His grave was later moved outside the ruins.","Work has not always been easy. In 2005 a new protective roof collapsed just before it was finished, killing one visitor, and the site stayed closed until April 2012. And discoveries go on: in 2018, a small shrine with a marble figurine of a woman."]},{"name":"Closing","minutes":1,"stand":"Take one last look down over the streets before you leave.","paras":["Picture the last morning here. People closing their doors, choosing what to carry, leaving the rest behind. Walking these streets, it feels as if they just stepped out for a moment."]}] },
  { id: "santorini-fira", v: "1-87325c", city: /Santorini|Thira|Fira/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(fira|fira caldera)\b/i, title: "Fira and the Caldera Path", where: "Fira and the Caldera Path", checked: "2026-11-22",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Santorini","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Santorini","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Nea Kameni","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nea_Kameni","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Fira","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fira","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Santorini","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Santorini","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Minoan eruption","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minoan_eruption","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Atlantis","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlantis","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Arrival","minutes":1,"stand":"On the caldera path in the center of Fira, at a low wall with a clear view out over the water. Stay on the path side of the wall.","paras":["Here we are, on the rim of a volcano. Fira is Santorini's capital, and its white houses sit right on the edge of the caldera, the great flooded crater in front of you.","Please stay back from the edges. The drops are long and some walls are low, so keep children close.","And the sun here is fierce, even when the breeze feels cool. Dehydration and sunburn are the most common health problems on the island. So keep drinking water, and find shade in the middle of the day.","The name is worth a moment. Fira simply comes from another way of saying Thira, the island's ancient name."]},{"name":"Reading the caldera","minutes":1,"stand":"Stay at the wall, looking straight out to sea across the caldera.","paras":["Look out across the water. The caldera stretches from Cape Akrotiri far to your left to Cape Agios Nikolaos far to your right. The dark island in the middle is Nea Kameni.","Across the water is the island of Thirassia. And if there are big cruise ships out there, they're anchored in the small harbor between Nea Kameni and Fira.","Now look down at the cliff itself. This inner wall of the caldera drops more than 300 meters at its highest, and you can see the layers of old lava stacked on top of each other, with the towns perched along the top. Each band is a page in the volcano's diary."]},{"name":"The great eruption","minutes":2,"stand":"Keep the open water in front of you.","paras":["How did this happen? About 3,600 years ago, one of the largest volcanic eruptions in human history struck this island, and left behind this huge bowl, flooded by the sea.","It was centered on a small island just north of where Nea Kameni sits today. Before it, this was almost a closed ring of land, with only one way in for boats. The eruption broke through on either side of Thirassia and opened two new channels to the sea.","And it wasn't the first time. Again and again over a very long time, this volcano erupted, then collapsed into a roughly circular basin full of seawater.","On Santorini, the white ash and pumice it threw out lies up to 60 meters thick, right on top of the old ground. Tsunamis struck the north coast of Crete, 110 kilometers away. How high they were, scholars still debate; the estimates vary widely.","Here's the mercy in the story. The volcano seems to have given a few months' warning. No human remains have been found in the buried town at Akrotiri, so the people probably got away in time. The exact year? Nobody knows. Scientists have argued about it for decades.","You'll hear the word Atlantis a lot here. Atlantis is a legend, a fictional island in the writings of Plato. Spyridon Marinatos, who found the buried town at Akrotiri, suggested this eruption lies behind it, but scholars today don't support that.","Scholars do still debate what inspired Plato's story. Some point to Egyptian records of this eruption; others reject that link entirely.","You may also hear the eruption linked to the Bible. One geologist, Barbara Sivertsen, has proposed a connection with the plagues of the Exodus. That's a disputed theory, not what Scripture itself says, and current archaeological research doesn't support such stories."]},{"name":"Nea Kameni, the volcano still breathing","minutes":2,"stand":"Look back out to the dark island in the middle of the caldera.","paras":["Nea Kameni means new burnt. It's the young volcano, grown out of the sea over the last two thousand years, eruption by eruption.","People watched it happen. The first of the Kameni islands broke the surface in 197 BC. Writing about the year 47, the Roman historian Cassius Dio recorded: \"This year a small islet, hitherto unknown, made an appearance close to the island of Thera.\" His islet may have been Palea Kameni.","A later writer, George Cedrenus, describes how in the summer of 727 \"a vapour like an oven's fire\" boiled up for days from the sea, \"and the whole place burned like fire.\" People read it as a sign from God against the worship of icons, and Emperor Leo the Third used it to justify his campaign against them.","The island you see began to take its present shape in 1707, when an undersea volcano broke the surface. The last small eruption was in 1950. It's quiet now, but steam and carbon dioxide still rise from the active crater, and scientists watch it closely.","One steaming vent is nicknamed Jorgo, George, after King George the First, because it was active the day after the king visited. If you take a boat out, a gravel path climbs to the top of the crater, about 130 meters high."]},{"name":"Fira's own story","minutes":2,"stand":"Turn to face the town, with the caldera at your back.","paras":["Fira has known hard times. In 1956 an earthquake destroyed 529 houses on Santorini, and camps for the homeless went up on the edge of Fira.","It's a town of churches too, among them the Cathedral of Ypapanti and the famous Three Bells of Fira. Nobody's quite sure why the Three Bells became so popular, since plenty of churches hang their bells the same way. The spot, up on the caldera ridge, may have something to do with it.","Below us, a zigzag footpath and a steep cable car link the town to the port. The path is 588 steps, winding, narrow in places, with low walls, and the donkeys won't step aside for you. Go slowly, and give the donkeys room.","If you walk the path north toward Oia, just above Fira is Imerovigli, the highest point of the caldera cliffs. The full walk can be hard going, with climbs, rocky stretches and unprotected edges. Don't linger under the cliffs, which are prone to rockslides.","One last picture. In the early 1950s, visitors came here a few at a time, on yachts. When they left, the island's children lit small lanterns all along the steps from Fira down to the port, to wish them a good voyage.","A little line of light on the side of a volcano. Hold onto that one when you go back down."]}] },
  { id: "santorini-oia", v: "1-6851d2", city: /Santorini|Thira|Fira/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(oia)\b/i, title: "Oia", where: "Oia", checked: "2026-11-22",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Oia, Greece","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oia%2C_Greece","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Santorini","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Santorini","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Santorini","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Santorini","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Arrival","minutes":1,"stand":"On the main cobbled lane, Nikolaou Nomikou, at a spot where you can step aside and see over the caldera.","paras":["Welcome to Oia, at the northwestern tip of Santorini. This cobbled lane is its main street, Nikolaou Nomikou.","The lanes are narrow and crowded, and the walls above the drops are low. Step aside when you stop, and keep children close.","The lane winds along the rim of the caldera. Oia has a nickname, the Eagle's Nest. Out over the water you can see the two volcanic islands, Palia and Nea Kameni, and the island of Therasia.","The name itself has wandered. For centuries this place was called Apano Meria, the upper side. It only became Oia in the later 1800s. The ancient Oia was somewhere else entirely, a harbor in the southeast of the island, where Kamari is now."]},{"name":"The captains' village","minutes":1,"stand":"Keep walking along the lane toward the higher part of the village, and stop where the larger, grander houses begin.","paras":["Oia was a sailors' town. In the late 1800s its merchant ships traded across the eastern Mediterranean, from Alexandria to Russia.","In 1890 it had about 2,500 people and about 130 sailing ships. The wealthy captains built themselves neo-classical mansions, and their two-story houses up on the highest part of the village are still a reminder of that money.","One of those mansions, a block back from the cliff-top, is now the maritime museum. Inside are ship figureheads, sailors' sea chests and models of island ships. And it wasn't only ships: fine wine from the farmland behind the village went out to France and beyond.","Then the world changed. Steamships came, shipping gathered at Piraeus near Athens, and Oia's sea trade collapsed.","In the early 1900s wars, economic depression and overfishing hit the town too. And people moved away, many of them to Piraeus."]},{"name":"Earthquake and rebirth","minutes":1,"stand":"Find a place along the lane where you can look down at the houses dug into the cliff below.","paras":["In 1956 an earthquake destroyed part of the town. At Oia, parts of the ground fell into the sea.","People left. By 1977 Oia had only 306 inhabitants. Imagine these lanes nearly empty.","But help was already on its way. In 1976, Oia was taken into a program of the Greek National Tourism Organisation to preserve traditional villages, under Aris Konstantinidis. The simple seamen's houses on the cliff edge became guest houses, hotels and restaurants. The work won the Europa Nostra Prize in 1979.","And the visitors came. Oia became known as a picture-perfect town, crowded every summer. Filmmakers noticed as well: Oia was the center of the story in the 1982 film Summer Lovers."]},{"name":"Cave houses and white walls","minutes":1,"stand":"Stay looking down at the cliff houses.","paras":["Look at the houses below you, carved into the cliff. The pumice around them is full of air, so they stay warm in winter and cool in summer.","Many of these cave houses were homes for the ships' crews. And why so white? Explanations differ. One is practical: lime wash helps rainwater run off the roofs to be collected. Another is simply that it looks beautiful. And there's a story that under Ottoman rule Greeks couldn't fly their flag, so in Oia they painted the whole town white in defiance.","Notice the churches too. Oia has many restored churches, including the Panagia church. Some were built in memory of sailors."]},{"name":"The castle and the sunset","minutes":1,"stand":"Walk to the end of the village, near the ruined castle, and find a spot facing west over the water.","paras":["This is Oia's ruined castle, Fort Londsa, a lookout with a view all the way around.","It was part of the same Venetian defense system as the castle at Pyrgos, and once one of the strongest fortresses on the island. The Venetian age here began in 1207, when Marco Sanudo founded the Duchy of Naxos, and Oia was home to one of the island's five local citadels.","Look for the old windmill too. It's one of the most recognizable buildings in Oia, and it turns up on a lot of postcards.","North of the castle, steep stairs lead down to the little harbor of Ammoudi, about 300 steps. It's a long way back up, so save your legs if the day is hot, and carry water. The sun here is strong, and dehydration and sunburn are the island's most common health problems.","Down at Ammoudi there are a few tavernas and a pebble beach, right on the water.","And at sunset, all of Oia fills with people. For a few seconds, the white houses and the whole caldera catch the colors of the setting sun."]},{"name":"Closing","minutes":1,"stand":"Stay where you are, facing the water.","paras":["Look around at all these strangers from everywhere, standing together by the old walls and going quiet for the same few seconds. That shared quiet may be the best thing Oia gives anyone."]}] },
  { id: "santorini-prehistmus", v: "1-7a9184", city: /Santorini|Thira|Fira/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(prehistoric thera)\b/i, title: "Museum of Prehistoric Thera", where: "Museum of Prehistoric Thera", checked: "2026-11-22",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Fira","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fira","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Museum of Prehistoric Thera","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Museum_of_Prehistoric_Thera","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Santorini","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Santorini","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Akrotiri (prehistoric city)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Akrotiri_(prehistoric_city)","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Akrotiri Boxer Fresco","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frescoes_of_Akrotiri","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Santorini","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Santorini","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Arrival","minutes":1,"stand":"Outside the museum entrance.","paras":["This is the Museum of Prehistoric Thera. It tells the story of the Bronze Age town at Akrotiri, the town buried by the volcano, and it holds a very large number of finds from digs all over Santorini.","It sits at the southeast corner of the Cathedral of Ypapanti. And it stands where the old Ypapanti Church stood until the 1956 earthquake destroyed it.","Don't mix it up with Fira's other museum, the Archaeological Museum of Thera. This is the prehistoric one."]},{"name":"The people who dug","minutes":1,"stand":"Just inside, before the first cases.","paras":["Digging on Santorini began with a French geologist, F. Fouque, in 1867, after local people found old objects in a quarry. From 1895 to 1900, the German Baron Hiller von Gaertringen uncovered the later city of ancient Thera, up on the mountain called Mesa Vouno.","Much of what you'll see here comes from Akrotiri, where the great modern excavation was begun by Spyridon Marinatos in 1967, under the Archaeological Society of Athens.","The town was at its best in the 1600s BC, and the finds from that time are shown here in abundance."]},{"name":"From the first potters","minutes":1,"stand":"At the earliest cases, where the displays begin.","paras":["The rooms run in time order, from the Late Neolithic onward. So as you walk, you move forward through the island's past.","Look for the early pottery and the Early Cycladic figurines carved in marble.","And look for the bird jugs, often painted with swallows. They date from the 20th to the 18th century BC, and were found at Ftellos, Megalochori and Akrotiri. Swallows turn up again and again.","Along the way, the museum also explains how far this little island's contacts reached in the outside world."]},{"name":"The painted walls","minutes":1,"stand":"In the rooms with the wall paintings.","paras":["And now the wall paintings. This museum shows the art of wall painting in great detail. Akrotiri's frescoes survived far better than the ones from Knossos on Crete, because the volcanic ash helped preserve them.","We don't know which paintings are on display today, so ask the staff. But here's what the excavators found at Akrotiri: two antelopes, a fisherman holding his catch, a fleet of boats with leaping dolphins, and girls gathering saffron for a seated woman, perhaps a goddess.","The painters used white, yellow, red, brown, blue and black. They started on wet plaster but finished on dry, so in places the paint soaked in, and in others it flakes off easily.","One famous painting shows two young boys wearing boxing gloves and belts, a little over life-size, wherever it hangs today. The version usually shown has large sections reconstructed by archaeologists, which you can tell by their smooth texture. Was it a real fight, or just sport? Nobody knows."]},{"name":"Closing","minutes":1,"stand":"Before you leave, near the last displays.","paras":["One more thing to carry with you. At Akrotiri, almost no gold was found, just a single object, and no bodies at all. The people seem to have left in good order.","So Akrotiri's real treasure isn't gold. It's a swallow on a jug, two boys in boxing gloves, a girl gathering saffron. Somehow, that tells us more about these people than gold could."]}] },
  { id: "santorini-pyrgos", v: "1-e1dd3d", city: /Santorini|Thira|Fira/i, site: /^(?!.*\bhotel\b).*\b(pyrgos)\b/i, title: "Pyrgos Village", where: "Pyrgos Village", checked: "2026-11-22",
    sources: [{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Pyrgos Kallistis","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pyrgos_Kallistis","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Santorini","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Santorini","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikivoyage","title":"Santorini","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Santorini","when":"2026-11-22"},{"pub":"Wikipedia","title":"Oia, Greece","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oia%2C_Greece","when":"2026-11-22"}],
    parts: [{"name":"Arrival","minutes":1,"stand":"At the foot of the village, where the lanes start to climb toward the top of the hill.","paras":["Welcome to Pyrgos. The name means Tower. The village wraps around this hill like an amphitheater, and it's one of the places on Santorini least spoiled by tourism.","We'll climb up through the lanes to the top. The steps and paving can be uneven, so take it slowly, and carry water. The sun here is strong.","It's a real village, not a stage set: 1,078 people lived here at the 2021 census, some 7 kilometers from Fira."]},{"name":"The maze of lanes","minutes":1,"stand":"Partway up, in one of the narrow lanes between the houses.","paras":["Notice how the lanes twist and turn. Pyrgos is a classic medieval village, with narrow, maze-like streets, fortified walls and hidden passages.","There was a reason for the maze. In the 1400s and 1500s, pirates raided these islands, stealing harvests and carrying people off to be sold as slaves. So islanders built their villages on the highest, hardest-to-reach ground, with houses packed tightly together. The outer walls had no openings, so the village itself became a wall.","There were five of these fortified villages on the island, called kasteli: at Oia, Akrotiri, Emborio, Skaros, and here at Pyrgos. And every one had a church at its entrance dedicated to Saint Theodosia, the protector of castles."]},{"name":"The Kasteli at the top","minutes":1,"stand":"At the top of the village, beside the ruins of the old castle.","paras":["Up here are the ruins of the Venetian castle, the Kasteli. For a time it was the administrative center of the whole island.","The Venetians conquered Santorini in 1207, and over the following centuries five fortified castles went up around the island. Of them, the castle at Pyrgos is the newest and the best preserved. It belonged to the same defense system as the castle at Oia.","Most of these castles had a goulas, a tall rectangular tower. It was a lookout and a last refuge, with thick walls, iron gates and murder holes, the openings for dropping things on attackers. Whether one stood here at Pyrgos, we don't know."]},{"name":"The view","minutes":1,"stand":"Find a spot at the top with a clear view out across the island.","paras":["And now look around. From this hill you can see Santorini in almost every direction.","Here's something you might not guess. Most of Santorini is volcanic, but under it lies a small, older island that isn't. That old rock comes to the surface in several places, and the Pyrgos area is one of them. Around here, you're close to the island that came before the volcano.","Up on the highest hill of the island, near Pyrgos, is the monastery of Profitis Ilias, the Prophet Elijah. It's rarely open to visitors. A walking trail starts just south of the village and passes it on the way to Kamari.","In the last years of Ottoman rule the monastery was one of the most important monastic centers in the Cyclades. And in 1822, during the Greek War of Independence, sixteen of its monks, led by their abbot, Gerasimos Mavrommatis, declared in writing their support for the revolt.","And if you enjoy wine, there are local wineries you can visit in Pyrgos itself."]},{"name":"Closing","minutes":1,"stand":"Stay at the top for a moment before heading back down.","paras":["Up here, the old walls guard nothing anymore, and anyone can wander in. Take your time going down. Let the twisting lanes slow you down; somebody once wanted them to, and they still do it well."]}] }
);
